      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>BEST &amp; GOES fARTHEST</head>
        <p>BEST &amp; GOES fARTHEST</p>
        <p>boat from Lakeside to Ambleside is one of the delights of a visit to this district, but if the lake comes to be appreciably polluted, the number of visitors from Lancashire and Yorkshire' and other parts of Britain, will certainly be diminished. There is unfortunately no happy medium in our system of ventilating ordinary railway carriages. In winter one has to choose between a cold icy blast, or a horribly stuffy atmosphere, and at this time of year when open windows are an absolute necessity, the dust and black from the engine almost ruin a delicate light summer toilette. Putting the window half way up scarcely makes matters any better, and some improved system, by which we could obtain fresh air in railway , carriages, withont the pi'esent attendant discomforts, is 1 sadly needed. Many an otherwise delightful house is spoiled by the pictures hung therein, and this state of affairs is more ' often due to the frames than the pictures themselves. Photographs should be framed in oxidised silver, or 1 light enamel, or their delicate tones will be killed. ' Pencil drawings for the see reason should also be framed in pale oolours, while etchings and pm and ink sketches, may on the contrary, have far heavier frames, and for these nothing looks better than fluted oak and walnut. If water colours be low in tone they may be lightly framed in enamel or oxidised silver but if the colouring is rick, there is nothing more suitable than dull gold. For oils alone, is burnished gold really suitable, and even for these, it should be sparingly used. As regards hanging pictures, the most common error is that of arranging them too high, and in dotting them evenly on each wall expanse. The centre of pic- tures should, as far as possible, be on a line with the eyes of a person of average height, and they should be arranged in pretty uneven groups, where all are of the same kind, and the framing identical; then a restful, undecorated piece of wall should be left, before placing I another group. All pictures, save oils, should be hung , flat against the wall, and not tipping. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>STONEHENGE.</head>
        <p>STONEHENGE.</p>
        <p>The Commons and Footpaths Preservation Society held a meeting on Wednesday to consider the first steps -to be taken for bringing before a tiibunal of the High Court the question of the public :right of way to Stonehenge. The owner of the land, Sir William Antrobus, some time ago enclosed the monument with a wire fence, and raises a toll af Is. for admittance. The Wiltshire County Council has decided not to take action in the matter, and negotiations between the Government and Sir William Antrobus for the purchase of the piece of land for the nation have come to an end, it being found impossible to agree upon the price to be paid. The Society has therefore decided itself to challenge the action of Sir William Antrobus. To cover the legal expenses it is anxious to raise the sum of £2,000. Of this some £4OO has already been promised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>FIELDSON FIRE AT SANKZY.—At DOOR on Tuesday a number of hayfields adjoining the Cheshire Lines Rail- way at Sankey, and belonging to Mr. Richard Wright, of Chapel-brow Farm, and Mr. Harry Fairolough, of Long- lane Farm were found to be on fire, presumably caused by a spark from a passing train. Efforts were made by a number of railway officials and farm labourers to put out the flames, and after much exertion they suc- ceeded,</p>
        <p>A THATCHED COTTAGE BURNT Dow.—On Monday moming;a thatched cottage, situated near the Friends' School, in Stocks-lane, Penketh; and occupied by Mr. Joseph Carman a tanyard employ 4, was totally des- troyed by fire. 'lt is.upposed that the high wind which was blowing carried a spark from the Manchester to Liverpool expreskand ignited the thatch. Mr. Carman was lying ill in bed at the time, but he immediately got up: and with the assistance of his wife and some neighbours, several articles of furniture were removed. The gale which prevailed, however, fanned the flames. and the roof collapsed. The Pen- keth Tannery Brigade and the Warrington Fire Brigade endeavoured to cope with the fire; but it , was seen that it was useless to save the cottage and the firemen directed their efforts chiefly to saving the out- buildings at the rear of the cottage. They succeeded in this respect, but the cottage and most of Mr. Carman's belongings were destroyed. One of Mr. Carman's sons also lost his savings in the fire. The property belongs , to Miss Tinsley. Damage to the extent of .Cl5O was I done.</p>
        <p>ACROBAT IN DANGER. Popular Entertainer's Hard Lot. THE clever and popular acrobat, Jack Drooko, has had evil times. Few but theatricals realise the terrible strain on an acrobat. Practising a new feat is very laborious.To keep fit,the men live careful lives;but often the"tureis given in an ill-ventilated hall, andthe heated performer retires to a draughty dressing TOolli. Drooko j saccumbed to his profession. He found that he was in ' the clutches of that cruel disease locomotor ataxy, and it is doubtful whether he would ever have recovered but for Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. A reporter recently interviewed Mr. Drooko; at his home at 274, Whitefleld-road, Liverpool. "For' many years," he said, "with my brother, I have given gymnastic and acrobatic shows in large towns. But I was suddenly compelled to give up the work. M 'es became weak, then I could not see at all unless I shut one of them. I had fearful pains in my legs, and later on in my arms. Twelve months ago I gave up. It was impossible for me to do a thing. After four weeks' treatment I was told that my case was :ncurtible. The doctors id I had locomotor Waxy,. and would never</p>
        <p>be iible to work again. By degrees I became absolutely helpless. It was inporsible for me to lift up my arms, and if I looked up at the ceiling or shut my eyes I FELT DIZZY, so that I would fall down. Finally, I lost my speech,</p>
        <p>1,,,,, i , % 'WV \ I 7 ( - /- . ;. 1</p>
        <p>eYt</p>
        <p>a&amp; ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Prince and Princess Charles of Denmark have been ! overwhelmed with congratulations on the birth of their little son. This event is a particularly happy one, as Prinoess Maud, as she is better known, was always very fond of children' and never happier than when petting or indulging her little nieces and nephews. Princess . Charles has never played e, prominent part in publio life, but she is extremely clever, being one of the gifted -number who can do anything, and can row, ride, cycle. play chess, bind a book, take a photograph, or converse in any one of five languages. Princess Maud is now in her 34th year, and was married to the second son of the Crown Prince of Denmark in 1896. Her little son is the King's eighth grandchild, and moreover comes twelfth in the line of succession to the throne. Canon Horsley, who is the vicar of one of the poorest parishes in London, has initiated a new kind of baby show. As arnle it is simply weight that decides these competitions, hut fat is sometimes produced at the ex- pense of health and the Canon gives the prize to the healthiest, instead of the fattest competitor. Amongst the poor, it is a commen habit of parents to give their children" just what they have themselves," such as tea, cheese or even beer, and the Canon's Object in organising these baby shows, is to encourage the best methods, of bringing up infants in crowded districts. One would scarcely think it, but,the shows are very popular with the poorest classes,and they help to diminish improper dieting, which to a large extent accounts for the high infant mortalily in the crowded parts of London. Mrs. Ryle, wife of the Bishop of Winchester, is not the only person who has drawn the lineat the opening of bazaars. Mrs. Ryle does not give us tne reason for her intimation; perhaps she has read the advice of a learned judge who said, "Never give a reason for your decision"; the "decision may be right, but the reason may be wrong." In any event Mrs. Ryle merely says that she "does not wish it to be considered that the opening of bazaars and sales of work come naturally within the dioeesan activities of a Bishop's wife." Oa the other hand, Mr. Sydney Webb has given the reason -why his wife and he are not open to accept invitations to preside at these functions. Having attended a bazaar for the purpose of opening it, and spent two or three hours in the journey from their Loudon residence, they discovered that they were expected not only to give their influence and their time, but also their money.— Not being a millionaire, Mr. Sidney Webb objected to</p>
        <p>this arrangement. Deep cape lace collars of inferior quality are being offered at all the sales, at almost ridiculous prices, but the wise woman 'will ,pass them by. These collars nn longer find a place on any of the best costumes and ouch purchases can only prove useless. Another "bar- gain "to be avoided at the sales is the Soiled muslin gown. This never proves a satisfactory one. For those lengths of silk too short for blousos. however' a use may now be found in the little sac visite, which can be well] cut out of a tliree yarcL remnant, or the round -</p>
        <p>shoulder cape which take i less material still. The only I trimming these modish little coverings require, is a I " I felt dizzy!" and I then thought my Jack. Drooko onthe high bar, end was near. The agony silk ruching which can also be quickly rim up by the home dressmaker. I was terrible. Then at the eleventh hour I was Linon de soie,a very delightful fabric, which closely recommended to take Dr. Williams' pink pills, and right " resembles crepe de chine is enjoying a very wide vogue glad am I that I did. They cured me. My strength re- just now. It is very soft, with a silken turned, and in a very short time I was completely re- strface, and stored to health—active, with a clear vision, and with falls well. For presentation toilettes it has proved particularly successful, and several brides have appeared my power of speech restored.. You can imagine how in gown and train built of linon de soie. Speaking of pleased lamto be myself again." trains,. Dame Fashion has laid down no hard and fast Probably no other case is on record oi a paralytic rule this season as to whether they shall be fastened to being cured so as to be an acrobat. Mr. Drooko's doctors said what all doctors say about locomotor ataxy both shoulders, or only one, or fall from the waist, but except tho few who have witnessed the effect of Dr. the latter idea which permits of the figure showing, is perhaps the one most in favour. Transparent trains Williams' pink pills for pale people, the only medicine e have lately carried all before them, and provided they ver proved to have cured this terrible disease. Mr. are not too transparent, they are undoubtedly the, Drool was snatched from a lingering death of torture. prettiest and least cumbersome. I Nor is the disease rare. It may attack anyone. Broken- A coat and skirt built of crush linen is an excellent down nerves, a weak back, loss of balance, and every investment for holiday wear. This fabric does not sort of numbness in the membees are c