      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>SMART BLOUSBJ</head>
        <p>A SMART BLOUSBJ</p>
        <p>%vnatever the surroundings may be when one of pink, blue, or champagne would look out of place. It therefore possesses the good quality defined as chic, while it is at the same time economical. White muslin, spotted or figured with black and trimmed with some fine lace and insertion such as Valenciennes is one of the most charming and elegant forms in which the magpie mixture is ex- pressed; while black with tiny white spots intermixed with white lace is a. costume suited to young or old. Any sort of headgear can be worn With these toilettes, whether a bright floral trimmed straw or a large chip picture hat laden with soft curling ostrich. plumes. The smart blouse depicted is of white cambric spotted with black, with a rolled back white linen collar which extends down the front through which some emerald green silk ribbon is threaded. The neck is finished by one of the new stiff white linen collars with embroidered points which turn down over the cravat of black silk embroidered with green. The belt is of green patent leather. A narrow piping of black gives a finish to the outer edge of the collar. Black pipings are cinite a feature of some of the new linen, crash, and tussore silk dresses of pale colours verging on white and are some- times used to soften or emphasise a second piping of some bright colour with a very pleasing effect. Whatever may have been said as to the demise of the blouse its reign</p>
        <p>THE LATEST HAT:</p>
        <p>by no means over for womankind all the worid over has realised its capabilities and adapta- bility to all circumstances, not to mention the fact of its economy, as it greatly lessens the number of gowns in our wardrobes, One feels this especially at this season of the year when country and sea-side visits necessitate the oft recurring packing and unpacking of our 'smartest frocks. The most sensible plan (as all must admit) for affording plenty of variety and change in our toilettes together with as little luggage as possible is to be well provided with blouses which range from the simple morning blouse of cambric or muslin to the more elaborate arrangement of silk, lace, or embroidery for afternoon or evening wear. Our blouses, however, must harmonise in colour and material as far as possible with the skirts with which they are to be worn, and 'lilt as in former years offer a contrast. This is a fact that must be carefully borne in mind by those who are desirous of being altogether up to date as regards their clothes. The alarming hat illustrated is quite the latest thing in the world of millinery, and sets off a pretty face and a well-dressed coiffure to advantage. It is made in very light almost lacey straw edged with a ruching of tulle to match the straw. Clusters of small pink 4nksia roses with their buds and leaves rest under the brim in the hair, while some folds of pink tulle are swathed round the crown which is flat and brought over the brim where, after twining through the roses is brought round from the back in a long end to the throat where it finishes in a chou. This style of hat is vecy</p>
        <p>popular and emmently becoming. Sometimes a fringe of pink or white banksia roses or other small flowers is arranged round the brim in place of the ruche with excellent results. Daisies tipped with pink or buttercups look well used in this way. The hat to be well worn must he placed well back on the head to show the waved hair in front of the face.</p>
        <p>The simple coatee sketched is one which could be expressed in any sort of material which best suited the wearer. In this in- stance a cream canvas is used over silk with stitched strappings of silk to match, while ornamental buttons are placed in front. The design is a very ,simple one which any girl clever with her ;needle and fingers might undertake at home. It is essential, however, that the bolero should fit the upper part of the figure accurately. A simple coat like this allows of the addition (when desired) of a lace collar, while the popular feather boa or shoulder cape will fall elegantly over its grace- ful lines. Champagne silk piped with black would make a dressy little coat cut in this fashion, while black chine silk strapped with itself piped with ,white or colour, or trimmed with lace and a- chou and ends of black accordion pleated chiffon would be very smart, The sleeves of coats of this style are tight fitting at the upper part of. the arm, widening towards the wrist, and the elbow sleeve is one of the most popular. Tucks or box-pleats, the latter often reversed, cover the coat bodice and sleeves in many instances, while occasion- ally one sees the coat cut up in a V shape at intervals to show the dressy bodice worn underwathd COQUETT*</p>
