      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>:ESIDENT</head>
        <p>:ESIDENT</p>
        <p>ÜBET.</p>
        <p>London has been en fete this week to receive M. Loubet, the French President, and he certainly cannot complain of the heartiness of his reception. For the best part of a decade Great Britain and France have been very strained in their relations, especially during the progress of the Boer war. But that has all now been forgiven and forgotten, and this week saw a perfect reunion of friend- ship. No trouble or expense have been saved on any side, from the King down to his humblest subject, to make the President's visit a completc success. M. Loubet has been on every hand thoroughly well received, and in doing honour to him Great Britain, in the person of its capital, has been doing honour to France. A very full programme . was arranged and one which must have made the President glad of a rest by Thursday! On Monday he arrived at Dover, where he was Tecetved by the Duke of Connaught. He travelled to Victoria in the royal special train, and was welcomed at Victoria by the King in person, accompanied by the Prince of Wales. After receiving a deputation of the French •colony ,in London at York House, which had</p>
        <p>ADMIRAL CAILLARD, In command of the Squadron. THE CRUISER GUICHEN, WHICH BROUGHT THE PRESIDENT OVERI PRESIDENT LOUBErS VLSI?.</p>
        <p>M. DELCASS. Who accompanied the Presidenti</p>
        <p>would find greater enjoyment in an aesial flight than in either of the other pastimes Everything new tried for the first time has,* sensation peculiar to itself, but the sensation derived from a flight in one of my husbanth aeroplanes gives me keener pleasure and more</p>
