      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>ARRIVAL</head>
        <p>THE ARRIVAL</p>
        <p>been prepared for him, M. Loubet dined with the King and Queen at Buckingham Palace, where a reception was afterwards held. On Tuesday, after a busy morning of visits and deputations, came the great banquet at the Guildhall, to which the President drove through t 7 aily decorated streets, and in the evening he entertained the King, the Am- bassadors and other prominent people to dinner at the Embassy. After this was held the gala performance at the Opera, for which ,great preparations had been made. Wednes- day was equally busy; for the morning a visit to Windsor'Castle was arranged, to be followed by a quiet lunch at the Embassy. Then in the afternoon came the great review at Alder- -shot, dinner witlr the Marquess of Lansdowne, at Lansdowne House, and a state ball at Buckingham Palace. Nothing more could possibly -have been squeezed into a three days' programme, and the visit was a very 'brilliant affair. M. Loubet was accompanied `by M. Delcasse, the Minister for Foreign Affairs, and in London was supported by M. Cambon, the French Ambassador. The visit must be accounted a great success, both politically and socially.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>KING'S NEW GRANDSON.</head>
        <p>THE KING'S NEW GRANDSON.</p>
        <p>Princess Charles of Denmark (Princess Maud), who has just presented our King with •a new grandson, was born in 1869, and was married to Prince Charles, the second son of the Crown Prince of Denmark, on 22 July, 1896. Thus, the Princess and her husband aTe first cousins. Prince Charles is one of the tallest princes in Europe, inheriting his stature from his mother, a daughter of King Charles XV. of Sweden, who left her an immense fortune. The union was generally popular in -this country, as the Princess had endeared</p>
        <p>OF DENMARK.</p>
        <p>lnerself to all classes by her frank and un- affected ways, and her desire to throw off, when it was possible to do so, the trammels of royal state. Her resemblance to her mother the Queen also contributed to her popularity. Princess Charles used to be fond of travelling incognito before her marriage, and many pleasant stories are told of her delight in the free and unconventional life of the country houses where she visited her friends, as kplain English lady, and not as a princess.</p>
