        <p>T.R.H. PRINCE AND PRINCESS CHARLES</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>NEW LADY AERONAUT.</head>
        <p>THE NEW LADY AERONAUT.</p>
        <p>Numbers of ladies have ascended to great heights by balloon, but it has been left to Madame Lela Cody to make the first ascent by kite, or aeroplane. This lady is performing some daring experiments with a kite made by her husband. Madame Cody, however, is not an Amazon of commanding appearance, but a gentlewoman of a most retiring disposi- tion, who confesses that during the first stages of Mr. Cody's kite experiments she suffered pangs of anxiety for the safety of her husband and sons, "but," she said smilingly to an interviewer, "those were early days; now I have the greatest confidence in my husband's invention, and encourage him to go on and improve upon his present apparatus, and have asked him to promise that during the next experiments he will allow me to go much higher than I have yet been, and feel sure that any lady who possesses the nerve to ride in a speedy motor, or to follow the hounds,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>MADAME LELA COI</head>
        <p>MADAME LELA COI</p>
        <p>THE AEROPLANE:</p>
        <p>:ielight than anything I have yet experienced: in fact, I shall not rest content until I have soared higher and higher like a bird on the wing." Mr. Cody's kite is distinctive for its eminently practical character and the steadi- ness with which it soars to a great height, being always under perfect control.</p>
