      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>U.S. NAVAL VISIT.</head>
        <p>THE U.S. NAVAL VISIT.</p>
        <p>Another reunion of an international ,charac- ter which has also taken place this week has been the reception of the United States squad- ron at Portsmouth. The Channel' Squadron was present to receive them, and Lord Charles i3eresford, who is in command, played a pro- minent part in the many hospitalities. Ad- miral Cotton is in command of the United States squadron, and he and his officers have been entertained on every side. His Majesty invited the Admiral and his officers to Lon- don, and they spent a part of their short visit in town. festivities were arranged to cover the whole period of their time at Portsmouth.</p>
        <p>from July 7th to July 14th. The Mayor and Corporation arranged to entertain the Ameri- can bluejackets to a dinner; while the Admiralty preferred, instead of another ban- quet, to get up an athletic meeting for both British and American sailors. The keenest interest has been evinced in this event, and the international rivalry is very strong. The British officers have invited the American officers to a ball, to be given in the handsome mess-rooms of the new Naval Barracks, Private entertainments during the visit are very numerous; and the American squadron should go away with a good idea of British hospi- tality.</p>
