      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>PREMIER MOTORIST.</head>
        <p>THE PREMIER MOTORIST.</p>
        <p>The race for the Gordon-Bennet Cup sur- passed all expectations, and was in every way a huge success, especially as there was no loss of life to mar the proceedings. Accidents to the cars, however, were very numerous in proportion, and out of twelve starters only five finished. The course, which was in the form of two triangles partially overlapping. had to be traversed three-and-a-half times, and the total distance was close upon 3-7 o miles. The winner was M. Jenatzy, the well- knowit Belgian automobilist, who was driving a6o h.p. Mercedes in the Germany team. He won by iri minutes in the wonderful time of 6 hours 3o minutes, which gives a wonderful average speed of close on sixty miles an hour., At times he was doing between eighty and ninety miles an hour; and the result is truly</p>
        <p>M. JENATZY ON HIS 6o H.P. MERCEDES. man e.llous, when the difficulties_ of the course</p>
        <p>taken into account. De Knyff and Far- man, driving Panhards for France. were second ;,nd third with one minute bet•veen them, d the other French representative,. Gabriel, vv.as lout th on is large Mors, twenty minutes behind them. The next and last car was Edce's Napier, but it was two hours behind Gabriel owing to no less__ than seven tyre</p>
        <p>:ccidents. The other cars did not and Hlgland fared otly one whit better than the Yankees, who were hopelessly out of it front rtart to finish. Jarrott broke his collar-bone, and broke his car in two; and Stocks broke a wheet oft in a 7 ditc:t. 'lke honours of the race p.) completely to M. jenatzy and Germany, despite the fact that their racing Cars had been destroyed in the recent /Daimler fire, .and they were only using an ordinary type of touring.car with a racing body.</p>
