      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July 11, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 11, 1903.</p>
        <p>requisition the servie,es of the military authority, and PRIZE DAY AT WARRINGTON the officer commanding had immediately despatched a company to the scene of conflagration, and it was re- solved that the best thanks of the committee be given TRAINING COLLEGE. to the officer commanding the Bth and 40th Regimental District for the valuable services rendered by Captain Gorenand his men in maintaining orderat the fire. The 'Watch Committee have passed the follow- INTERESTING PROCEEDINGS. ing resolution :—" That an expressi6n of satisfaction be conveyed to the Fire Engine Committee for the ex- cellent services rendered by the superintendent and Speeches by the Principal and Members of the Fire Brigade in saving the adjoining property." and The Fire Engine Committee resolved Sir James Hoy. "That the best thanks of this committee be given to Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co.,Ltd., and Messrs. Joseph Crosfieldand Sons,Ltd., for the valuable services rendered by their respective brigades at the recent fire at Messrs. J. H. Fleming and Company's Tannery." The committee also decided that the super- tendent should be instructed to communicate by tele- phone with the reservoir keepers at Winvvick and Apple- ton immediately on receiving information of a fire within the borough, and further that the Water Com- mittee be requested to instruct the keepers to increase the supply of water on receiving the information. Alderman Smwrkturtsr proposed that the minutes be confirmed. He said it was very gratifying to himself the superintendent, and the men to receive thanks for the able manner in, which they dealt with the fires which took place in Warrington. He had seen many fires in his time but he did not think there had been such a fire in Warrington as that at the tannery, and the firemen showed great ability in dealing with it and in saving the surrounding property. (Hear, hear.) Councillor BURTON thought the Warrington brigade were entitled to congratulation. The alarm was given at ten minutes past ten, they were on the scene at 10.16 and at 10.20 they had several hydrants playing on the fire. For such despatch they certainly deserved com- plimenting. (Hear, hear.) Alderman BENNETT said the Fire Engine Committee so seldom got its due meed of praise that he must add to what praise had been said. The men grappled with the fire with wonderful courage and skill. The MAYOR said he was sure they were all only too glad4to thank the brigades. The minutes of these committees were then passed.</p>
        <p>There was a large gathering at the Warrington Train- ing College on Saturday afternoon on the occasion of the annual prize-giving. A large number of local friends of the College responded to the invitation of the Principal to be present on the occasion and there were also present many of the parents and friends of the students. Sir James Hoy (chairman of the Man- chester Education Committee) presided over the pro- ceedings and distributed the prizes. Sir James was supported by the Principal of the • College (the Rev. Morley Stevenson), and the following members of. the Committee :—The Dean of Manchester, the Rev. Canons Penrhyn, Spooner, and Willis, Dr. Handyside, Mr. W. S. Kinch, and Mr. R. H. Joynson. There were also pre- sent :—Mr. Stevenson and all the members of the resid- ent staff. The following accepted the Principal's i ivita- tion :—Rev. H. A. and Mrs. Lester, Rev. W. Bracecamp, Rev. W. and Mrs. Stanton, Rev. W. Pollock Hill, Mr. Chas. White, Dr. and Miss Bowden, Dr. and Mrs. Adams, Dr. and Mrs. Guest Gornall, Mr. E. M. Hance (Liver- pool Education Council), Mr. Robert Jones (Birkenhead Education Council), Mr. J. Moore Murray (Warrington Education--- Council) and Mrs. Murray, Mr. Brownell (Manchester School Board Inspector) and Mrs. Brownell, Mrs. Penrhyn, Mrs. Willis, the Misses Watkins, the Misses White, Miss N. Platt, Miss Comber, Mrs. Cameron, Mr. and Mrs. Atkin, Mr. and Mrs. C. S. Bur- gess, Mr. and Mrs. Coxon, Mrs. Et. and the Misses Fairclough, Mr. and Mrs. Carruthers, Mrs. Pickmere, Mr. and Mrs. May, Miss Massy and friend, Miss Kennedy and friends, Mr. and Mrs. W. S. Woods, the Misses Brookes, Miss Taylor, Miss M. M. Timewell, Mr. J. H. Fisher, Mr. F. Morris, and over a hundred parents and friends of the students from other towns.</p>
        <p>THE SLACK CON raNcrs.—COUNGILLOR LORD'S QUESTIONS. PROPOSED INVESTIGATION. Councillor LORD asked the Chairman of the Water Committee the following questions respecting the slack contract :-1. Has the Water Committee completed its investigation regarding the slack contract? 2. When will a complete report thereon be presented to this Council? 3. What firm is at present sending in sup- plies? The Water Committee's minutes contained a resolu- tion that Councillor John Roberts be appointed a mem,- ber of the Committee in Place of Alderman Monkd, resigned. _ _ _</p>
        <p>THE PRINCIPAL AND TEIE NEW EDUCATION ACT.</p>
        <p>In opening the proceedings, the PRINCIPAL said he was sure they would all be sorry to hear that the Bishop of Burnley had been prevented from attending and had telegraphed at the last moment to that effect. Two great friends of the college, both of whom were present at their last prize distribution had been called to their rest. He referred to Mr. Gilmore and Dr. Watkins. Mr. Gilmore in his latter years was not in the best of health, and they did not see so much of kim as they had been accustomed to in former times, but he was a very munificent supporter of that institution. Dr. Watkins was for many years its secretary, and always took the warmest interest in the college. If his (the Principal's) recollection served him rightly, Dr. Watkins never missed a prize-giving during the whole of the time that he was a member of the committee. He (the Principal) was sure all present would feel sympathetic- ally the loss of the presence of both those gentlemen. Nor must he pass over the death of Miss Goddard, their former lady superintendent, who left them a few years ago to enter upon a period of well-earned leisure. She, too, not many weeks ago passed away. Although she was unknown to the present students of the college she was well known to many former ones. having lived there for over forty years, and she was as much respected and loved as she was knGwn. Passing from those sad reminiscences, the year just spent had not been in any sense of the word a very eventful one. That day they were finiqhing the completion of the course of the first students who entered under the new regulations, which laid upon the colleges the duty of drawing up their ownsyllabuses for examination, instead of taking the syllabus prescribed for them by the Board of Education. Those students had had no examination at the end of their first year. They had now but one examination, and that was upon their leaving at the end of their second year. In fact, he might say they had their pleasures yet to come. (Laughter.) Therefore it was impossible to make any report as to what the students had done in any examination outside that conducted by the college itself, and he was' sure they would agree with him that they would give the second year students their best wishes in the examination which they were going to enter upon in the coming week. (Applause.) They earnestly trusted that the period of two years which the students had passedat the college would prove fruitful for good, and that they would see gratifying results when they were made public. In educational circles the great event of the past year had been the new Edscation Act. It was too new for them to gauge the exact effect it was going to have, and certainly so far as training colleges were concerned they had not yet experienced anything from it. Training colleges at all events had made no resist- ance—either active or passive. (Laughter,) He thought that in the future the Education Act would have indirectly a very great effect upon them. One effect of the Act would be a very increased demand for the highest class of teachere—those who were certifica- ted and trained. That demand could not possibly be met by the supply of the present moment, and as it was the duty of the education authori- ties to cater for the supply of teachers and to provide for that supply it was obvious that there must be a very close relationship in the future between the education authorities and the existing training colleges. He was happy to say that had been recognized in Warrington by the Training college having been asked by the education authority to\nominate a representative upon it He felt I glad that that honour had been conferred upon them, and it was a very wise step. The only danger he foresaw was that when the education authorities of the future began to feel their feet and to recognize their power they would rush at once—seeing the great demand for - teachers—into the establishment of many new and large colleges. That might be dangerous to some of the older colleges, and he would like to express the hope that what he thought would be the wiser policy that might be followed was that they would regard the older ' colleges as a valuable educational asset in their balance sheet, and would do all they could to uphold, to improve and to enlarge those which already existed before they turned their attention to the creation of the new dues which might also be needed. (Applause.) The com- mittee of this college had taken a step this year towards doing something as far as, possible to increase the supply of teachers. For many years they had been trying to enlarge the college and open its benefits to a larger number of students, but the obstacles in the way had been too great to be surmounted, They had, however, recently acquired Orford Hall, which would be a hostel for an additional 30 students. It was a very beautiful old house standing in very charming grounds, and he felt sure that the students whose lot it was to be located there would have, in many ways, a very happy time. One drawback to it was its distance from the college, which they would have to try and overcome in the best way they could. Notwithstanding that he felt more and more convinced that the step had been a wise and right one. The necessary funds re- quired for furnishing the house and for making certain additions to their existing classrooms was £1,500. Towards that they had collected the sum of about £l,OOO, and they hoped by the generosity of their friends to be able to collect what was necessary to make up the deficiency before very long. He would not express an opinion as to whether the breaking up of the Victoria University into two uni- versities at Liverpool and Manchester was a good thing or not, but he hoped one or both would take a paternal interest in any educational efforts which were being made in Warrington. He had long hoped that the uni- versity of the future might have a wider basis in some ways than the university of the past. There were many students who were urtable from many causes to go through the whole of the university course, but there was no reason that he could see why a student could not go through a course of one or more subjects. Would it not be an advantage for a student to receive the lectures of a university professor in a given subject and pass the university examination in that subject, even if not able to pass the university examination in all sub- jects? He had had an informal and unofficial conversa- tion with authorities in Manchester and Liverpool, and they looked favourably on some plan of that sort. His own committee had appointed a strong sub-committee to go into the question, and he trusted that ,before long there would be good fruits from their deliberations. (Applause.) Sir JAMES Hoy, before distributing the prizes, de- livered an address. He expressed regret that Dr. Hopkinson of the Owen's College, Manchester' was detained at another function and was unable to be present. It was trim that a great change had taken place in regard to educational matters and it was equally true that some of them had had their ground of work very much enlarged. If he (Sir James Hoy) had not had the aid of those who had full knowledge of all work carried on in Board schools,he shouldhave shrunk from accepting the position of head of the Education Committee in Manchester. As yet he felt quite incom- petent to say anything further about elementary education or about the relation of such a college as this to education authorities. He could quite believe it might be interesting on an occasion like that, if one were to communicate a policy or if one were to , give some inkling of what might be the attitude of a considerable, educational authority like that of Man- chester to this college but he had nothing to say on that point. (Laughter.) The question td not been considered, only this he would say, that looking at it from the point of view of a person who in these matters likes to be practical and likes to take and make use of existing institutions for the furtherance and the better doing of the work in Which he is engaged, it would look as if there was an oppor- tunity here, perhaps, for fostering and extending the work which plainly had been of such advantage not only to the college, but more especially to the com- munity which was served by trained and experienced teachers. To use a dismal phrase, so suggestive of an execution—(laughter)—"the appointecllday"had come and gone, and certain institutions had ceased under that name to exist. After a long experience of local govern- ment in the city of Manchester, he hoped and believed that the interests of education would in the long run— he hoped within a short period—be advantaged by the change that had been made. (Hear, hear.) Above all</p>
        <p>Aiderman FRA.NCOMR, in replying • to the questions, said: (1) I gave all the information I could at the last meeting. Still the next question will perhaps answer that. Mr. Doss, the water engineer, says he will give a full report at our next Council meeting. Ques- tion 3: At the present time Messrs. R. Evans and Company are sending in the whole of the supplies, and they are giving satisfaction. I should also like to say that since our last Couacil meeting the Slate Company have sent in, according to the figures I have here, 9 tons 12 cwts. 1 qr. It appears that someone from the Slate Company came to Mr. Deas and told him that they had this slack on hand, and having no ether customer for it, they thought it was only fair that we should take it off their hands. (Hear, hear.) That is the only informa- tion I have to give you at present, and I move that the minutes of the Water Committee be confirmed. Councillor JOHN EVANS Seconded. . Councillor LORD: I think at the last Council meet- ing SOMA accounts were referred back on account of this. I should like to ask the Chairman if the payments are included in the present accounts? The MAYOR: I understand that there were no accounts referred back.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD: Are there no payments for slack? If so, to whom? Alderman FRANCOMB: There is a payment to both I)rms. We are bound to pay for slack if we have had it. Councillor LORD: I suppose, Mr. Mayor, that if we have had it we must pay for it. The minutes were then put and carried. ' INCREASED DEMAND FOR ELECTRIC LIGHT. At a meeting of the Electricity and Tramways Com- mittee on the 24th June, the Town Clerk submitted the plans prepared by Messrs Preece and Cardew of Light Railway No 1, and at a meeting on the 24th June it was resolved that a new electric feeder be laid in Horse- market street, and that the electric mains be extended to Orford Hall.</p>
        <p>Alderman MONKS, in moving the adoption of the minutes said the new feeder and mains to Orford Hall had been rendered necessary by reason of the increased demand for current.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY MAYOR seconded, and the minutes were accepted.</p>
        <p>OPEN SPACES. SUGGESTED IMPROVEMENTS.</p>
        <p>The following report and recommendations- of the Open Spaces i Sub-Committee was approved by the General Purposes Committee, at its meeting on June 29th. (1.) That a row of trees be planted in St. James' Old Burial Ground along the river side, and that the same be protected by an unclimbable fence. (2.) That the ground near the entrance to Victoria Park be suit- ably laid out. (3.) That Messrs. John White, Maclver and Co. be approached witha view of obtaining an open space for the Fairfield Ward. (4.) That application be made to the Local Government Board for their sanction to the borrowing of the sum of £5OO, being the estimated cost of fencing and laying out the Recreation Ground off Liverpool-road recently purchased from Lord Lilford. (5.) That the members of this committee beauthorized to inspect the open spaces in the Wallasey District with a view of laying out the open space in Orford-lane in a similar manner.</p>
        <p>Theminutes were agreed to on the proposition of the MAYOR, seconded by the DEPUTY MAYOR. The discussions on the proposed new street from Sankey-street to Rylands-street and the inquiry into Municipal Trading will be found in another part of the paper.</p>
        <p>he thought they would all feel bound to put forward their best endeavours because none of them had sought this change, speaking distinctly of the school boards on the one hand and the local authorities on the other. The change had not been brought about as the result of an agitation on the part of councils or municipal institu- tions to secure this power; it had been passed on to them. Neither was it sought by school boards that they should get rid of themselves and hand over their work to other bodies. The duty had been put upon the new authority by the Legislature, and that being so one knew enough of the value of municip4l effort to be assured that this new duty would be accepted in a lsrge spirit and with an earnest desire for the benefit above all of those who came to be taught in the schools which were now committed to their charge. (Applause). Speaking more particularly of the students, Sir James by said he had the greatest sympathy for young teachers in their arduous task of having to control undisciplined children. He hoped that there might be courage, and diseietion and a wise use of whatever had been learnt at that college in the trying circumstances in which any young teacher was placed in the public elementary schools. There was also something of risk to the person whb had to go through the dailyround of teaching one year after another. There was a call for an elevation of character and an interest in other things than those with which they were directly connected. If they were to keep their balance right there must be something of aloofness from what we got our grub by—(laughter)— or what we palled our daily work. We needed to keep alive in our midst an active human interest, a keen interest in the affairs of those under our control, and that interest would never be sustained ualess our minds were attracted to the noblest and best that eur literature affords. This thought had been expressed by Matthew Arnold in the words:—</p>
        <p>We nod and glance nnd bustle by And nc'er once possess our souls before we die. PRIZE MST. SECOND YEAR STUDENTS.</p>
        <p>Margaret Bowman (religious knowledge), prize The Teacher's Bible; Lilian Plant (religious knowledge), The Teacher's Bible; Eva Jones (highest marks in all subjects—lst prize), Scott's Novels; Nellie Bayley (highest marks in all subjects, second prize), picture, "The Angel Choir"; Lilian Duckworth, Florence Thorniley (theory and practice of teaching), Dowden's " Mind of Shakespare " Prayer Book and (Hymn Book • Nellie Bayley, Eva Jones 'arithmetic and Algebra), Longfellow's Poems, Ruskin's 'Ethics of the Dust," "Frondes Agrestes"; Eva Jones (Euclid), travelling bag; Lilian Duckworth (reading and recitation) Browning's Poems; - Eva Jones (English literature and composition), Browning's Poems; Eliza Williamson (the best letter), .vriting case •, Eva Jones (French), writing case; Nellie Bayley, Ellen Saxon (general history and geography), Scott's Poems, Tomyson's Poems ; Margaret Bowman Constance Pople, (industrial history), Tennyson's Poems, Prayer Book and Hymn Book ; Nellie Bayley (elementary science )Browning's Poems; Nellie Bailey (drawing), Ruskins"Ethics of the Dust"," Sesame and Lilies,'"•Queen of the Air" ;"Crown of Wild Olive"; Margaret Cress- well (Needlework), Picture "The Angel Choir " ; Emily Howarth (general improvement), Shakespeare's Works ; Eliza Williamson (general improvement), Shakespeare's Works; Ethel Jackson (general improvement), writing case; Annie Taylor (general improvement), writing case; Edith France (general improvement), writing case. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL</p>
        <p>THE FIRE BRIGADE COMPLIMENTED. More About the Slack Contract.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Town Council was held on Tuesday evening. The Mayor (Mr. J. Charlton Parr) presided, aturthere were also present :—Alderman Fairclough (deputy Mayor), Aldermen Sutton Fran. comb, Smethurst, Bennett, Bolton, Monks, Hutchinson' • Councillors John Evans, James Evans, William Roberts, John Roberts, Peacock, Barton, Dodds, Burton, Brophy, Sampson, Ellison, Tinnion, S. Davies, Crnik, Rogers, Forshaw, Wilkinson, Pemberton, Lord, F. R. Roberts, Upson; with Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk)„ T. Longdin (borough surveyor), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), J. Deas (water and sanitary engineer),W. S, Haddock (gas engineer), J. M. Murray (director of education), W. H. Grimsdale (electrical and tramways engineer), B. M.. Ker (deputy borough surveyor), F. V. L. Mathias (assistant electrical engineer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LATE COUNCILLOR BAIRD.</head>
        <p>THE LATE COUNCILLOR BAIRD.</p>
        <p>The IVIa von said he had once again to record a loss to the Council in the death of their esteemed member, the late Councillor Baird. It would be in the minds of all of them that the first attack of his fatal illness occurred in that Council Chamber after a long and protracted sitting. He fell at his post in the tattle of life, after ' long and faithful services rendered to the town and ' with a record of work well and honestly done. He was a faithful citizen and a conscientious and worthy man. He moved that the Council receive with deep regret the announcement of his death and desire to record the loss thus sustained, and that the Town Clerk be requested to convey to the widow and family the sincere sympathy of the Council. (Hear, hear.) Alderman SurroN seconded, and remarked that Councillor Baird was always very attentive to his duties, and worked hard on all the committees of which he was a member. There was no doubt the town had suffered a great less by his death. Alderman BENNETT thought there was no more useful member of the Council, in a quiet way, than the late Councillor Baird. He had not a spark of ostenta- tion. Those who had the pleasure of knowing him intimately, could not have anything but the greatest possible respect for him. Not only would the whole town regret his loss ,but those who had the pleasure of his friendship would lament the death of a very dear friend.</p>
        <p>The resolution was unanimously carried. NEW PRESIDING ALDERMAN.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Councillor TINNION, seconded by Alderman. BOLTON, Alderman Bennett was appointed pre- siding officer for St. Austin's Ward. THE POLICE BUILDINGS.—QUESTIONS BY COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON asked the Chairman of the Watch Committee the following questions :-1. What was the amount allowed by the contractor for the police buildings for the old materials under the contract? 2. As the contractor did not take all the materials, was not an allowance made, and if so, what was the amount allowed? 3. Has the whole account been adjusted, and if so, what are the figures now? ThelVia.von. said the answer to the first question was £665 us. sd. To the second.—The contractor pur- chased all the old materials on the site, and they are his property. If he is not allowed to remove the materials now standing on the site he will be entitled to an allowance in respect of them' and the value will be fixed by arbitration as provided in the contract. The answer to the third question was :—That the architects have not yet certified the final payments under the contract, and cannot do so until the claims of the builder in res- pect of the unfinished portion of the contract and for the old materials have been settled. BISHOP'S WHARF. RENEWAL OF THE LEASE TO THE TRANSPORT COMPANY. At a meeting held on the 15th June of the Finance and Estate Committee it was resolved that the Chairman and Borough Treasurer be appointed to attend the annual meeting of the Institute of Municipal Treasurers and Accountants, to be held at Oxford on the 16th and 17th July, 1903—At a meeting on the 29th June the committee received an application from Messrs. Leeoh, Harrison and Forwood for the option to renew the lease granted to the Manchester and Liverpool Transport Co., Ltd., of the wharf know* as Bishop's Wharf for a further period of 7, 14, 21, or 28 years after the expiration of the existing term in 1920. The com- mittee recommended the Council to grant the option of a further term of seven years at an increased rent of £l4O per annum. The DEPUTY-MANOR moved the confirmation of the minutes. He said the Council would remember that in 1896 the Corporation built a wharf on the south side of Wharf street. at a cost of £1,486, which was leased to Messrs Bishop &amp;Co for a term of seven years at a rent of £5 a year an addition to a sum equal to 5 per cent on the cost of construction. In 1899 the lease was transferred to the Manchester and Liver- pool Transport Co., Ltd., who built an adjoining wharf on the foreshore belonging to the Corporation at a cost of £472. In 1900, the Corporation purchased the wharf built by the company, and repaid them the sum of £472, and granted a lease of the two wharves to the company for2l years at a rent of £ll2 12s. As they were prob- ably aware the affairs of the Manchester and Liverpool ' Transport Company were in.the hands of a xeceiver, and an application had been received from Messrs. Leech, Harrison and Forwood on behalf of the debenture holders wh;\ proposed to take over the Warrington branch of the business, and they asked for the option of extending the term of the lease from seven to 28 years at the expiration of the existing lease, "so as to seoure a continuous trade." The warehouses and shed adjoining the wharf belonged to the company. The Committee didnot wish to lose their tenant, and there would be some difficulty in letting the wharf except to the owners. of the warehouses. The Committee, after carefully considering the application, believed it would be to the advantage of the Corporation to give the company the option of extending the term for seven years at an increased rent of £l4O. Alderman BENNE'TT seconded. Alderman SUTTON thought it was a very long time to extend a lease,andinhis opinion there should be a clause in the lease providing that the Obrporation should be I entitled to put an end to the lease if the wharf was ' wanted for other purposes. They might want to make alterations there, and it was only reasonable that they should have such a provision. Alderman BOLTON said the lease was for less than 21 • yea?s, and that was very little time for a company to I build up a business in. He thought the committee had done right. (Hear,hear.) To cavil about little matters of detail was out of the question. The minutes were adopted. WIDENING OF KNUTSFORD-ROAD. The Street Improvement and Lighting Committee, at their meeting on June 25, resolved that the Borough Surveyor be instructed to prepare plans and estimates for the widening of Knutsford-road near Victoria Park by the construction of a permanent river wall. I On the motion of Alderman SirrroN seconded by Alderman BENNETT, the minutes were confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>ROUTINE:</head>
        <p>ROUTINE:</p>
        <p>The minutes of a number of committees, which were published in the "Examiner" last week were confirmed without remark.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN MONKS AND THE SANITARY WORKS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Sanitary Works Committee on the 24th June it was resolved that Councillor Sampson be appointed a members of the committee in plane of Alderman Monks, resigned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>MUSEUM COMMITTEE.-THE NEW PI9TURE.</head>
        <p>MUSEUM COMMITTEE.-THE NEW PI9TURE.</p>
        <p>Amongst the items of interest on the minutes of this committee was ono intimating that a letter had been received from the Rochdale Free Library Committee conveying thanks for a gift of Roman pottery. In moving the adoption of the minutes, Alderman BENNF,rr referred to the purchase of the new picture by the, late Mr. Brewtnall, which he said was an excellent addition to their art gallery. He thought they would all agree it had been a very advantageous purchase. pouncillor ELLISON seconded, and it was carried. WEIGHTS AND MEASURES ACTS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Markets Committee washeld on the 22nd June, when it was resolved that the Weights and Measures Acts urgently require amendment, for the protection of the public and to secure uniformity of administration; and that the President of the Board of Trade be urged to introduce at an!early date a Govern- ment Bill dealing with this important subject. Tile annual report of thelnspector of Weights and Measures was read and ordered to be printed and submitted to the Council. Councillor ROGERS proposed the adoption of the minutes. Councillor Peacock seconded and the motion was approved. GAS CO.NIMITT2E.—A COMPLAINT. The DEPUTY MAYOR proposed the confirmation of the minutes. Councillor ROGERS called attention to the lighting of the streets running parallel with Church-street and School-brow, and said it was very late before the gas was lighted. He had also received a complaint from Willis-street. Some of the residents said that their gas ovens were practically useless to them. If he was right it was the fault of the main. The MAYOR said it would have been better if Coun- cillor Rogers had given notice to the Committee. Alderman FAIRCLOUGH observed that the Gas Com- mittee lighted the streets as they were told by the Street Improvement Committee. The minutes were agreed to. THE RECENT FIRE.—BRIGADES COMPLIMENTED.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Watch Committee, held on June llth, the Chief Constable reported that owing to the enormous crowd Dissembled at the recent fire at Messrs. Fleming's Tannery, he had found it necessary to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>LITTLE LUNCHES FOR LITTLE FOLK.</head>
        <p>LITTLE LUNCHES FOR LITTLE FOLK.</p>
        <p>Just the Right Thing.</p>
        <p>It is difficult in an ordinary way to provide dishes in sufficient variety to prevent little folk from getting tired of "the same thing over again;" but a varied selection of nourishing summer dishes readily digested, and thoroughly wholesome and enjoyable, can be easily and gnickly made with Brown &amp; Poison's "Patent" Corn Flour, by methods explained in their new booklet, "Summer Dishes," which will be sent, post free, by Brown &amp; Poison, Dept. Rio, Paisley, on receipt of a id. stamp. In this booklet will be found a number of recipes for the wholesome serving of fruit along with Corn Flour, making dishes which will be "just the right thing "for the children at lunch or dinner time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>WATER POLO.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>WM-AN Ar. WARRINGTON.—At the Corporation Baths, Wigan, on Wednesday night. Wigan, who were without Swift, defended the shallow end in the first half. They quickly got to work, and Nevison scored two goals. Before the interval Pagett and Magraw added goals. Wigan leading by four goals to nothing. Wigan again pressed on resuming, and the visitors' custodian had several good shots to clear. Smith also got rid of a couple of hot shots. Result :—Wigan, 4, Warrington, none.</p>
        <p>In a Second Division match, Southport beat Wigan Second by 5 goals to 3.</p>
        <p>MERSEY CLUB. LORD NEWTON'S CUP.</p>
        <p>The final for Lord Newton's oup was played on Satur- day between Messrs. Parry and J. D. Fairclough, and resulted in a win for Mr. Parry. This cup was presented for competition in 1894, and Mr. Party having twice previously held it now takes possession of it. In his match with Mr. Fairclough Mr. Parry also broke the amateur record of the course, hitherto held by Mr. Gilbert, by one stroke, his score for the round being 70.</p>
        <p>Out 4 3 3 4 4 4 5 3 5 35 In 4 3 4 4 3 6 4 3 4 35</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>WEAR AND TEAR OF CITY LIFE.</head>
        <p>THE WEAR AND TEAR OF CITY LIFE.</p>
        <p>Why do so many of our City men look so sickly? Because they drink tog much tea, and when they are fagged out cannot take more substantial and nutritive food. The system thus gradually becomes weakened and debilitated; there is no relish for food, no energy for work, and the whole of the physical and mental condi- tion become:: seriously deranged. One City clerk—Mr. H. W. Elliott, 23, Norfolk Road, Canonbury, N.—writee:—"l work late hours, but I find Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa a delicious and stimulating drink. My doctor, grocer, aid chemist speak very highly of it." And if you use Vi-Cocoa regularly you will speak very highly of it too. Sold in 6d. packets and 9d. and is. 6d. tins everywhere:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
