      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>FIRST YEAR STUDENTS.</head>
        <p>FIRST YEAR STUDENTS.</p>
        <p>Lilian Craik(religious knowlgdge), prize The Teacher's Bible; Lucy Wrenn (highest marks in all subjects, Ist prize), stationery cabinet; Ivy Adkin (highest marks ia all subjects, 2nd prize), Browning's Poems ; Lilian Craik, Bertha Jones (theory and practice of teaching), Prayer Books and Hymn Books ; Hannah Osborn, Fanny Williams (arithmetic and algebra), Tennyson's Poems ; Lucy Wrenn (reading and recitation) Prayer Book and Hymn Book, Tennyson's Poems ; Ella Yates (enclid), Wordsworth's Poems, Tennyson's Poems; Annie Highet (English literature and composition), Prayer Book and Hymn Book, Tennyson's Poems; Lucy Wrenn (history and geography), Shakespeare's Works ; Elsie Bissell (French), Ruskin's "Sesame and Lilies," "Ethics of the Dust," " Queen of the Air"; Ivy Adkin, (elementary science), Prayer Book and Hymn Book, The Vulgate; Mary Elkin (nature study), Shakespeare's Works ; Ivy Adkin (needlework), work basket; Clara Hayes, Amy Riley (drawing), Tennyson's Poems, Milton's Poems. ' The DEAN of MANCHESTER, in proposing a rote of thanks to Sir James Hoy said if there was one merit that belonged to the Education Act that merit was co- ordination, and co-ordination could not have been in better hands than those of the old chairman of the Technical Instruction Committee and now chairman of the Education Committee for the city of Manchester. (Applause.) _</p>
        <p>Mr. R. H. Joyssos seconded and the vote was carried with acclamation.</p>
        <p>The company then adjourned to the lawn, where re- freshments were served. The Volunteer Band played a number of selections. A sale of work was also held in the Library in aid of the College Extension fund, the goods on the stalls being those which remained over from the recent bazaar. A fairly large number of goods were purchased.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>ERA OF ELECTRIC TRAVEL. A Saving to the Railway Companies.</head>
        <p>THE ERA OF ELECTRIC TRAVEL. A Saving to the Railway Companies.</p>
        <p>More about the Mono-Rail.</p>
        <p>Before many years are over it may be safely prophe- sied that thesteart locomotive will have been entirely superseded by electric traction for the carrying of pas- sengers on railways. Most of the companies realise this and the North-Eastern and Lancashire and Yorkshire Railways are busy electrifying portions of their lines. Other companies are holding their hands, and are await- ing the results of. the Electric Express Mono-Rail at 110 miles an hour about to be constructed. It has been incontestably proved that the electric working of railway 4 and Mono-Rail lines in particular so far as passenger traffic is concerned will be much cheaper than the pre- sent steam hauled trains. The working expenses of the Manchester and Liverpool Mono-Rail will be far below those of any existing railway. The current will be supplied from a central generating station at War- rington, thus avoiding the wastefulness of numerous steam locomotives. The trains will be made up of single oars, some carrying 50 passengers and some more, and they will be run at ten minutes intervals (or less). The rolling stock will be less than that on ordinary lines. On the double Mono-Rail track there will never be more than four cars at a time. The staff to work the Mono Rail will be very small for there will be no coupling of cars, no crossings, no points, no shunting, &amp;c. The wages to be paid will be less than one half those on the three lines now running between Manchester and Liverpool, the number of porters, &amp;c being far less. Each car will carry but two operators, and of course there will be no stoking, no taking in of water, &amp;c., while the upkeep and the electric machines will require far less labour and be much less expensive than that of a steam engine. It was stated before Parliament that the total annual expenditure for traction maintainance, and working expenses of the Electric Mono-Rail would amount to £65,554per annum equal to a cost per train mile of about 71d. On this basis an average of 0n1.7i third class passengers carried per train would therefore cover all working expenses. An average of 20 passengers per train or 2,000 per day in each direction would be snfficent to pay 5 per cent. per ann urn, and for the capital of the Manchester and Liverpool Electric Express Railway Company, (the prospectus of which will be issued in a few days' time,) and every additional 2i passengers per train will be sufficient to pay about an additional 1 per cent upon the capital. The 10 minutes service provides for a traffic of about 1,000 passengers each way per day, and the Mono-Rail is quite capable with larger and more frequent cars to carry 25,000 per day in eacl direction. It has been proved again and again that the supply- of more rapid and frequent com- munication, between populous centres creates a great and ever-increasing traffic. There is now a very big daily traffic between Manchester and Liverpool. The Mono-Rail will absorb most of this and will create, it may safely be predicted, a new traffic which will increase in volume yearly. There is every prospect that. this new railway—the pioneer high speed line in the world—wilt be a great financial success from the first and will earn such profits as to induce the great railway companies to adopt the Mono-rail system for their express passenger traffic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>FEMALE " BOY " SENSATION.</head>
        <p>A FEMALE " BOY " SENSATION.</p>
        <p>A sensation has been caused at Blackburn by a seven- teen-year-o/d girl masquerading in male attire. She replied to the advertisement of a barber named O'Bryan for a lathering boy, and, dressed as a boy, held the post eight, weeks before her sex was discovered. She is described as an attractive " boy " and an exemplary assistant. Her excuse was that she wanted to be a man and to earn money to support herself.</p>
      </div>
