      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>EXAMINEE,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEE,</p>
        <p>JULY 11th, 1903.</p>
        <p>President LOUBET FRANCE AND ENGLAND. and the large num-</p>
        <p>ber of French- men who have either accompanied him officially or have come to England "on their own" will prob- ably carry back to France some new impressions of English weather. They have been taught to believe that the sun never shines in England, but despite the storms which were raging round the Scottish coast and bringing incomplated buildings to the ground in Newcastle-on-Tyne and else- where, it appears to have shone very prettily in London for the occasion of the visit of the PRESID- ENT of the French Republic. The temperature fell, but only as if to make the days more endur- able. There was a little rain, but at a time when t could occas ion no inconvenience or discomfort to our French visitors. We may reasonably hope, therefore, that the report . upon our climate will be favourable upon the whole. It has never before happened that we have been visited at one time by so many of those whose duty it is to report upon such matters Something like forty of the most distinguished of French journalists have come over in the PRESI- DENT'S train. They were packed, as our London correspondent has informed us, in a corner of Victoria Station, where,even if they had possessed SAM WELLER'S double million magnifying glasses, they could have seen hardly anything of what was going forward. The treatment of journalists at public functions is one of those things that are done better in France, as these Parisian gentle- men can scarcely fail to remark. But this inci- dent excepted, the reception of PRESIDENT LODE= in this country has been carried out</p>
        <p>exceedingly well, especially as regards the KING'S share in it, and wet may erpect what has taken place during the last two days and also the events of to-day to produce an abun- dant crop of good feeling. No head of the French State has been received on these shores for nearly fifty years. LOUIS NAPOLEON visited us ir. 1855, when we were in the thick of the Crimean War, He was received with popular rejoicings, for he was our ally, but he was also looked upon With suspicion. The blood of the coup d'aat had not been long washed from his bands. He was distrusted. " Ooly the devil knows what he naeanc," wrote the POET LAUREATE, in most uncourtly language. That the distrust was amply justified Mr. KINGLAKE has since proved with much conclusiveness. There has been no arriere pensee about the recaption of President LOUBET. It has been wholly sincere and without reservations. The relations of England and France have been subject to many fluctuations since the French Republic was establibbed. We have had rivalries in Asia and in Africa. We have had most dangerous rivalries in Egypt. In rela- tion to Siamese affairs we came within an ace of war, and the danger was almost equally imminent over the trumpery affair of Fashoda. The Boer War was a great disturber of good rela- tionships. Very numerous indeed have been the incidents which have tended to bring us into conflict with the French mind and the French people. But the desire on both sides for more knowledge of each other and for a closer friend- ship has of late been active, and will he greatly streugthened and deepened by the PRESIDENTS visit and its accompanying circumstances. Many illusions have been cleared away in the last few years, to some extent in England and to a large extent in France. The value of conquests in Asia and Africa is being subjected to a new kind of questi-ming to discern that certain matters which were near to setting our ironclads in motion were not worth fighting about, or at any rate were capable of easy and amicable settlements as in fact events have proved. For the present prevalent good feeling We OW@ much more tO KING EDWARD than to the tact and diplomatic skill of our states- men. HIS MAJESTY has brought to the Throne such a power of arrangement and of softening asperities as was not previously suspected in him. The system by which a King acted as his own Foreign Minister, as in the case of WILLIAM III• became so grievously discredited as to be gen- ' erally regarded as not merely dangerous, but uncoil- . stitutional. But if arguments for its revival were being sought for they might be found in the great success of his present MAJETY in assuaging in al- most all directions that feeling which led pnblie men to make speeches and authors to write books on such topics as" Why are we hated ?" The rapprochement has been most sudden and most decided between France and England. Frenchmen are possibly considering that after all friendship with this country is more natural than alliance with Russia. The mission of the two countries is to be the leaders of civilisa- tion and it may occur that we shall become the joint leaders by and bye when the visit Of PRESIDENT LOUBET has borne its due and appre" priate fruit.</p>
        <p>• The happy condition IRISH LAND BILL. of things which has prevailed in the Hoube of Commons during the Committee stage of the Irish Land Bill was disturbed on Wednor day by a slight breeze. The Government wishea to do a good turn to Trinity College, Dublin,whiell stands to lose by the Bill; and Mr. WymnEtAg'S good friends, the Nationalists, were immediatelY up in arms. The richest educational establishroso° in the country was picked out for exceptional treatment, said Mr. REDMOND. The proposal WO opposed by the solid representation of Ireland. Mr. T. W. RUSSELL, acting with his new &amp;Heel observed that the clause proposed by gr. WYNDHAM would disturb the whole edacational settlement in the sister country, as if that were a prospect likely to perturb or deter Government like the present, bent, as usual, on doing something for its friends. There must have been great astonishment among members when the division bells rang. The sound of those noisy little tintinabulations is so seldom heard nowadays. The division of Wednesday is only the fourth that has taken place whilst the Bill has been in committee. With these few exceptions it has all been graceful concession on one side and graceful acceptance on the other. The side of the House from which the concessions have come hag been almost solely occupied by the Irish Secretary. Other Ministers seem to have assured each other that they can "leave it to WYNDHAM." The aver- age Unionist member probably goes down to the smoke-room and laments over the fact that the Irish members are not now so amusing las they once were. At any rate he disappears from the House, which should pro .- duce some rather good pictures for the illus' rated papers, with Mr. WYNDHAM on his feet amid empty benches blandly addressing himself to the Irish members crowded together in a far corner on the other side. There is much for Englishmen to consider with some seriousness in condition of things so unexampled. The measure which Mr. WYNDHAM is piloting through with such flattering succees is not at all popular with his party. rany gentlemen on the Conservative benches dislike it heartily, but there is an under- standing that in high quarters there is a desire that the Bill shall be passed.Whatevar concessione may require to be made for this reason, and per' haps for some others, those who would have been keen critics, if not active opponents, have di'• sambled their dislike; they have kept away frog' the Chamber. Like the Irish SEcamiAp.y's leagues they have "left it to Wyndham." Liberia criticism has also been silent for the most part. Liberal politicians do not wish to appear t° be hostile to a settlement of the Irish land ques' tion, although it may not be the best that could be made. The case for the general taxpayer can" not be said to have been put with force or effectiveness at any stage of the Bill. Yet for the taxpayer who is making a present of £12,000,000 to the Irish landlords, and who is assuming re" sponsibility to the extent of anything betweel3 £100,000,000 to R150,000,0A.90 on behalf of the Irish tenant, there is surely something that might be usefully said. Is the vast operation that h. being carried out likely to be safe? Will it really settle the land question in Ireland ? We have maintained from the first that it will not do this. The bribe to the landlords will be paid in vain, for the Bill carefully lays down a basis for a nevi agitation by making the purchasing tenant paY average of twenty-five years' purchase, where the average should not be above serentes° or eighteen years. It would be unreasonable ti)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
