      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <p>*ezPoot permanent contentment on such conditions. After a few years the tenant will in all probability turn round and declare that he has been made to Pay too much for the boon conferred upon him— that, in fact, the English Government, a landlords, government, took an unworthy advantage of his 'and-hunger; and when the Irish agriculturist nExt turns round it will not be on the landlord, but on the English Government, which will be in the position of creditor appearing to the small Irish holders as a sort of colossal gombeen man. 111 such a prospect there is much reason for dis- quiet, and there is reason for more disquiet in the fee., that the Government is leaving so many other questions unsettled. There is, for instance, the ease of the labourers, which:was raised on Wednes- 4aY. Mr. WYNDHAM promised a Bill. "Will the 13111 be brought in next session F" asked Colonel tAUNDERSON and some others. Mr. WYNDHA.M would not say. How could ha ? Where will the llovernment be next session?</p>
        <p>Rowley Ward will be in the throes of a taunicipal election on Tuesday, when the vacancy caused by the elavation of Councillor Bennett to the Aldermanic bench has to be filled. The candidates are M. . Adam Horrocks and Mr. facob Sankey, one a Non-trade, and the other an Independent Conservative "—whatever that is. Wa have no cmfidence in such a hybrid political (~Production, and we doubt very much whether Mr. tinkey's description will captivate the Howley *electors. Hid Conservative independence appears ta be a cloak under which he can slyly disclose 43, cu-ttading colours when convenient and likely 1,0 catch votes. Does that make him independent party,however ? We imagine not,and we should flke Mr. Sankey better if he contested the Ward 43. a Conservative pure and simple instead of trying aialy to stand on both aides of the fence. Mr. "aorrocke' profession of faith is plain and unmis- takable, and we should not be at all surprised if it tarried him to victory even in such a Tory strong- old as Howley.</p>
        <p>The municipal trading inquiry has had a shuttle Cock career ever since it was first mooted, and even Vet it is "in the air," so to speak. Tuesday's Council meeting did not advance the matter one etep further than the point it has reached on 'leveret occasions. There have been resolutions, Proposing an inquiry, in the name of quite a num- ber of people, but they have never got beyond the Proposal stage. When they came to be considered by the Council some insuperable objection has always been disowered, and the promising child of a fertile brain has,in each instance, been promptly buried in oblivion. On Tuesday we had a pro- Posal emanating from a joint committee repro- !tenting the Corporation and the Non-Traders,and !t Was generally felt that, at last, the inquiry was 111 eight. But, no, the resolution in the name of Alderman Boltin met with a scarcely better fate than its predecessors. It was not even debated, though a good number of I members had o )nae to the meeting armed for the tray. instead, a proposal, by Councillor F. R. Roberts, ad- Journing the meeting for a week, was carried by One vote. Mr. Roberts' argument was not !Inreasonable. He made it clear that he had not cad time to give notice of amendments he desired to propose, and as it is within the right of any thernber to suggest amendments, he carried his Point. Though the delay was unwelcome we think Ile only fair course was adopted. in agreeing to ! "la adjournment. Alderman Bolton, the proposer of the inquiry resolution, was exceedingly irate and there and then resigned all connection with the Zatter. We now learn that the resolution has „it./.en brought forward again by Councillor Tinnion, 'TEI will be discussed at the next monthly meeting °I the Council instead of on Tuesday next.</p>
        <p>unclllor F. R. Roberts has already given .4-'4lte of his amendments, which are to clauses 2, t'l,l4d 9, and for the sake of comparison we again onolish elsewhere the text of the resolution, gather with the amendments. The main point the proposed inquiry to which Councillor Itoberts objects is clear. He is against any of those gentlemen who have already been accusers taking subseqaently the position of judges. Again We must admit that the objection is not unexpected tr unreasonable. For people who have had charges of immoral trading brought against them—and 'oouncillor Roberts is looked upon as their spokes In%n—this is not an illogical attitude. They are entitled to make an objection on such grounds,but, and this is the question the Council will have to tiecide, has that objection to be upheld at the cost 91 burking the inquiry? This is certainly what it Involves, for the Non-Traders are not likely to 411brait to any such restriction on the nn.rnination of their own three Commissioners, and Without their acquiescence no inquiry of any real c.ervice can be held. It is obvious that the Coun- II Will have to make a definite concession one way .°l' the other. If they take the course suggested 11331 Councillor Roberts, then there will probably no inquiry—unless the Non-Traders prove more 'conrarnodating than there is any reason t 9 anti- c.l.Pate, The Council seems to us bound to take !,4eB line of least resistance, and in that case there 18 little hope oe Councillor Roberts obtaining the concession he desires, for the Non-Traders have Al3r.oved, at the polls, their right to,in some degree, `4lntate the nature of the inquiry.</p>
        <p>There was a lengthy discussion on the proposed "vo street from Sankey street to Itylands street, aid eventually this also was deferred owing to the 11°5nty information vouchsafed by Aldennan Sutton who introduced the scheme. It was, we under- arid, fully discussed at the meeting of the ‘,-eneral Purposes Committee on Thursday evening aid eventually rejected. The opposition was from 111,4ti0u5 sources and for various reasons. The idge street shopkeepers do not want a rival Street so near, the construction of which might 1,180 defer the widening of their own thoroughfare. he the Non-Traders were also in opposition on '4le ground of expense—which was put at the Letisiderable sum of £ll,ooo—and the new street "a no chance" at all, at all !" There seems to be a Spirit of restlessness actuating some of our tea nnicipal governors just now. Like .6klexander they are sighing for fresh Worlds to conquer, but unlike him they have not conquered those" in hand." Widening Bridge- Street and other improvements are more urgent I:latters and call for all the attention the Corpora- -I,ion can spare. As a matter of policy we have no i.'nubt that the cutting of a new street here would " a wise step in the light of Warrington's antici- tated growth and development, but the present ardly seems the proper time to carry out the rleme. There is no reason, as far as we can see, owever, why the Property Purchasing Sub-Com- loittee should not bestir itself and secure at once property which it will be necessary to demolish ,do the construction of the street at some future vEt,te. Delay in this respect only means inflated ,tlitles, and it should be possible to strike a bargain ,`,'°l7 which would prove an invaluable one in the tnture.</p>
        <p>„ :No ordinary Town Council business was trans- :2ted "with neatness and despatch,” as the trades- Zan says. The business of the Committees was rinetly routine, and aroused little comment. Some Ilterest, was, however, shown in Councillor Lord's sillestions respecting the Water Committee's slack onntracts. Alderman Francomb replied and btThnised a full report on the question from Mr. tkßee for the next Council meeting. Meanwhile ,119 original contractors are supplying the slack, ;tl,d the Committee has also generously used up ..„0.44t the Slate Company happened to have in nelr. There is a prospect of the tension slacken- with Mr. Dams' report,and of the chairman and -,I neer getting some sleep o' nights.</p>
        <p>1,/ir. R. Cameron, M.P., has been a frequent tor to this district recently bnc his addressas /1 always interesting and valuable and he never 44(i's to get a pAtient hearing. On Monday he v.l,9reased the Liberals at Penketh and had some- to say on Mr. Chamberlain's tarift proposals.</p>
      </div>
