      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <p>Oddfellowe Picnic. The annual picnic in connection .with the Grand United Order of Oddfellows Juvenile Burial Society took place on Saturday when thirty three members spent a very pleasant time at Bakewell and Afatlock. The party left Central Station in a reserved carriage at 5.45 a.m. and arrived at Bakewell at 8.39. After partaking of breakfast at the " Red Lion" hotel they drove to Haddon Hall. Subsequently they visited Chatsworth House, and then journeyed through Darley Dale to Matlock. Here dinner was awaiting them at the " Temple Hotel." The party arrived home at nine o'clock well pleased with the outing. The Mono-Rail.</p>
        <p>Next week a very imporcant Public Issue will take place via. The Manchester and. Liverpool Electric Express Railway Company," which has been incor- porated under Act of Parliament. The Company will be under most excellent auspices with a strong Board and having a capital of £2,100,000 in £lO shares. Particulars will be found in the Advertising Columris of our next issue. BASSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51,53, Bridge-street, and 15 re-street, \Varrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>. . Sudden Death. Ralph Norman, 65 years of age, an outdoor porter at Bank Quay Station, who resided at 19, Dakin's-yard died with painful suddenness on Monday morning. P.C. Fish was on duty in Horseruarket-street when received information that a man was lying in the public convenience in Bewsey-street. He proceeded to the place and saw deceased lying there, apparently very ill. With the assistance of Joseph Gerrard, 4, Bake- house-yard, witness took him to the Infirmary, but on arriving at the institution, it was found that Norman was dead.—The inquest was held on Monday evening, and it was stated that deceased was often troubled with pains in the back and fainting bouts.—A verdict of "Death from natural causes" was returned. A painful feature of the case is that deceased's wife died suddenly a short time ago in St. George's Mission.</p>
        <p>FIRE GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street,Warringion.—Advt.</p>
        <p>King's Picture and Bust for the Town Hall.</p>
        <p>An artist's proof engraving of the Coronation por- trait of His Majesty the King, by Luke Fildes, has just been hung in No, 1 Committee Roma at the Town Hall. The engraving is an excellent one, and by many competent judges it is considered to be an even better likeness of the King than the original portrait. The engraving is signed by Mr. Lake Fildes, the artist, and Mr. Pratt, the engraver. The engrav- ing is sure to become more valuable as only 200 artists proofs have been struck off. .It has been framed by Messrs. T. Agnew and Sons of Manchejter, in a frame, the design of which was specially -approved by His Majesty.—The bust of the Kinc, by Alfred Drury,A.R.A., which is to be presented to the town by the Mayor (Mr. J. Charlton Parr), as a memento of the Coronation,is at present being exhibited at the Royal Academy. It will shortly find a place in the entrance hall at the Town Hall. UNDER the CLOCK or GOOD-BOOM—Advt. Golf at Hoylake. -</p>
        <p>• "During -last week" says the "Manchester Guardian," " there was a good deal of interesting golf at Hoylake. Not only was there the match between the team brought from Cannes by the Grand Duke Michel of Russia and Mr. Arthur Crosfield's team,, but also the finals of the Queen Victoria commemora- tion competition and the foursome competition for the two cups presented by Mr. C. E. Goodwin. In the team match the Hoylake golfers proved the stronger. When the first match was played at Cannes Mr. Hilton and Mr. Graham were disappointing. If I remember rightly, Mr. Hilton was defeated by Mr. Hillyard, and Mr. Graham was easily beaten by Mr. Whigham, but Mr. Ball was too good for Mr. Edwards, the strongest player of the Cannes Golf Club. This time Mr. Edwards was missing from the visitors' team, and Mr. Graham from the home team. However, on Wed- nesday Mr. Whigham had to give way to Mr. Ball,who was no less than nine up on the first round, while Mr. Hilton wreaked his vengeance upon Mr. Finch-Hatton as a substitute for Mr. Hillyard, the thirty-six hole match being over at the twenty-third hole. Altogether on the first day's play Mr. Crosfield's team was lead- ing by forty holes to nine, but as the result. was counted by matches the Hoylake golfers, including the foursome results, eventually won by seven matches to four. After the first. day's play there was only one match in which the verdict was reversed—in the game between Mr. R..Greville and Mr. G. Barrington. The latter player was leading by one hole after the first eighteen holes, but at the finish his opponent was the victor."</p>
        <p>FOR CLASS AND CHINA. of every description try J.S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>TRAMWAYS. Record Receipts on Walking Day.</head>
        <p>THE TRAMWAYS. Record Receipts on Walking Day.</p>
        <p>Below we give. a comparative return of the tramways receipts during the recent holidays and of those of a year ago. It will be seen that the fares taken on Walking day totalled up to £lO2 17s. 2d. This is the largest amount received on a single day since the inauguration of the system in Warrington. It is con- ceivable that if the weather had been less threatening the receipts on Walking Day would have been even larger. The highest amount previously receivcd on a single day was ,tB7, so that Walking Day now stands an easy first. A. year ago only the Sankey Bridges and. La tchford routes were open. The system was then comparatively new and many rode on the cars for the mere novelty of the thing. The average per car mile on Walking Day, 1902, was 25 pence whereas last Friday the average was only 1597 pence. In the last named calculation five routes are taken into con- sideration whereas there were only two open in 1902. IThe receipts during the _holidays were as follows :</p>
      </div>
