        <p>' 14: i • , lz,,e: t.• , ; f ,*, "I • R 'T s, , t i i .11 l': 1 4 , , -,, • '• 1.,!-.:. ' 4': • • , it i :,.,,... ! , i 1....:- I 1 i v i'- 1 t !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>He was bold enough to advance the opinion that Mr. Chamberlain had started the controversy with the object of drawing a red herring across the path and diverting the people's attention from the mis- deeds of the Government in regard to education, licensing and other matters. This view may or may not be the right one, but if it is, such an electioneering dodge will as surely fail as the protective scheme itself seems destined to do. Nonconformists are not likely to forget the Education Act and the injustices it has im- posed upon them, even if the subject is driven into the backgrouni by the discussion on protection, and all friends of temperance are equally alive to the Government's partisanship for and even championing of the brewers' interests. Trades Unionists are also keenly sensible of the way many of their privileges have been tampered with in recent years and of the chilling indifference of the Government towards labour generally. The Government has a bad record behind it and it is impossible to obscure it by any discussion on pro- posed fiscal changes, with old age pensions thrown in. MI 4113iN AiriZA Mill 1,918 A vniEl The Committee of the Warrington Training College are unusually fortunate in obtaining the services of distinguished local men to distribute the prizes and address the students at the annual prize-giving. Last year, Sir Edward Russell tf Liverpool performed the duty and on Saturday last, Sir James Hoy,of Manchester, proved equally acceptable in discharging a like function. It was only natural that something would be said on the new Education Act. Sir James by spoke somewhat hopefully of the measure as it affected Manchester, where he has been appointed Chairman of the Education Com- mittee After a long experience of local govern- ment in Manchester, he said he was led to hope and believe that the interests of education would in the long run be Advantaged by the change tnat had been made. We hope this proguosttcation will prove to be true, not only in MaLchester but; in Warrington. It is too early,as yet,to speak with any definiteness on the subject. Manchester has not shown any disposition to eagerly partake of the advantages of the Act, for it was only this month that the Education Committee commenced its duties. It remains to be seen whether the new duty cast upon the authority will be accepted, as Sir James Hay anticipates, in a large spirit and with a desire, above all, for the benefit of those who come to be taught at the schools. In some cases which we have heard of, this can hardly be s tid to have been the case.</p>
        <p>The French journalists who accompanied Presi- dent Loubet on his visit to London seem to have been most impressed by the ceremony at the Guildhall. It was the tableau which eclipsed all others, with its medireval ceremony and quiet opulence. We, for our part, have never been able to understand how it is that the very title Lord Mayor of L mdon should have a confusing effect on the French mind. The Chairman of the L3ndon County Council is a much more important person; but the visitor passes him by as of small signifi- cance. It is, of course, because of the traditional interest,because the commercial greatness of Eng- land was to alarge extent established by the City of London, and because the magnificence is free of glare and glitter—one of half-tones of old-world stateliness, and yet of rare quality and richness. "This vision of Old England at the Guildhall will never fade from my memory "—so writes the editor of Le Main; and he adds, "It forms one of the most ineffaceable impressions of M. Loubet." The procession, it is said, reminded the foreign observer of those legendary groups that Wagner has so often put into his works from " Tann- hauser " to " Parsifal" ; and,againAhe comparison is made to a fairy scene, which shows how the mind was carried away, for we who know them would be the last to think of City Aldermen as fairies. But the old countries are losing their historic attractiveness of pageant. France: dare not keep up monarchical customs; and America has no institutions to compare for a moment in interest with those of the City of London. The German festivals, though they have poetry and art, are for the most rad coarser than our own. The new regulations regarding the admission of pupil teachers issued by the Board of Education are intended, as Mr. R. L. Morant, the new Secre- tary to the Board,stated in his preparatory memor- andum, to secure for the pupil teacher a more complete and continuous education, and to make the period of service in an elementary school a time of probation and training rather than of too early practice in teaching. The regulations, in fact, put into consolidated form the principle which has long been recognised in some of the more enlightened educational centres, that more comprehensive training is necessary betore boys and girls should be allowed to take charge in ele- mentary schools. The most important points in the new regulations are the raising of the age at which pupil teuhers will in future be admitted and the abolition of the probationers. On and after August Ist, 1904, pupil teachers admitted under the regulations must not be under sixteen years of age, except in rural districts, where the limit will be fifteen years ; and after August Ist, 1905, they will not be permitted to serve in a public elementary school more than half the time the school is open, and they will he re- quired to receive half-time instruction in an approved pupil-teacher centre throughout their engagement. The probationers, who are no longer to exist as such, are a class of very young teachers between the ages of thirteen and sixteen, and the Board desires that all boys and girls who intend to become pupil teachers shall, uutil they are admitted, spend the whsle of their school time either at a secondary school or at preparatory classes. These two alterations in themselves con- stitute a very healthy revolution in the system of elementary education, and they cannot but go a considerable way towards securing that efficiency in the earlier grades of the teaching profes•von Iwhich every educationist recognises as necossary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>WARRINGTON BUILDING SOCIETY SENSATION. Writs Issued against Former Officials.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON BUILDING SOCIETY SENSATION. Writs Issued against Former Officials.</p>
        <p>We understand that writs have been issued at the suit of the directors of the Warrington Permanent Benefit Building Society against Mr. Robert Proud (the late secretary) and Mr. James Bolton and Mr. William Dick (formerly auditors of the Society) in the Nisi Prins Court at Liverpool.—ln the case of Mr. Proud, the directors seek to recover the money which they allege he has misappropriated out of the funds of the Building Society. The claim against the two former auditors (Messrs. Bolton and Dick) is for damages for alleged negligence in the discharge of their duties as auditors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ENCAMPMENT. Latest Arrangements.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ENCAMPMENT. Latest Arrangements.</p>
        <p>The arrangements for the Volunteer encampment are practically completed and the authorities are confident thatlthe men will spend a very pleasant time during their sojourn in the Isle of Man from July 25th to August Bth. The camping ground is beautifully situ- ated at Lewaigue, near Ramsey, and every effort is being made for the comfort of the Volunteers. The battalion will leave Central Station, Warrington for Liverpool in two special trains between 8.30 and 9 o'clock on Sat- urday evening the 25th instant. The men will no doubt he pleased to learn that the "Empress Queen "has been specially chartered for the passage to the island and the Warrington Volunteers will embark at ten o'clock in company with the 2nd Cheshire Volunteers. For the convenience of the men and everyone concerned it has been arranged that the disembarkation at Ramsey shall not take place until day-break. On arrival at the camping ground light refreshments will be served.and the volunteers will then settle down to well-earned ' repose. Very little work will be done on Sunday, but work will commence in earnest on Monday. As usual each man will receive a pair of boots and a slouch hat, but. this year a new departure will be made in the shape of an extra gift of a shirt and navy belt. Should hot weather be experienced and parades in "shirt-sleeves " ordered, all the men will thus be similarly clad. Arrangements are being made for the trans-shipment of the horses of the Mounted Infantry Company to Ramsey as it is impossible,dnring the height of the season, to obtain the requisite number of horses in the Isle of Man. The Church of England section of the battalion will attend Divine service at the Parish Church on Sunday the 19th inst.</p>
        <p>FRIEMIIa;=Z.,,MSJ</p>
