      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, July, 11, 1903</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. THOMAS CROOKS.</p>
        <p>The death occurred on Thursday at St. Helens of Hr. Thomas Crooks, private secreta 7 to Sir Dlvid Gamble, Bart. He was a well-known Churchman and Freemason He was also a prominent member of the Independent Order of OddHlows, and was treasurer of the St. Helens district for 15 years and Secretary of the Rose of Sharon Lodge. He leaves a widow and thirteen children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>ST. HELENS MAN'S AILD ACT.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS MAN'S AILD ACT.</p>
        <p>James Devine, aged thirty-two, living at Peasley Cross, was admitted to the Providence Hospital, St. Helens, in the early hours of Sunday morning, having cut his hand very severely and severed the arteries and sinews at the wrist. It was alleged that Devine was taking part in a violent quarrel and struck at a house window, with the result above stated. He lost a tremendous quantity of blood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>EXPLOSION AT A ST. HELENS COPPERWORKS.</head>
        <p>EXPLOSION AT A ST. HELENS COPPERWORKS.</p>
        <p>A serious explosion took place on Sunday evening at the Old Ravenhead Copperworks, St. Helens, belonging to Messrs. Thomson, by which two men were very seri- ously hurt. It appears that a copper furnace, attended by William Joyce, who lives at the works and a man named Myers suddenly exploded, and the men were shockingly burnt all over. They were both removed to the Providence Hospital in an unconscious state. Myers lived at 1, Copperas-court, and is forty years of age, while Joyce is much older. The accident was of a most unusual kind. While the men were attending to the furnace it suddenly collapsed, and the molton copper poured into the flrehole and out at the sides. Myers saw the collapse begin, and shouted to Joyce,lsut neither of them was able to get out of the way before they were encircled with flames and fumes and severely hurt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>LABOUR CANDIDATE FOR ST. HELMS.</head>
        <p>LABOUR CANDIDATE FOR ST. HELMS.</p>
        <p>A conference of the St.. Helens Trades Council .and representatives of the National Labour Representation Committee was held on Saturdayaftemoonat St. Helens Town Hall, Mr. J. A. Seddon presiding. The following resolution was at the close communicated to the Press: "That this conference proceed to accept names for a Labour candidate for St. lielens,such names to be sub- mitted to the trades and labour bodies who shall be in- vited to send in names within one month from this date and when the list is completed to be sent to all bodies concerned, and a final conference called to definitely decide upon a candidate not later that three weeks after the close of the nomination." Two candidates were nominated on Saturday, viz„ Mr. T. Glover, J.P„ miners agent, and Mr. J. A. Seddon, president of the Council. Mr. Sexton who was nominated, but declined, explained the constitution of the National Labour Party, and made it perfectly clear that only the candidate that accepted that constitution would receive the support of the National organisation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>POLICEMAN ASSAULTED AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court on Monday, John Keogh, labourer, James-street, Widnes was charged on remand with assaulting Police-constable Lord. The constable stated that he found the prisoner in James-street on Friday night behaving in a very disorderly way. Witness remonstrated with him, and told him to go home quietly. The man, who was under the influence of drink, at once attacked the constable and struck him violently in the face.—Police-d,onstable Macedeld corroborated.—A fine of 10s. and costs or 14 days was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>SATURDAY NIGHT SCENE AT RUNCOILN.</head>
        <p>SATURDAY NIGHT SCENE AT RUNCOILN.</p>
        <p>On Monday at the Runcorn Police Court an elderly woman named Mary Ellen Rose was charged with hav- ing been drunk in Percival-lane on Saturday night. While Police Constable Mottishead had a prisoner in custody Rose came up and got hold of him and tried to get him away. The woman was then taken into custody,and on the way to the station her daughter (Maria Evans) came on the scene and trio 1 to pull her mother away. She was also drunk, and was taken into custody. Rose was committed for twenty-one days and Evans for one month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ASSAULT ON THE POLICE.</head>
        <p>ASSAULT ON THE POLICE.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor (Alderman W. W. Gossage), and Mr. Thomas Smith, John .Keogh, James-street, Widnes, a labourer, was charged on remind from Saturday with assaulting P. C. Lord.—The constable stated that on Friday even- ing he found the prisoner drunk and disorderly in James-street. He remonstrated with the man and told him to go home quietly. The prisoner then turned upon the policeman and struck him violently in the face.— P.C. Macefield corroborated.—A fine of 10s. and costs or 14 days was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WALKING MATCH AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>WALKING MATCH AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>There have been many walking contests in Widnes, but none of them has evoked so much interest as that last Saturday between William Abbott and Joseph Caldwell. The course was from Prescot Church to Widnes Park gates, a distanee of six miles, for a stake of £lO. Caldwell led all the way, and won by 50 yards. The finish was witnessed by hundreds of spectators.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>WANTED NEW CLOTHES.</head>
        <p>WANTED NEW CLOTHES.</p>
        <p>John Gray, an inmate of the Runcorn Union Work- house, was charged at the Runcorn Police Court last Friday with having destroyed his clothing on Thursday. From the evidence given by Lewis Anthony, the indus- trial trainer at the house, the man was admitted on Wednesday night and on Thursday was given some oakum to pick. Later on it wai found that he had destroyed his trousers and boots with the object of getting new clothes. lie was committed to gaol for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>ASSAULT ON A. RUNCORN PUBLICAN.</head>
        <p>ASSAULT ON A. RUNCORN PUBLICAN.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at the Runcorn Police Court, Annie Schou, 24, Cooper-street, Sutton, was summoned for having assaulted James Bradley, licensee of the Three Crowns beerhouse, King-street. Mr. Lake appeared for the complainant, and said about nine o'clock on Satur- day, June 27, his client was serving behind the bar when the defendant and another woman came in. As Schon was drank he refused to serve her and asked her to go out. She became insolent, and he then attempted to put her out, when she struck him in the face. The defendant said the complainant did not give her time to go out.—The defendant was further summoned for assaulting Mrs. Bradley, wife of the above complainant. Mr. Lake stated that later on the same night as the above assault took place the defendant returned to the public-house, and without any provocation struck Mrs. Bradley on the head and threw a glass at her.—The defendant was ordered to pay the costs, 13s. 6d., and to be bound over in each case, or in default four months' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>SYMPATHETIC _JURY.</head>
        <p>A SYMPATHETIC _JURY.</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning, Mr. J. IL Fisher (deputy coroner) held an inquest at Dutton Locks Cottage, Pickering's Bridge, upon the body of Mary Tittle, daughter of Roger Tittle, of Pickering's Bridge, farm labourer.—Sarah Tittle, the mother, said that at 7.30 on the let July, she left deceased and her other children sitting on the doorstep of her cottage, and a few minutes later heard cries that deceased was in the river Weaver. She went at once and saw men grappling for the body, which they recovered later.— John Dutton, labourer,of Pickering's Bridge, deposed to hearing shouts, and running to the river. He could not see the child, but set to work with grappling irons, and ultimttely recovered the body.—The jury returned a verdict of " Acaidentaldrowning."—Mr. Sydney Stokes (foreman of the jury) expressed their sympathy with the bereaved fatuity, and suggested that they should give their fees to the mother, to which the jury unani- mously agreed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>SALVAGE BY A RUNCORN CA PTAIN.</head>
        <p>SALVAGE BY A RUNCORN CA PTAIN.</p>
        <p>Information has been reogyed in Liverpool from Bombay stating that the steamer Henry Bolchow,under the command of Captain William Fowles, of Runcorn, has towed into the port of Lingha, in the Persian Gulf, the Liverpool steamer Oceana in a disabled condition. The Oceana, with a valuable cargo, was on a voyage from Manchester to Bussorceh and Bushire, and shortly after leaving the formerport she came to a sudden stop. On examination being made it was found that the tunnel shaft was broken, the vessel thus being left in a crippled condition. After drifting about in this con- dition for some time she fell in with the Henry Bol- chow, which took her in tow, • A POLITICAL SQUABBLE.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, before Messrs J. Ockleston and J. Pritchard, Herbert Johnson and Thomas Clay, of Hughes-court, were summoned for having used obscene language on the 29th ult. P.C. Brayford said he was in Church-street and heard some shouting. He went to the Court and heard the men using bad language. They were falling out as to which Government was in, Tory or Liberal. (Laughter.) De- fendants said they were having a few words, but did not use any bad language. There was some loud talking in another house, but the lights were out when the officer came on the scene. Defendants were given the benefit of the doubt and discharged.—Bridget Ann Jones, Hughes-court was fined Bs. 6d. for having , used obscene language on the 27th ult. P.C. Preston proved the case.—James Hamilton, a boy, was fined 4s.6d.for stone throwing in Canal-street on the 26th ult. P.C. Oakes proved the case. PRESENTATION AT RUNCORN—MR. J. MEADOWCROPTS'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>RETIREMENT.</head>
        <p>RETIREMENT.</p>
        <p>At the offices of the Upper Mersey Navigation Com- missioners, on Friday evening a presentation was made on behalf of the office staff and general employs of the Manchester Ship; Canal Company (Bridgewater Depart- ment) in the Runcorn district to Mr. John Meadow- croft, who some little time ago resigned the position of Runcorn agent to the company after a long record of faithful service. The gifts were two large and hand- somely framed oil paintings of Mr. and Mrs. Meadow- croft and a framed illuminated address. The paintings had been done by Mr. H. Crowther, of Runcorn, who is to be complimented upon the success of his work. The text of the address is as follows Presented to John Meadowcroft, Esq., by the office staff and dock em- ploy&amp; of the Runcorn section of the Manchester Ship Canal Co. (Bridgewater Department), accompanied with oil paintings of Mr. and Mrs. Meadowcroft, upon his retirement from the management of the Runcorn Docks, &amp;c., after 25 years' meritorious serviee, during which time the trade has been largely increased and the dock estate at Runcorn largely extended. 30th April. 1903"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>ROUGH ON THE LANDLADY.</head>
        <p>ROUGH ON THE LANDLADY.</p>
        <p>A curious tale was told at the Runcorn Police Court on Saturday, before 31,r. E. liaison, when Angus McDougall, a seaman, wig charged with having been drunk in Devonshire-square early on Saturday morning. P.S. Cooper said he found the man drunk and asleep in the street. He had £3 14s. H. upon him. Defendant said he was paid from a German ship at Liverpool on Friday and came to the Ellesmere Arms Inn at Runcorn. It was his intention to go to Glasgow later in the night but he got too much drink and on the wayto the station it got the better of him. The Magistrates' Clerk said the defendant was going upstairs at the public-house and the landlady was following when he fell upon her and broke one of her legs. Inspector Egerton said he saw the landlady that morning. She said it was a pure accident and she did not prefer any charge against defendant. Mr. liaison informed defendant that he was fortunate in not having been robbed. He had given a good deal of trouble and would be fined ss.and ss. costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>1......1.111.......Ar NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>1......1.111.......Ar NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held on Monday afternoon in the Town Hall, Eirlestown. Mr. T. Ormand (vice-chairman) presided, and there were also present: Messrs. J. Simpson, W. Gilmour, N. Siddeley, J. W. Emmett, G. F. Clarke, E. T. Evans, Dr. Latham, A. Bowes (surveyor), and C. Porter (assistant Clerk). THE FLAGGING OF MARKET-STREET.—LIVELY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>DISCUSSION.</head>
        <p>DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Siddeley moved anamendment that the resolution passed at the meeting of the Highways and Sanitary Committee referring to the flagging of Market-street be rescinded. He said that he thought they ought to take into consideration the places that really required flagging. In his opinion Market-street was in very good condition, and ought to last at least a year or two. Wargrave-road on one side was in a very bad condition, and really did want attending to. Dr. Li.tham seconded the amendment proposed by Mr. Siddeley. He must congratulate that member upon the courage he had displayed by proposing such a resolution after last Council meeting,when such Infamous conduct took place. Mr. Gilmour: I must object to those remarks. The Chairman: I did not catch them. Mr. Gilmour: He said something about the infamous conduct of the Council.</p>
        <p>Dr. Latham: I said nothing about the Council. The Chairman: I must ask you to withdraw those words.</p>
        <p>Dr. Latham: I made no reference to the Council. The Chairman: I cannot allow you to proceed unless you withdraw. _ _ __ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Dr. Latham : Ido withdraw.</p>
        <p>Dr. Latham, continuing, said ho seconded the amend- ment because in his opinion Market-street was in fairly good condition, and he thought it would last for some years—at any rate until the tramway went down the street. He thought it most unnecessary, and it would look better if they attended to other streets in the township. He could name a dozen parts of the town- ship that all required attending to. He thought it was unwise of the Council to spend the money on Market- street at the present time. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour: I object to this matter being brought forward now.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: On what grounds? Mr. Gilmour: Mr. Siddeley has not given notice of motion.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: There is no need in this case.</p>
        <p>Mr. Clarke said that he wished to support the amend- ment. He thought when the matter was up in com- mittee they did not know what was the condition of Marketzstreet. He had since the committee meeting taken the opportunity of looking down Market-street on both sides, and he thought its present condition did not call for repair. He thought it would last for two or three years. Wargrave-road was in a very bad con- dition, and on one side of the road the water lodged in the middle of the footpath and caused very great incon- venience. He thought the money would be better spent if they attended to that path. Mr. Simpson also supported the amendment.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour: Will ion allow me- The Chairma.n : No, I think you have spoken enough already. Mr. Emmett said he should like to ask whether the Surveyor reported that Market-street required flagging, or was it first brought up bya member. The Chairman The resolution was passed on the statement of the Surveyor. The Chairman sl that before they took a vote on the matter he wNuld like to say that when they had I passed a resolution in committee and the matter had been duly considered they had continually moved the rescinding of such resolutions. During the last four or five years they had done a great deal down Wargrave- road. He thought he wouldibe right in saying that they had spent more money on Wargrave-road in repairing and making footpaths than any street of its class in the district. Mirket-street was one of the busiest streets in the town, and he thought the main arteries of a town should be considered first, and then such streets as Wargrave-road. . _</p>
        <p>On the resolution-being put to the meeting, five voted or and four against the rescinding of the resolution. TECEINICAL INSTRUCTION.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee the Clerk reported the.l receipt by the treasurer of a cheque for £4l 10s. from i the County Council, being £4O 10s. final instalment of grant, and £1 capitation grant for the session 1902-3. It was resolved that the Finance Committee recom- mended to pass a cheque in favour of the Viaduct Institute Technical Instruction Committee for the amount. With respect to the letter from the Clerk of the County Council (submitted atthe last meeting of the committee) as to the payment of special grants for the encouragement of technical instruction in urban districts a letter was read from the Secretary of the Viaduct Institute Technical Instruction Conunittee, stating thatany sum which may be allocated to this district would be spent in making good any deficiency which might arise in carrying on the evening continuation classes conducted under the Board of Education in this district during the session 1902-3, and the balance, if any spent in accordance with a scheme to be first submitted to the Technical Instruction Committee for its approval, and that in addition to the sum of £B5 which, had for some years been annually voted by the Urban District Council a further sum of at least £5O would be given by private subscribers for prizes in connection with the classes for the:session referred to.—lt was resolved that the Clerk be instructed to forward such information to the Director of Education for the county.—A letter was read from the Director of Education, stating that at the last meeting of the Higher Education Sub-committee it was decided to recommend to the Lancashire Education Committee onthe 20th July next "that, as a temporary expedient, the allocation of grants as made by the late Technical instruction Committee, and the delegation as heretofore of powers to urban districts,be continued for the session 1903-4." The minutes were passed. THE FIRE BRIGADE SUPERINTENDENT.</p>
        <p>The Cleric submitted to the Gas Committee the reso- lution of the Fire Brigade and Ambuldnce Committee with respect to the appointment of the gas works manager (Mr. J. T. Haggard) as superintendent of the fire brigade, and Mr. E. P. Cleworth (meter inspector) as deputy superintendent, which resolution was referred by the Gas Committee to the sub-committee for con- sideration, when it was resolved that the appointment of these officers be approved. The minutes were adopted. HIGHWAYS AND SANITARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above committee, a letter was read from the Local Government Board, enclosing their formal sanction (dated the Bth June 1903),t0 the borrow- i,tg by the Council of the sum of £4,324 for works of private street improvement in Legh-street, Heald-street, Bank-street, Brick-street, Back Market-street, and Brunswick-road, the loan to be repayable within a period of six years from the date of the borrowing. It was resolved that the matter of the payment of the expenses by owners as soon as the streets are completed be considered by the Chairman of the Finance Com- mittee, the Clerk, and the Surveyor. With reference to the sum of £lBO set apart in the estimates for the kerb- ing and flagging of streets, it was proposedthat the foot- paths on both sides of Market-street from Earle-street to Legh-street be re-flagged. It was then proposed and seconded as an amendment, that the footpath in War- grave-road, leading from the railway bridge to the corner of Old Wargrave-road be flagged. On theamend- merit being put four members of the Committee voted for and five against the same, and it was declared lost. The original proposition was then put and carried. The minutes were passed. MR. CARNEGIE AND THE COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee a letter was read from Mr. Carnegie, stating that he had been unable hitherto to reply to the letter which wasad- dressed tohim on the 20th December last regarding a public library, and asking for particulars to be again sent to him. It was resolved that the clerk be instructed to fill in, and return to Mr. Carnegie, the list of queries enclosed with his letter. A letter was read from the General Manager of the London and North-Western Railway Company, acknowledging the receipt of the clerk's letter with respect to Newton-le-Willows station and stating that the matter would receive attention. It was resolved that the surveyor be directed to report to the committee as to public places in the district suitable for the provision of seats for the use of the inhabitants. The minutes were carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>MR. CARNEGIE ANCASHTON-IN-MAKERFIELD.</head>
        <p>MR. CARNEGIE ANCASHTON-IN-MAKER- FIELD.</p>
        <p>Offer of £5,000 A speciai meeting of the Ashton District Council was held on Thursday afternoon, for the purpose of consider- ing the offer of Mr. Carnegie of a sum of money for the erection of a free library. The Clerk read a letter from Mr. Carnegie stating that. ha would be pleased to give £5,000 to the Council provided that his usual terms be carried out.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robins, of Lord Gerard's estate, also stated that he had been instructed to offer the Council several sites, to be selected by them. The site would be absolutely free. lie also stated that the present Lord Gerard is also associated with the movement. The Chairman moved a vote of thanks to both Mr. Carnegie and Lord Gerard for their handsome gifts, and it was eventually decided to acoept the offers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <p>- • • „. - - • •••</p>
        <p>THE HOWLEY WARD VACANCY.</p>
        <p>Monday was the last day for receiving nominations of candidates to fill the vacancy in Howley Ward caused by the election of Councillor Bennett to an Aldermanic seat. There are two candidates in the field—Mr. Adam Horrocks, the Non-Traders' nominee, and Mr. Jacob Sankey, who comes out as an Independent Conservative, —and the election will take place on Tueschty next. The following nominations were delivered at the Town Clerk's office : _ _</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Adam Horrocks is proposed by Mr. Frederick Leigh. seconded by Mr. Thomas' Chantler, and sup- ported by Messrs. Ed. Lawson, H. J. Pearce, A. Taylor, J. W. Bowers, Henry Joliey, Henry Heald, R. Lee, and E. Pennington. In a second paper Mr. Horrocks is proposed by Mr. William Adam Leslie, and seconded by Mr. .George Shakeshaft.</p>
        <p>Jac-ob Sankey, dairyman, 21, Church-street, is nominated by Alderman Bennett, seconded by Councillor Davies, and supported by Messrs. .1. Hedg- cock, J. P. Oxley, J. Birchall, T. Sankey, J. Gresty,W. Daniels, E. Clough, and E. Naylor. In a second paper the candidate is proposed by Alderman Bolton, and seconded by Mr. William Box. In a third paper the proposer is Mr. John Percival Garst, and the seconder Mr. John Dewhurst, and in a fourth paper Mr. Sankey islproposed by Councillor John Evans, and seconded by Mr, John Gandy. MR. SANKEY'S ELECTION ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sankey'saddress—to the electors is published in our advertising columns. The candidate says he is a determined advocate of economy in the spending of public money is opposed to all improper and illegal trading with members of the Corporation, and would vote against such trading. ARRANGEMENTS FOR THE POLLING.</p>
        <p>As already indicated the poll will take place at the National schools, Church-street, on Tuesday. The booths will be open at 8 o'clock in the morning and remain open until 8 p.m. The presiding officers will be Messrs. W. E. Fowler and R. Crompton, and the poll clerks, Messrs. A. Clive and A. Sankey. The counting of the votes will take place at the Town Hall immediately after the close of the poll. The result will be known shortly before nine o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>MEETING LAST NIGHT. The Candidature of Mr. Horrocks. MR. NORBURY WILLIAMS AND HIS . EVIDENCE IN LONDON.</head>
        <p>MEETING LAST NIGHT. The Candidature of Mr. Horrocks. MR. NORBURY WILLIAMS AND HIS . EVIDENCE IN LONDON.</p>
        <p>At the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, last evening a meeting in support of the candidature of Mr. Adam Horrocks was held. Mr. James Dodds presided, and ho was supported on the platform by the candidate and Mr. Norbury Williams (Manchester), Mr. Councillor Pemberton, Mr. A. Hahne, Mr. Henry Thornton, and Mr. S. Kenyon. The CHAIRMAN said he wished to call serious atten- tion to the gravity of the occasion, and the supreme duty of the hour which was to return their good friend, Mr. AdamHorrocks for the Howley Ward by such a majority as should satisfy the two political parties and all whom it may conoernthat there must be no doubt in regard to the loyal and complete adhesion of the rateyayers to the programme of the non-trading movement. The few non-trading members in the Council, he contended, had done their duty. They had exposed many serious and some scandalous irregularities. (Hear, hear) They had scotched several ill-advised, premature,uanecessa.ry, and ridiculous attempts to waste the resources of the burgesses, as witness the further extension of the tech- nical schools, and the proposed extension of the water- works at Daresbury. Only last night they stopped another of these schemes by killing the scheme for making an unnecessary new street between Cairo street and Bridge-street at an estimated cost of £15,000 0r.E.20,000 Referring to the respoctive candidates in Howley Ward the Chairman spoke in warm terms of Mr. Horrocks' qualifications for the position of Councillor, and in referring to his opponant he said Mr. Jacob Sankey was only a non-trader to catch votes. The hand would be the handof Esau but the voice would be Jacob's. (Loud laughter.) Adam came many generations before Jacob and he expected Adam would be well to the front and Jacob a very long way behind on Tuesday next( Renewed laughter.) Nothing would induce them, the Chairman continued,to play into the hands of either political party. The good government of a town ought not to be made a political issue. (Hear, hear.) If the leaders of the parties would bring out men who were really sound on the Non-trading question and would pledge themselves in writing to the satisfaction of the Non- trading Committee, they (the Non-traders) would not attempt to alter the present relative strength of the political parties in the Town Council. But they were not going to put into the Council men upon whom they could not absolutely safely rely—men like Mr. Sankey who told them he was with them but who would be bound fast to the party call, or men like Councillor Rogers who wrote to him (Councillor Dodds) saying he was entirely with them but Iwho had gone over body and soul to their oppon- ents. He (the Chairman)had never given a party vote in the Council and never would. In appealing for the votes ' °tithe burgesses of Howley Ward on Tuesday, the chair- man observed that if the ratepayers did not support the non-traders he should have to consider whether he would not retire into private life. The non- traders had done a great deal because they had the voice of the people behind them. If they were not to be supported now and right on for two or three more years the ratepayers would soon see where they (the Non-traders) would be in the Council. The members of the Council would sit upon them very thoroughly. The Non-traders were fighting the, rate- payers' battle and they relied upon the burgesses to support them as they did last November and return Mr. Horrocks by a crushing majority. He believed they would do so. (Applause.) Mr. A. HIMME /proposed a resolution pledging the meeting to support the candidature of Mr. Horrocks. In doing so, he asked the meeting not to forget the Arley-road matter or the slack contract. In asking Councillor Pemberton to second the resolu- tion, the CHAIRMAN alluded to Mr. Pemberton as" the ablest man in the Council to-day." (Applause). Councillor PEMBERTON went into detail with respect to the proceedings of the Water Committee with refer- ence to contracts. At present he said he was only one in ten on that committee, and he pleaded for re-iinforce- fifents of non-traders to be sent to the Council. Mr. ADAM Honnocics, who had a hearty reception. recounted an interview he had with . Mr. Dens, who refused to allow him to descend one of the wells at Winwick at his (Mr. Horrocks's) own risk. He had never been satisfied with the explana- tion given for closing one of the wells at Winwick. If returned to the Council he should support Mr. Dodds. No bribeano intimidation, oranything would frighten him from doing so. It was for the electors to rise to the occasion and send men to the Council to vote and support the Non traders. (Applause.) Mr. S. KENYON also spoke. . . -</p>
        <p>Mr. NOKBURY WILLTims said he thought he surprised a committee of the House of Lords and Commons on Thursday,by telling them that in his judgment there had never been in the United Kingdom a series of meetings so largely attended, so full of enthusiasm;and aiming at so good an object as those which had been held in War- rington in connection with the non-trading movement. ' Mr. Williams alluded to Mr. Horrocks's long services in the agitation and said that twelve months before the series of meetings commenced, Mr. Horrocks came to his (the speaker's) house in Manchester and asked him to come and assist them in Warrington. He was one of the old originals, not one of the new-fangled men who were being put before the electors, who had been converted on their death-bed, so to speak; and who had only become Non-traders five minutes before they were candidates. It was against these nn that he wanti3d to warn them. They were going to have thern like if plague of flies when November came. (Laughter) At the inquiry in London he said that Warrington was a wimple of what a town might become, where a man's actions might be strictly legal, but utterly immoral, and where the effect of them was utterly demoralizing. So far as he could judge, especially among the six or seven lords he had to confront, the feel- ing appeared to be one of surprise, not exactly at the position of things in Warrington, but because the people were at last risingagainst the condition of things which, in his (the speaker's) oninion,they ought to have risen against long ago. in alluding to the Water Corn- mittee!s slack contract the sneaker said it ought never to be possible kr such a deviation from a contract to take place without the knowledge of the i Chairman of the committee. At the London inquiry, one of the lords asked him if the inhabitants of Warrington had not the remedy to the trading in their own hands. He replied "Yes; they have the remedy in their own hands and they have be- gun to work it out already. (Applause.) We have made a start last November and before two years are over I have not the slightest doubt that a great change will be seen, even in this borough of Warrington." He told the committee that there was one man in War- rington,without giving any name,who owned along with his family 57 per cent of the shares of one of the great companies which did thousands of pounds worth of Cor- poration work even .!vinle that particular man was hold- ing the highest office in the borough. (Applause.) One of the noble lords asked if that were not perfectly legal and he Mr. (Williams) replied " Yes ; but it is fearfully demoralizing." Such trad- ing ought not to be lawful and they must work in Warrington and elsewhere until it was no longer legal for such a state of things as he had described to exist. In conclusion, Mr. Williams said they were going to make a big fight in November, but he hoped that all present would do their best to bring about a victory in. Howley Ward. (Applause.) The resolution pledging the meeting to do all in its power to secure the return of Mr. Horrocks was carried with applause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S WARD.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S WARD.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Thornton, J.P., has been selected by the Non-traders' Association to contest St. John's Ward at the bye-election caused by the death of Councillor Baird. We understand that Mr. John Maginn, and Mr. W. J. Shaw both intend to contest the Ward as -111 dependents.</p>
      </div>
