        <p>i At the outset it was discovered that Turner of St. Helens .was unable to play pal for one of the selected teams, and Wood, a promising Warrington player,was called upon to fill the vacancy. He did so in a manner which called forth warm praise from the spectators, , and it is within the range of probability that Wood will develop into a player of the first order. The trial game also gave Warringtonians an opportunity of again seeing A. G. Robertson, who at one time did such excellent service for the Warrington polo team but who is now associated with the Osborne team. The only other local player was A. Ashton, of the Warrington Polo team.</p>
        <p>Of the two matches played, the first was decidedly the better one. The team under the captaincy of Sharrocks had the best of the game in the first hall, goals being notched by Sharrocks, Forsyth, and Gallagher. Robertson defended well in goal, and near the interval Wilkinsin (Hyde) scored for Robertson's side. Turning round, Robertson's side did nearly all the pressing, and Wilkinson scored three goals. Gallaher added another goal for Sharrock's side, and the result was four goals each. The next tears were captained by Coe and Lister, of the Osborne Club. Play was even throughout. Gray scored for Lister's side. Turning round, play was again even, no further goals being scored.</p>
        <p>. . lessuess, Neuralgia, Low Spirits and all kinds of Weakness. It has often proved very beneficial to persons suffering from great Weakness • either after an illness, long @onfinement to ill-ventilated rooms or any other cause. It strikes at the source of the Disease, removing the cause of the illness, and strengthens those parts as the system which have been weakeued by it, and therefore most liable to colds and other ailments. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters being purely vegetable, it isa Natural Tonict and suitedto all ages at all seasons of the year. It is everywhere acknowledged to be a powerful aid in restoring the body to a healthy state when deranged by disease. If you suspect that your health is beginning to fail, brace up your nerves, and fortify your constitution by taking now a course of this excellent Tonic, whfch is sold verywhere in bottles at 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each, but would any difficulty be experienced in procuring it, the proprietors will forward same for the above prices. carriage free. Avoid Imitations. When purchasing,see the name, "Gwily-m Evans," on Label,Stamp,and Bottle Sole Proprietors: Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Com- pany, Limited, Llauelly, South Wales.</p>
        <p>party a... church party in Le House of Commons, Mr. Cameron said the High church party seemed to him not to be a true Protestant ele- ment in the country at all. They objected to what were called Erastian principles. They wanted to be independent and yet possess all the emoluments and prestige which belonged to a State Church. Then there was the Low church party. Mr. Taylor, he contended, was an honest, out- spoken, straightforward man,who believed in Protestant- ism and in reform, and it was very interesting to him and other members of Parliament to watch the action of the two parties he hadinamed. It was unworthy of the Conservative party to say that they were united, they should not boast of union with all these divisions in their camp. There were also divisions with regard to the Army. There were those who wanted a big Army and those who wanted a small one. There was also an economical party in the House as it was felt they had gone too far in • the spending of the people's money daring the past few years. Our national debt had been increased and money borrowed and millions had been spent at a rate never previously known inthehistory of a party during the past century. At the head of the Conservatives in this respect was Sir Michael Hicks-Beach, a man whom he (the speaker) honoured. He was a very able man and a patriot and, perhaps, the i best financier n the country, but he felt that the nation must not go deeper in debt. He had given the people a I distinct warning that it was important they should pull up, and he (Mr. Cameron) had no doubt it was because he was so outspoken that he had left the Government. But there was still another division, namely, that upon Free Trade and Protection. That division went deeper into their midst, and he hoped it would split the Con- servative party up. Sir Michael Hicks-Beach was the leader on one side supported by the Duke of Devonshire young Churchill, and some of the ablest thinkers. Gosobem was the giant of them all, and he congratu- lated him on his magnificent speech. Here, therefore, they had the elements of division. He pointed out these things to show that the Government could not last long. A house divided against itself, divided upon such great principles—national economy, the finances of the nation, the fiscal system upon which this country had lived for the last 50 year., and yet it had to be brought up again and re-die cussed—could not go on long. (Hear, hear.) In an interesting manner Mr. Cameron referred to the results of the recent bye-elections which he considered were very significant, and might be regarded as protests against the Government. Allusion was also made to Mr. Morley's reception at Newcastle, and it was a remarkable thing that the very city which turned him out was nose ready to receive him with open arms. The Government had become alarmed at the feeling in the country against them, and Mr. Chamberlain had come to their rescue. Mr. Chamberlain no doubt thought it should be counteracted, and that the people must have something new to talk about. They must be raised from the discussion of the doings of the Government; the people must be induced to overlook our national extravagance and also get out of the educational debate which was dividing all Bngland. They must not think about the doles to parsons and farmers and the £12,000,000 for the Irish landowners to make them willing to sell their land. Mr. Chamberlain saw the condition the party was sinking into, and it was his (Mr. Cameron's) opinionthat the fiscal policy had been so abruptly introduced to distract attention from the mis- deeds of the Government. He (the speaker), had sat beside John Bright and talked with him in the House of Commons; and there was gladness in his soul when he and Cobden had won for the people of England what was, perhaps the greatest victory that had ever been won from a fiscal point of view. They started with new methods for conducting their industries. How did it come about that they got Free Trade ?—because the people of England before 1846. the large masses of the working classes, were in workhouses, most of them I were discontented, riots took place and the troops were brought down to trample them down at a place called Peterloo and at Bristol. And why did that state of things exist ?—because there was no work, no wages, no food. Englishmen were on the verge of starvation, the workhouses were overflowing when Carlyle wrote his terrible pamphlets on the condition of England. Sir Robert Peal became converted to the principles of P'ree Trade and Gladstone was his lieutenant. Raw inaterials,cotton, timber, and everything else we needed when it cam* into the country was taxed ; a heavy tariff was put upon it, and yet, in spite of that, English- men managed to create manufactures, and make a con- siderable number of inventions. Yet England could never produce much on account of the tariff which was paid on the material. Gladstone saw that the first thing to do was to abolish the tariffs to give industry a fair play, and the manufacturers a chance, so that when goods were produced they could be sold to the world. Let them have a free course from our ports and pay no tax. Within five years export and import tariffs to the number of 1,700 were abolished, and that was the beginning of Free Trade. (Applause). Was it noti necessary? Was it a bad systemlhat was impoverishing the country? It wagtail nonsense to talk in that strain. The result of Free Trade had been that the trade of the country had grown by leaps and bounds. Mills were built all over the country,mines were sunk, and ironworks opened, and the population had doubled itself in the county of Durham since he entered it becalm there was work for the men to do and wages to receive. The people also gotchee.p food. Would they like to see a tax put on corn and theibread as dear as it was in the time of their fathers and grandfathers? Would they like to see raw materials such as timber taxed? The argument now was that the foreigner did not give us the advantages we gavehim. That was true and they regretted it, but in spite of that greatjaisadvantage, during the last 50 years we had grown in wealth, our population had doubled, and our industries had increased more than four-fold. He thought, therefore, that the system could not be condemned as bad for this country. Ha granted there were some industries that had suffered, but if they were to benefit by the reciprocal arrangement, for one industry that benefitted the great majority of the people of the country would have to suffer. (Hear, hear.) Germany was a protectionist country and she was improving fast. The people were the best educated in Europe, but yet the workingmen there worked longer hours than the workingmen in England and received less wages. Mr. Cameron alluded to the German military system, and said the taxation which was required to maintain it was so hard and oppressive thatlthe people were becoming socialists by way of pro- I test against the method of Government. In regard to the hours and wages of the workingmen, the conditions were the same in Fra,noe and Italy. America was easily explained. They did not tax corn there because they sold corn, and had more than they could use. Conse- quently food was cheap in America, but they had Pro- tection in everything else. They received higher wages than Englishmen,but they had to pay more for house rant, furniture, and everything else. Higher wages were of little use to a manif he had immediately to :Tana them. , In view of how England had developed and made Bach great progress under Free Trade he should be very slow to dream of altering the system to which he had alluded If we commenced a system of retaliation it would bring about a system of social war between this and other countries, and in the arrangement of the tariffs so much , selfishness and friction would be provoked as to bring us back again intodanger of war. "I will retaliate. If youlpunish me I will punish you,"and it would,therefore,be an immoral system. If the old methods were revived we should simply be undoing the best that humanity, had reached to in the way of trading and as a cause of real national prosperity. In conclusion, Mr. Cameron said he wished to speak very briefly upon the education question. The Government wanted them to forget the question but he hoped the Liberals were determined not to do so. They must not be wasting their whole strength and argument on mere tariffs,but they should bring up every act of the Government during the past eight years and debate them as if this newscheme had Dot been launched by Mr. Chamberlain at all. The education system had to be revised and put upon a national foundation. Unless this were done there would be no peace between Church and Dissent and the Church would find that the Education Act was the worst measure she had demanded and re- ceived from a too flexible and a too pliable man. Mr. Balfour was a man of great abilities, but unfortunately he had lent himself to be an instrument in the hands of the clerical party to throw back education. The clerical party were jealous of the democratic feeling; they believed in nominations rathw than elections. The democratic feeling was not native to the Conservative mind. The Goverment sympathised with the clergy and although the ratepayers had to pay the rates they had not the right to oontrol education. The isms and dogmas of the clergy, which were only</p>
        <p>After th 2 trial games the Executive selected the following to represent the North against the South, at Bradford :—Robertson (Osborne), goal; Coe (Osborne) and Wildgoose (Hyde), backs; Shaw (Stockton), Ormondroyd '(Durham), Gilffilan (Burnley) and Swift (Wigan), forwards.</p>
        <p>the opinion of a section of the community, were to be paid for by the public whether the public believed in them or not. (" Shame.") If they asked him to pay for what he did not believe in and did not want they were committing a wrong, and Dissenters had to suffer that wrong through the Educa- tion Act. He trusted the Liberals would soon be back to Parliament, and that they would put this question upon a more just foundation—upon a foundation that , would not wrong the conscience of anyparty but would ' see that the first thing to do was not to waste time in ecclesiastical wranglings, but, if they wished to com- pete with the foreigner they musthave scientific know- ledge and braia power awakened in their working classes. They could not do it if half ofj the schools were under the control and direction of the clerical party The clergyman might come in and take a share of the work, but he (the speaker) objected to the clergyman being the master, ordering what was to be done, and lugging in a catechism as if it made men Christians—a thimblefull of knowledge. While they had the whole 'ocean to get their supplies from they were compelledto I stop and drink a little thimblefull from a catechism. ITeachers in church schools had to subscribe to a test as if a man was never to grow, never to understand more • than was written in the creed,and never to look wider into the universe! Limit a man's thinking and growth and they weakened his teaching power,' because they , weakened his character, and he contended that it was a cruel thing to put educated men under a test which I had been abolished in universities and which no man who respected his liberty of thought would submit to. The mind was expanding in all directions and that was Inever dreamt of when that little creed was drawn up. It might be true or it might be false, but it could not cover all; men were bound to think, and he objected to the teachers of the young being cribbed, cabined and confined. He hoped, therefore, that when the Liberals came back to power they would give them the same freedom for the schools as was given in the universities. Why not have it in the small universities as in the big? (Applause.) There was plenty for. the Liberals to do. Therewas the question of old age pensions—they were sure to get them because they were going to get higher wages. (Laughter) But where was the higher wage to come from? The higher wage was constantly spoken of, but no one said where it had to come from. They were told that they should be willing to pay more for their bread for the sake of the Empire and the Colonies. £147,000,000 of trade was withforeign countries, and we would have to sacrifice for the possible value of £10,000,000 that large sum. Three-quarters of our trade was with foreign countries, and a quarter with the Colonies. Why should we saorifice the bird in the hand for one in the bush? Figures could be obtained from the Board of Trade without holding an inquiry. It was all very well to promise high wages. Let them see the wages first, and then they would tell the Protectionists whether they were with them ornot. (Applause.) Mr. C. E. Parker said he had been called upon unex- pectedly to propose a vote of thanks to Mr. Cameron for his excellent address. Whenever Mr. Cameron spoke he lifted everything he touched on to a high platform. He dealt in all his speeches not. in a partizan spirit but in a great imperial manner, and he trusted the address to which they had just listened would be to them a great stimulus and the beginning /of a work and an effort in connection with Liberalism that would know no abatement. (Hear, hear.) They were in for a big fight. Mr. Chamberlain had said so, and what, he had said he had said. (Laughter.) There was no doubt whatever they thought of his motives, that he had the intellectual force, the power and the ear of a very large proportion of the community. In many ways he deserved the authority he had attained, but with regard to the fiscal proposal he thought it was rotten and bad from, stem to stern. He would call their attention to a correspondence that was being conducted in the Warrington 'Examiner." He did so,not because he happened to be one of the contri- butors butbecause he thought they would have an oppor- tunity of considering the arguments on both sides and possibly it would help them to come to a conclusion. It was very pleasing for them to hear that night one who was through the fight with such men as Cobden and Bright. The great principles which Cobden' Bright, Peel and Gladstone enunciated and converted this country to, and which made it so successful, coinmercially, were as true now as when they were enunciated, and not only as true, but just as applicable. The con- , troversy would have to be carried on to a point somewhat i further n many direotions than they had heard that night. Nine men out of ten he had spoken to about the matter did not seem to approve of the policy of Mr. Chamberlain in respect of a tax on corn or raw goods, , but a great many thought there ought to be a tax put. on manufactured goods that came into this country and were sold under the price they were • manufactured4or. In his opinion—and he had given the subject consfder- able study for a good many years—it was one of the great advantages that sprang out of free trade that we should get goods cheaper than they were produced It was very possible that it might act a little injuri- ously to certain sections of the community but they had to view this question not as it applied to special in- dividuals but in connection with the whole national well-being. (Hear, hear.) He had much pleasure in ' proposing that they express to Mr. Cameron their very hearty thanks. (Applause.) The vote was unanimously carried. Mr. Cameron, in acknowledging the compliment, said Mr. Chamberlain wanted them to debate the fiscal pro- posals and nothing else, so that by a new enthitsiasm he could cover over the multitude of the Government's sins. He for one meant to dwell on the Government's past and then he was perfectly willing to inquire into the fiscal policy of the country, but he was not going to. be drawn off the scent by a olever performer. (Hear; hear.) He liked a man who grew with his opinions. He did not like a man who jumped from one set of principles right to the opposite set of principles and argue for both as sweetly as if they were gospel or part of the Ten Commandments. We-had other things to settle—very important things—andwe must not be distracted by one great question. If Mr. Chamberlain forced them to settle it,he (Mr. Cameron) waaafraid it would settle Mr. Chamberlain as well as others. (Hear, hear, and laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT FROM BEWSEY FORGE.</p>
        <p>Charles Naylor, joiner, 21,-ild-o-n:sWeet; ;7Z -charged with stealing, between 'June 30th and July Ist, lcwt. 3qrs. 41b. of bronze steps of the value of'.£B 6s. Bd., the property of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles, Bewsey ; and Robert Martin, general dealer, Long-lane, Orford, was charged with receiving ithe same, knowing them to have been stolen.—Superintendent Monaghan stated that the forge premises, where the brass was taken from, were left apparently secure on June 30th, and the following morning were found to have been broken into and the brass was missing. Detective Forster, P.C. Lewis and himself made inquiries and seerohed the house of Martin at Orford. ,They found the brass under some cl4ders and slack in the yard. They then went to Naylor's house, and Martin identified him as the man who had brought the brass to his premises. The Superintendent asked for a remand until Saturday.—Martin applied for bail, but the Superintendent strongly opposed it, remarking that there had also been another robbery of brass.—The magistrates granted the remand, but refused to allow any bail.</p>
        <p>AN ECHO OF WALKING DAY.-NOTORIOUS CHA.RACTIIR</p>
        <p>SMARTLY PUNISHED-. There was an echo of Walking Day at the Police Court yesterday, when James 'Smith. a stranger, was charged on remand with frequenting Bewsey-street at 8.30 p.m. on the previous Friday with intent to commit a felony. —Superintendent Monaghan said that at the time in question a crowd of people was standing round an organ grinder and his monkey. Prisoner was seen to act in a suspicious manner, and he put his hand in a lady's dress pocket. The lady accosted the prisoner, and he returned to her a shilling which he had abstracted from her pocket. He then attempted to run away but was arrested by P.C. Fish. Prisoner was brought before the Court on Saturday and was remanded in order that inquiries might be made. It had been ascertained that prisoner had been before various courts 40 times and undergone penal servitude for picking pockets. He was a very notorious character.—Prisoner who stated that he was under the influence of drink at the time in question, was sentenced to three months' imprisonment with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Minor Cases. Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Minor Cases. Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (presiding), Aldermen Francomb and Hutchinson, and Messrs. H. Roberts, T. Birch&amp;ll, H. Thornton, B. J. Guest, J. Geddes, and Charles White.</p>
        <p>BEGGDIG.—For being an idle and disorderly person and begging in Bridge-street, Mark O'Brien, who made his twentieth appearance, was sent to gaol for a month. INGRArrrtiDE.—Mary Ann Clarke, aged 18,was charged with stealing a skirt, a blouse, a pair of boots and other articles of wearing apparel, between July 4th and sth, the property of Martha Gaiter, 58, Tanners- lane—She pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that on Walking Day night, the prosecutrix found the prisoner in the neighbourhood of her house in a desti- tute and foot-sore condition. Moved by compassion, she took her home and fed her. On Saturday the prisoner, who had access to the bedrooms, dressed her- self in the stolen articles and made off. The pollee ,were informed, and Detective Meacock telephoned to the police stations in the neighbourhood, with ehe result that prisoner was arrested at Prescot.—She was sent to gaol for a month.</p>
        <p>- FURIOUS RlDlNG.—William Burns, 48, Dallam-lane, greengrocer, was tined in the costs (lie.) for furiously riding a horse in Orford-lane.—PC. Clough proved the oase.—Trara Conductor Cook stated that defendant was driving at about ten or twelve miles a hour and a num- ber of school children were endangered. DlSORDER.—Elizabeth Peplow, 45, Tanners-lane, was fined Bs. 6d. for making loud and unseemly noises in Tanners-lane on June 28th.</p>
        <p>GAMBLING.—The falowing were lined ss. eack for playing virds at Howley :—Peter Nevins, 14, Platt street; James O'Brien, 16, Dial-court; Henry Chad- wick, 5, Dial-court; William Collier, 17, Bennett- street. TUESDAY—Before Alderman Sutton_ (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>Hutchinson, Dr. Fox, and Mr. J. Young. TH RE A TEN ING HIS FATH E R.—Peter Taylor, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Water-street and with using threats towards his father, Peter Taylor, son., in Water-street, on Monday. He pleaded guilty to the first charge, but denied all knowledge of the second.—Police-constable Clarkesaid that on Monday night defendant's father came and made serious com- plaints to him about his son, in consequence of which witness went into Water-street, and saw the defendant in the street in a very drunken condition. Every en- deavour was made to persuade him to be quiet, but they were at length obliged to lock him up. The father gave evidence, but said that he was not really afraid of his son's threats, as they' were made when he was in drink. He had been drinking all through the present year.—A line of 10s.and costs was inflicted.</p>
        <p>FnmaY.--Before Mr._H. Roberts (in the chair), Alder-</p>
        <p>man Smethurst, Messrs. Hepherd and Thornton. DISGRACEFUL CONDUCT ON A TRAM CAL—John Concannon, Wash-lane, and Alice Concannon, his wife, were summoned for making use ef obscene language on a tram car in Knutsford-road on June 27th.—Mr. Stanley Wilson; Deputy Town Clerk, said defendants were passengers on the 10 p.m. car from Rylands-street to Latchford. The male defendant refused to pay his fare when asked for it by the conductor, and made use of filthy and abominable language. The conductor insisted upon the man paying and the woman commenced to abuse him. It was some time before the conductor obtained the fare and at the terminus the woman made use of abominable language ti the driver. The defendants' conduct was simply outrageous. The woman only came out of prison on the date in question, The manwas a foreman in the employ of the Corpora- tion, and Mr. Parfitt, the superintendent of the depart- ment, gave him a good character—The male defendant alleged that the conductor swore first. He (defendant) was under the influence of drink but he did not refuse to pay the fare.—The magistrates fined each defendant in the costs (88.6 d. AsS4ULT CAsEs.—Catherine McNicoll, 10 Friars' Green, was summoned for assaulting Aim Davies, 17, Friars' Gree.,, on July3rd. There was a cross-summons I against Day. ‘s for assault.—After hearing the evidence</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>No More Backache</head>
        <p>No More Backache</p>
        <p>The millennium here Fels - Naptha has brought it, so far as selling and using it go. Men like it as well as women: the money- back soap.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha 39 Wilson street London E C</p>
      </div>
