      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Foot Racing.</head>
        <p>Foot Racing.</p>
        <p>Several local successes at the sports on Saturday are to be noted. H. Watkins, of Latchford, secured the second prize in the 220 yards handicap at Wigan Police meeting. Starting from the 11 yards mark, he was only beaten by inches by C. Blather, of Liverpool, whom he conceded seven yards start. The distance was covered in 23 1-sth sec. Police Constable Fred Whittaker was fourth in the 100 yards race for the Poole and Waring Challenge Shield at Liverpool,while A. Ford, of Warrington, was second in the 600 yards obstacle race at the same meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Pedestrianism.</head>
        <p>Pedestrianism.</p>
        <p>J. Rimmer, of Cadishead lioss, figured prominently in the great walk—Lancashire v. the Rest of Eng- land—on Saturday. He covered the distance from Hanchester to Blackpool in 9hr. 22min. 24sec. was the thirteenth man to finish and the sixth for his county. Rimmer's performance was considered very satisfactory by the inhabitants of Cadishead. Pre- vious to the "walking craze," Rimmer's prowess in this direction was not suspected. His performance in the walk to Knutsford, when he was fifth man showed his good quality. With more systematic training, Rixamer would probably have done better in the walk on Saturday. On the Friday he was busy loading hay in the fields until a late hour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>TERRIBLE RESULT OF CONSTIPATION An Astounding Bile Bean Cure.</head>
        <p>TERRIBLE RESULT OF CONSTIPATION An Astounding Bile Bean Cure.</p>
        <p>Every sufferer from constipation should read the following facts, which have been copied from a legal document, duly attested before Mr. J. Seymour Price, of Birmingham, a Commissioner for Oaths. They teach two lessons very forcibly—first that constipation if neglected leads to very serious consequences ; second, that in Charles Forde's bile beans will be found a safe, effective, and purely vegetable remedy for this serious ailment. The statement below has been made by Miss Ettie Palmer, of Back 253, Great Colmore-street, Birmingham, who swears as follows : "In my ease, acute constipation led to stoppage of the bowels. I could not take nourishment, and conse- quently grew too feeble to work. Having taken to my_ bed I got weaker and weaker. It was not thought I should live. Indeed, the clergyman of the parish came to pray at my bedside, so near death was I. The doctor called in a physician, and it was said my only hope of life lay in an operation, which might or might not be successful. I was taken in the Queen's Hospital, and peepared for operation, but at the lastl moment my parents stopped all the proceedings and had me taken back home. This was in consequence of what they had heardabout the power of bile beans, and I was started upon a course immediately. From the first I improved, and in a few days my bowels were acting normally. I gradually got stronger, and in a short time was back at work. Since then I have worked for twelve months, and have never had a trace of my old complaint. I was so weak at the time I was taken to hospitalfor the operation that I believe I should never have survived it." Such was Miss Palmer's experience, and her case forms one of the most sensational proofs of the power of Chas. Forde's bile beans. They are without doubt the finest medicine known for constipation, liver dis- orders, indigestion, headache, all female ailments, anmmia, skin eruptions, etc. They are obtainable at is. lid. or 2s. 9d. per box from all medicine vendors. an•••••••••=••••••••• • BETTING CASES AT NEWTON. Penketh Man Fined.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE. THE REPRESENTATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Parochial Committee was held oh Thursday evening. Mr. G. Pilling presiding. There were also present :—Messrs. Clarke, Bouchiei, Whitley, Fairhurst, G. F. Ashton (clerk), Farrington (inspector of nuisances), and Swinton (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>NEW STREETS-.</head>
        <p>NEW STREETS-.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor submitted plans for the making and paving a Dundonald-avenue and Kimberley-drive, and the committee approved them.</p>
        <p>THE NIGHTSOIL CONTRACT. The Clerk reported that Mr. Lunt had sent in an account for £5O ss. for carrying out this contract during the past three months. The Inspector said he had no fault to find with tho way in which the work was now being done. Since the last meeting five complaints were received, but some were very trivial, and all were attended to after a little pressure. Satisfactory arrangements had been made for the introduction of the pail system in the centre block of property in Bostock-street. Mr. Whitley said he had received complaints from certain quarters that the ashpits were nit properly emptied and that the tubs and pails were in a very bad condition.</p>
        <p>The Inspector said Mr. Whitley was under a wrong impression. Only one ashpit in the property he had mentioned was overlooked, and that had now been attended to. Some people would always be complain- ing. They could not satisfy them. Mr. Clarke said there was not the slightest doubt that the work was being clone very unsatisfactorily, and the committee ought to insist on all the ashtubs and pails being thoroughly cleansed and disinfected. It was evident they could notget Mr. Lunt to do the work,and he asked the inspector if there was any chance of getting Mr. Lunt to give up the contract before the expiration of the full term.</p>
        <p>The Inspector said he had been told that Mr. Lunt would give £lOO to get out of the contract. (Laughter.) Mr. Clarke thou moved that Mr. Lunt be asked if he were willing to give up the contract; and this course was agreed to.</p>
        <p>The-committee agreed to pay Mr. Lunt's account. THE REPRESENTATION QUESTION. MR. WHITLEY PERSI4TENT.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the Rural District Council had considered the application of the committee for anothei representative on that authority. The com- mittee which discussed the matter reported that they did not consider that the points submitted to them were sufficient to entitle Stockton Heath to another representative.</p>
        <p>/'he Chairman saitl two or three districts were in exactly the same wition as Stockton Heath. Mr. Whitley gaTe notice that at the next meeting he would ask the following questions : (1). What, where, when and with whom did the Sub-committee make in- quiries into the matter? : (2). Did the Sub-committee decide that it would be best for the interestsofiStookton Heath to have only one district Councillor ? ; (3). Did the Chairman of the Sub-committee influence and recom- mend the Sub-committee not to make any change in the representation on the District Council for Stockton Heath ? ; (4). Was the Chairman of the Sub-committee aware when he gave in the report of the Sub-committee to the District Council that many of the District Councillors there wereiprepared to vote for an additional Councillor for Stockton Heath, and that they were utterly sur - prised at the decision of the sub-committee? He would also move the following resolution:—"That the District Council be asked to reconsider their decision respecting an additional district councillor for Stockton Heath, and that a sub-committee be formed by the District Council together with our own District Councillor (Mr. Davenport). Mr. J. Mounfield, and the overseers of Stockton Heath to investigate fully the question of ad- ditional representation on the District Counoil and to report at a future meeting of the District Council." This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>TRIP DAY.</head>
        <p>TRIP DAY.</p>
        <p>WORKS EXCURSIONS FROM WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. A. Naylor's Employees at Llandudno.</p>
        <p>Saturday was an ideal day for the tripper. Had Mark Tapley been pursuing the congenial pastime of holiday-making, he would have found precious little credit in being jolly under such circumstances. The ordinary mortal is not as a rule very richly endowed with a temperament such as Mark Tapley was said to possess, and jollity found expression in the face of almost every day " tripper who chanced to cross one's path. There were many sombre pre- dictions on Walking Day in regard to the climatic conditions which might he expected on the morrow, but they had no fulfilment in fact. Under the cheerful influence of radiant sunshine and balmy breezes, several thou- sand Warrington excursionists made merry at popular resorts, and the works trips were an unqualified success. Mr. R. A.. Naylor's employees were fortunate in selecting Llandudno for their destination. This lovely spot was seen at its very best, and the visitor would indeed be hard to please who was not thoroughly satisfied with the outing. The day-tripper is not afraid of early rising. He can be up with the lark when there is a long day of pleasure in prospect, but those who accompanied Naylor's trip were not called upon to make any;very exceptional effort. Seven o'clock was the hour fixed for departure from Bank Quay , Station and by that time the platform was thronged with several hundreds of happy mortaLs.The train soon; steamed in and there was plonty of friendly competi- tion to secure good seats. In a remarkably short space of time the compartments were filled and the passen- gers were being borne on their way to Wales. The route to Llandudno presents many features of interest. On passing through the quaint old city of Chester one caught a glimpse of the Roodee race course, whilst a littic farther on the train crossed a bridge which spans the placid waters of the Dee. For a con- siderable distance the railway runs alongside the river which gradually broadens as it nears the sea. Rhyl, with its fine marine lake, next attracted attention, and from this resort to Llandudno there is plenty of charming scenery. Bathed in the glorious sunshine the hills, some rugged and others richly wooded, presented a beautiful sight. Farmers were busily at work in the fields, and often the trippers enjoyed the scent of new-mown hay. Conway, with its grand old castle and wooded heights, madea pretty picture, and with so many interesting scenes the railway journey was not at all monotonous. Llandudno was reached a few minutes before ten o'clock, and the holiday-makers dispersed to spend the day just as inclination dictated. There was no set programme. All were, so to speak, free lances. It would require an eloquent pen to adequately describe the beauties of Llandudno. It is delightfully picturesque and on Saturday its charms could be appreciated to the full. The azure sky,relieved here and there with white cloudlets, was mirrored in the clear waters of the bay, which was almost as calm as the proverbial mill pond. Great Orme's Head towered above majestic,ally,and in the back-ground the magnificent Welsh hills could be perceived. Sedateness and refinement are carried to a high pitch at Llandudno. Even our canine friends are expected to be on their best behaviour. Prominently displayed on the promenade were notices calling atten- tion to the fact that numerous complaints had been re- ceived of the annoyance caused by the bark- ing of dogs. Visitors were, therefore, "earn- estly requested" not to encourage the dogs by throwing stones into the sea. Poor dogs! They seemed to wear quite a subdued expression. The Warring- tonians.were not by any means the only day-excursioniets. One large contingent had journeyed from Derby, and during the morning several hundreds arrived by boat from Liverpool. They appeared to be in a partioularly. happy frame of mind,and some marched along the pier singing energetically to the strains of a concertina. Many and varied were the ways in which the day was spent, but nearly all the Warringtonians wended their way to the Happy Valley which certainly justifies the name, for amid delightful surroundings there is a scene of gaiety and animation quite cheering to the worker in an industrial town. In the afternoon the performance of the nigger troupe afforded constant pleasure and amusement. Without an ascent of Great Orme's Head a visit to Llandudno cannot be said to be complete. Some of the more venturesome scaled the steep hill which rises so abruptly from the Happy Valley, and had the satisfaction of priding themselves upon a feat requiring perseverance and agility. A very pleasant mode of ascent was by the trams which conveyed the visitors almost to the summit of the Head. The gradient at times is remarkably steep, and the ride was a novel and enjoyable experience. The view from the highest point of Great Orme's Head was extensive and magnificent. Wherever one turned the prospect was equally pleasing. Hard by was the quaint little church of St. Tudno, with its peaceful graveyard overlooking the sea. Beyond, the town of Llandudno with the lovely bay unfolded itself to the gaze of the visi- tor, and there was a grand background of hill scenery. There were other attractions on the promenlde and in the town itself, but it was upon the handiwork of Nature that most of the trippers preferred to feast their eyes. Tired but happy the excursionists assembled at the station at nine o'clocklin the eve ‘ing and dusk was setting in when Llandudno was left behind. The old town was reached at 11.25 after one of the most grati- fyingly successful outings in the annals of the firm. It was a matter of regret to all that neither Mr. Naylor nor Miss Naylor were able to accompany the excursion, but the genial manager, Mr. L. Harahan, was present and did everything in his power for the comfort and enjoy- ment of the employees. To him it was in a large measure due that the affair passed off without the slightest hitch to mar a glorious day. The workmen employed in the tube department at Messrs,: Monks, Hall and Company's Works also went to Llandudno and spent a pleasant time. They travelled by the same train as Mr. Naylor's employ4s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Corporation Workmen._</head>
        <p>Corporation Workmen._</p>
        <p>The workmen of several departments of the Corpora- tion spent an enjoyable time at Blackpool on Saturday. Altogether the men (who were accompanied by their wives and children) numbered 364, 123 being from the gas department, 116 from the highway and scavenging departments, and 107 from the water department. They left Bank Quay Station in a special train at seven o'clock, their destination being reached shortly after nine. Each man not only received a free ticket, but was given 2s. 2d. to spend. The return journey was com- menced at nine o'clock, and Warrington was safely reached shortly before midnight.</p>
        <p>Armitage and Rigby, Limited. On Saturday the employ&amp; had their 36th annual excursion, when the place visited was Blackpool. Fortunately the weathe.• was fine, and the excursion proved to be one of the most enjoyable ever organized in connection with the firm. Not only were many of the principal places of interest visited, but a great attraction was the arrival of the competitors in the great walk from Manchester to Blackpool. The em- ploys, who numbered over 1,300, left the Central Station in two special trains at an early hour in the morning, their destination being reached in good time. All returned safely to Warrington late at night. Mr. Warburton acted as secretary to the Trip Committee.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., Limited,</p>
        <p>Bedstead and Mattress Works.</p>
        <p>The annual excursion of the employs of these works took place last Saturday to Windermere. The train de- parted from Bank Quay Station about 5.30 in the morn- ing, and although the time of departure was somewhat early, still it was pleasing to note that praotically all the employs turned up. Their efforts in this respect deserve the highest praise. The outward journey was both pleasant and interesting, the beautiful scenery en route lending enchantment to the travellers. After the excursionists arrived at their destination about 8.5, they were free to go wherever they chose. The majority of the party after partaking of breakfast wended their way in the direction of Bowness. Afterwards some made a halt at Messrs. Mawson Bros.' Nursery and suffice it is to say, the time occupied there was well speut. Moreover, the visit gave greater stimulus to those of the party who took an active part in gardening. Leaving Bowness with its beautiful surroundings and its houses of quaint and peculiar structure, a trip was made across Lake Windermere to Ambleside. The sail was greatly appreciated by the employees, and they appeared fascinated by the magnificent landscape. Towering above the lake were those beautiful aud undu- lating ranges of mountains, several of which were capped by the proverbial "Scotch mist." At intervals the mist dispensed its moisture in slight showers of rain, thus marring the comfort of the excursionists to some extent during the fore part of the day. Ambleside and its beautiful environments were taken full advantage of by the visitors, the weather at this stage now being magnifi- cent. As the afternoon advanced the excursionists began to retrace their steps homeward, although it was fully evident from the comments generally passed that it was with regret that they had to leave such a blissful resort. The train left Windermere at 6.43 p.m.. and made a fine run to Wigan. The latte place will no doubt recall some unpleasant experiencer to local readers. In the present case the train arrived at Wigan at 8.40,, and finally reached Warrington about 10.5., thus doing the latter twelve miles of the journey in one hour and 25 minutes, being equivalent to 7.08 minutes per mile. This creates another record for the London and North-Western Railway. With the excep- tion of this incident it is admitted that the employ4s spent one of the most pleasant and enjoyable outings they have ever had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co's. Works.</head>
        <p>Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co's. Works.</p>
        <p>The employs in the lion works journeyed to Black- pool and thoroughly enjoyed themselves. The various attractions were well patronised. The employ4s in the bedstead department spent a pleasant day at Winder- mere, and those in the tube department proceeded to Llandudno.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Saturday was a perfect day for cricket, and local enthusiasts enjoyed themselves to the full. Cheadle Hulme were the visitors to the Arpley enclosure, but neither side derived very much satisfaction from the encounter, which ended in a draw. As matters stood at the close,Cheadle Hulme occupied the most advan- tageous position. After clmpiling 179 for the loss of 5 wickets, they declared their innings, and Warring- ton had made 111 for the loss of the same number of wickets when stumps were drawn.</p>
        <p>The great features of the match were E. Rome's and G. E. Radfo'rd's sploadid innings. Each batted in fine style and Rome had knocked up 61, when a par- ticularly smart catch by Hallam brought about his dismissal. Radford was not less succesful and carried out his bat after cnnpiling 53. H. Hoyle was responsible for 31 and G. E. Howarth for 17.</p>
        <p>On the Warrington side B. Silcock and J. W. Bibby exhibited capital form their aggregates being 37 and 33 (not out) respectively. E. A. Connor also rendered valuable service, hi g innings realizing 21. Mr: Bibby hit six boundaries and Mr. Silcock's best strokes included five fours.</p>
        <p>A draw was the result of the second eleven's en- gagement with Cheadle Hulme. The Warringtonians made a good display and scored 208 for the loss of 6 wickets, at which stage the innings was declared. The home tom had put together 136 for 4 wickets when play ceased. E. Pierpoint is deserving of special praise for his excellent batting which produced 55 (not out). W. Ashton also distinguished himself, his total being 46, whilst R. Connor was to the fore with a contribution of 36. T. Wright placed 20 to his credit. For Cheadle Hulme, H. Smith rendered yeoman service the outcome of his innings being the addition of 66 runs to the score. H. Bazley was re- sponsible for 26 (not out).</p>
        <p>As may be judged from the tbtals there was plenty of excitement in the match between Sankey Wire Mills and Moore and Daresbury, the Wire Mills running out victors by the small margin of 5. Singular to state the top score on each side was 13, ad the batsmen who attained the figure were W. Woodcock and S. Taylor.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptists were easily victorious in the match with Crosfield's. They ran up the substantial aggregate of 113 and the efforts of the soapery eleven realized only 56. T. Albinson (28) and Hunt (19) were the chief contributors for Latchford, and A. Dutton (17) and T. Dixon(l3) were the top scorers for the opposing eleven. &amp; v</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath sustained a reverse at the hands of Stretton and Appleton. They were all dismissed for 43 and their opponents had amassed 84 for the loss of seven wickets when stumps were drawn, Cr. Hatton batted in grand style and scored 45 (notlout). Moorfield rendered good service with the ball, taking six wickets for 18 runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>C. Berry of Warrington, carried off the second prize in the half mile face at Wigan County Police Sports on Saturday. He was beaten by a length by E. Hughes of Sale.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday's run in connection with the War- rington C.C. wag to Siddington but as many of the members were touring in Derbyshire, North Wales and the Lake District, there was only a small turn- out. The weather was glorious and an enjoyable time was spent. On Tuesday evening 30 members had a spin to Stretton and the first bowling main was concluded. The winners are :-1, F. Barber; 2, 3. Podmore ; 3, S. H. Antrobus ; 4, H. Whittle. Several games in the second main were played and the con- test will be continued next Tuesday evening at Warburton.</p>
        <p>To-day's fixture is the most popular one of the season. The members of the club will once more be the guests of the es teemed President (Mr. John Naylor) at Beeston Tower. There is sure to be a large muster, and no doubt the President's Day of 1903 will be as successful as any on record.</p>
        <p>Fixtures. Warrington C.C. To-day, Beeston Tower; Tuesday, Warburton.</p>
        <p>Royal C.C. To-day, Altrincham ; Thursday, Hollins Green.</p>
        <p>Llvely-lane C.C. To-day, Tarporley ; Wednesday, Appleton v a Stretton.</p>
        <p>Sankey Bridges Wesleyan C.C. To-day, Holmes Chapel; Monday, Lymm. ,</p>
        <p>Angling. A great deal of angling was indulged in by local rods last week end, the holiday enabling anglers to visit fishing localities far and near. The Bridgewater</p>
        <p>Canal provlded some sport with roach and iiidgeon. 1 At Newton Petty Sessions on Thursday, John Swift, A pike of some 3lbs. 3ozs. was caught by Mr. N, cf Penketh, was charged with betting at Liverpool-road, Sankey, near Warrington, on the 18th and 19th June. Harper at Mickle Trafford. The river Dee in the Mr. Browne (Warrington) prosecuted for the police, neighbourhood of Chester was unproductive to the alaicl.Mr. Steel defended, and pleaded not guilty. coarse fisher; whilst the river Weaver according to Mr. Browne described defendant as "a silly fool" for one angler was "Dead off." Messrs. Harry Taylor and !accepting slips and money in the presence of. a stranger, J. Starkey report satisfactory sport amongst the bream : a plain-clothes constable being at defendant's elbow for in the Bottom Flash at Winsford. A party of local an hour. The offence took place near the Warrington i anglers have mentioned that in certain Cheshire meres i borough boundary, and when the county police ap- which they visited the waters were "broken" and thus proached defendant stepped into the borough. This was the nfirstt prosecution under the new county bylaws. poor sport resulted. Some other locals visited Shrews- I bury, and had sport with small chub, therhgh Mr. W. and cost?. 1 Thomas Mellor, Sankey, escribedas a tout for Swift, Hamblett did get one of 11b. gozs. and a ruffe of some i was also fined £5 and costs. 2 ozs. At Walcot, sport was paor, as well as at Four Matthew Seton was charged with betting in Earle- Crosses. Coarse fish provided - fair sport at street, Earlestown, on ths 9th,l2th, 16th, 18th, and 19th . Uanymynech, Mr. J. Waywell creeling a perch of lib June. Defendant was fined £5 and costs. The same penalty was imposed on Frank Dall and James Lee, des- 30z. and some chub. Several trout have been spoken • oribed as Se t on's agents; also on Walter Waterman, of as coming from North Wales. The river Bollin 'another Earlestown bookmaker, was rather low. Several nice dace were, however, I , Peter Kenny, Owen Morris, John Broster, and Thomas taken, Messrs Hough and Bate getting the two best-' Maley were charged with acting as touts for book- Mr. G. Shannon landed a large eel of 21b. 14ozs. makers, to whom they gove signals on the approach of Amongst the trout fishers, Mr. Henry Green was the the police. Concoalcid officers proved the cases. Defen- d: most successful getting three. Messrs. Barry, Willis, ants were fined 10a., including costs. Hutchinson, Kent, Rooke and Craddock all had nice ' William Unsworth, another Earlestown bookmaker, 'was charged with betting on the 13th, 23rd, and 24th trout. •i J Earl, .</p>
        <p>une in _ -.to-street. He was fined £5 and costs. Arthur Lee, George Dixon Holland (father-in-law to Ithe defendant Seton), of Earlestovrn, and John Burgess, sen., Newton all in the employ of Seton, were each fined £5 and costs; John Burgess, jun., likewise in Seton's employ, being given the benefit of the doubt as regards frequenting at Newton. Mr. Swift defended.</p>
        <p>The contest in aid of the Benevolent Fund of the Association will be fished to-day (Saturday), at Stockton Heath. The draw will be undertaken at London Bridge at 3 o'clock. ' Entries may be made on the bank.</p>
        <p>The Latch:ord A.S. held their monthly meeting on Monday evening last. The first evening contest will be decided on Monday evening next at Grappenhall. The fishing, which will be of lihrs. duration will commence at 6.45 p.m. There will be six prizes. The competitors must provide themselves with bait-cans o: keep-nets in which to keep undersized fish alive, until after being weighed. Mr. William Holland of Northwich has promised a gold medal to the member registering the largest roach this season.</p>
        <p>r The Latchford Excelsior A.S, have chosen tlie fol- lowing team to represent the club in the County Palatine Challenge Cup Competition i which will be fisited. at Poynton on July 18th :—Messrs T. Leigh, H. Aihurst, W. Oakes, J. Robinson, J. Wolfenden, and J. Oakes. Reserves, J. Haines and J. Oxford.</p>
        <p>The Mersey A.S. have picked two teams of their members to represent the club, in the Shield Com-</p>
        <p>15 Buns for id. You can make 15 large, light, delicious, and wholesome buns from id. packet of Eiffel Tower Bun Flour for a total cost of Rd. It is so easy to use that a child can wake delicious buns with certain succes&amp;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Gruesome Discovery at Arpley.</head>
        <p>The Gruesome Discovery at Arpley.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, held an inquest at the Police Station on the remains of a man unknown which were recovered from the Mersey at Arpley. It appeared that on Wednes- day of last week Samuel Appleton and other men were conducting dredging operations when the remains were brought up in one of the buckets of the dredger. They were in a very advanced state of decomposition and there was nothing to lead to identification.—A er diet of "Found dead" was returned.</p>
      </div>
