      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday July 11, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday July 11, 1903.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED NEW STREET</p>
        <p>INTERESTING TOWN COUNCIL DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>The Widening of Bridge-street.</p>
        <p>One of the most important recommendations under discussion at the meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening was.the one brought for- ward by the Street Improvement Committee with refer- ence to the proposed new street from Sankey-street to Rylands-street.</p>
        <p>The subject was introduced by Alderman StrrtoN,who said that since the last Council meeting the Street Improvement Committee had devoted a good deal of time in considering and discussing the question, and they submitted the following resolution, which they thought would be of immense benefit to the town at large and which he now proposed should be adopted:— I "Thal the Corporation agree to the following terms accepted by Mr. R. E. Garnett for the construction of la new street extending from the rear of Mr. Benson's premises to Rylands-street (1) Mr. Garnett will give three plots of land containing in the aggregate 919 sq. yards in exchange for a plot of land containing 920 sq. yards belonging to the Corporation; (2) the Corporation to retain a strip of land about 15 feet in width and containing 247 sq. yards, being part of the site of the passage proposed to be constructed by the Corporation; (3) the Corporation to include the pre- mises at eact end of the proposed new street in the first application for eompulsory powers of purchase, andthat such application be made as soon as possible; and (4) the Corporation to make and pave the street." The Committee also decided "that a piece of land contain- ing 47 sq. yards be conveyed to Messrs. Hodgkinson, Ltd., in exchange for 47 sq. yards required for the con- struction of the new street." Continuing, Alderman Sutton said that according to the scheme which the Council had already approved of, they had to make a 20ft. passage from Rylands-street to Sankey-street, and for the land required from Mr. Garnett they had to pay £750. By the scheme now proposed to enable the Corporation to make a new street 40 feet wide Mr. Garnett offered 919 square yards of land in exchange for 920 square yards. The Corpora- tion would retain 247 square yards for re-sale, which, estimating it at the low value of £2 per square yard, would realize £494. In addition they would not be compelled to interfere with Mr. barter's stables, !which would be a saving of about £5OO on the original pro- posal. In round figures the total saving to the Corpora- tion would be £l,OOO. The committee had discussed the matter overand overagain and had come to tbe con- clusion that a new street would be beneficial to the interests of the town.</p>
        <p>In reply to Councillor Sampson, Alderman SUTTON said the street would cut through the shops now occupied by Mr. Benson and Mr. Chantry whichwould have to be purchased. Alderman BENNETT seconded the motion, and said the matter had not been in any sense rushed or hurried. The committee had given the greatest possible con- sideration to it, and had held two or three special meetings. His first feeling was rather in favour of a new street. Then he heard strong representations made that it was not necessary, and it was suggested to him as an alternative, that it would be better to make Bridge-street 80 feet wide. Any suggestion about an 80 feet street always commended itself to him (Alder- man Bennett), forhe had been advocating 80 feet streets for the past ten years, but, he was sorry to say, up to the last few months he had not been supported. If his advice had been taken Bridge-street would already have been 80 feet wide. The proposal to make the remaining portion o f-Bridge-street 80 feet wide seemed beset with all kinds of difficulties, and much as he would like it he did not see how they could do it symmetrically and to advantage. Failing 80 feet in Bridge-street they needed this proposed relief street, and he supported it mainly on that ground. Heldidlaot think they would need it but for the congested traffic. Even when they widened Bridge-street he thought they would find it inadequate for all the heavy traffic. The bargain was on the whole a very good one, he con- sidered. There was a good deal to be said on both sides, but after considerable and protracted discussion the committee carried t ie recommendation only when they had been led to believe that it was impossible to make Bridge-street 80 feet wide. Councillor LORD asked if they could be supplied with any estimate of thetotal cost of the improvement, and what they were going to pay for the compulsory pur- chase of the property occupied by Mr. Benson and Mr. Chantry and that at the Rylands-street end. Alderman SUTTON considered that a rather "far fetched "question. He did not profess to know what everyone would reoeive for their property. There was no doubt it would cost a large sum but he would not offer to give any estimate, and he didnotthinkanybody else would.</p>
        <p>Councillor TrtiNioN said he had come to face the question with an open mind but he would like fuller information. He did not think Alderman Sutton had given them all the information in his possession. If he had, then he possessed very little to warrant him in bringing forward a scheme of that magnitude. (Hear, hear.) It was certainly desirable, as far as possible, to provide a front street for shop-keepers, and it would be an improvement to carry out the scheme. Some people seemed to think they had too much shopaccommodation in Warrington. He did not hold that view. He thought Warrington would progress, and they would be able to support more shop frontage than they now had. They had less in Warrington:than any other average Lancashire town, but while he agreed with the general principle of the scheme he was not prepared to commit himself on the information Alderman Sutton had vouchsafed to them. Alderman Sutton did not mention one item of £B5O to be paid for property standing on some of the land they were likely to take in, which was on the last agenda, while he also noticed that -the Corporation were to make and pave the street. Those were considerable items, and he thought they were entitled to know what the scheme was likely to cost before proceeding further. They knew nothing of the extension at the lower end of the street. It seemed that the Corporation were to include the premises at eaoh end. Last month the Sankey-street end nets entirely ignored, audit was only now that they learned that the Sankey-street end was to be interfered with. He was under the impression from a conversation he had with the Town Clerk, and from what transpired at the last Council Meeting, thatit was not intended to carry the street right through for some years. He would like to have some ides of the I cost of the property which would have to be acquired, becauSe judging from the amount which was paid for some property at the corner of Rylands-kreet and Bridge-street, the expenditure was going to be very considerable. He did not think it was fair—whatever the merits of the scheme might be—that the Council should be called upon to vote upon such an important matter without fuller information. Surely it was possible to say what the advantages of the scheme would be, and it was only right that the Council should have the benefit of the committee's deliberations on the subject. Councillor PEMBERTON contended that the matter had been rushed. He had not had an opportunity of visiting the place. Alderman SUTTON:' I should think you have. You have known about it for two or three months.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON was of the opinion that one person would benefit by the exchange of land—not the Corporation. There was a strip of land 16 feet wide on which they could build nothing and which was at present 'useless. If the Corporation paid £lO,OOO or £12,000 to buy the ends of the street and £2,000 for paving, the property became valuable. The Corporation were buying the salt and the other people were having the egg. He did not think with the present information before them they should be asked to pass such an extensive scheme. If they were making a new town would any sensible person make a 60ft. street and then 100 ft. away make another 40ft. street? He contended the proposed street was too near Bridge street. They ought to spend the money on Bridge street and finish it. (Hear, hear.) Councillor WILLIA.M ROBERTS strongly opposed the scheme. He professed to know a little bit about the feelings of his fellow tradesmen, and he had not heard a single person who was in favour of the scheme. As far as he could gauge public opinion it was that they ought to finish Bridge-street before they entertained any idea of making the street proposed. What earthly reason was theta for it? If they wanted to relieve Bridge-street they had got Cairo-st?eet. They had only to pull one block of property down and they had a thoroughfare to Arpley. Then they had Bold-street immediately adjoining,so there was no pressing necessity for a new street. The figures which had been submitted were not sufficient to influence the Council in coming to a decision. There were several interests to be con- sidered before they completed the scheme. He was told they would have to purchase a certain amount of land occupied by the distillery, and there was dilapidated property near the theatre which ought to have been con- demned years ago, but which had yet to be purchased. In regard to the land abutting the new street, did not lie principle of betterment apply? By making Use provement the Corporation would increase the value of the land to an enormous extent. He considered one person was going to benefit enormously. There were no details before them to come to a decision, and by bringing this forward they were delaying he carrying out of the Bridge-street improvement. A contract had already been let and buildings could be commenced but the present proposal was preventing the , improv "meat from being carried out. When they wanted anew street they might talk about improving Buttermarket-street after they had finished Bridge- street. There was a crying necessity for immediate attention to Buttermarket-street and the property on , both sides up to Scothnd-road wanted pulling down. Councillor JAMES EVANS said it was very easy to criticize the action of the Street Improvement Com- mittee, gut they had been standing between two alternative schemes for a considerable time. It was all very well to say off-handedly, as Councillor W. Roberts had said, "Get on withl3ridge-street." They Had got to pay the prioe of the passage if they were going to pro- ceed with Bridge-street, and it was going to cost the ratepayers £750 for which they would get absolutely nothing. He did not think Councillor Pemberton was quite right when he said he</p>
        <p>knew nothing about the land and had not seen it. He (Councillor Evans) knew he had seem it. He did not like that sort of thing. It was all very well to say the Corporation land was of considerably more value than the other. The 920 yards belonging to them was a piece of back land. If they constructed the passage it would remain so, but if they made a street it would become front land. With regard to the £B5O mentioned by Councillor Tinnion, the committee had since the last meeting had interviews and considered the matter, with the result that they had made a level exchange. (Hear, hear.) The liability to pay £750 was also taken away if the passage became a part of the street. He was jafraid the strongest criticism cams from Bridge- street. For a considerable time.. the committee had been pinched again and again with regard to making shop accommodation for people who would lose their premises when the thoroughfare was widened. It was painful to contemplate that they should have to turn out a person holding an old business establishment in Bridge-street or anywhere else and the new street would relieve the congestion in Bridge-street. Councillor W. ROBERTS: They would not go there. Councillor JAMES EVANS : How do you know ? Councillor W. ROBERTS: You will see.</p>
        <p>Councillor JAMES EVANS: If you know, say so. Councillor W.ROBERTS : lam certain.</p>
        <p>Councillor JAMES EVANS : Then hold your noise till I have finished. You seem to know a great deal. Councillor W. ROBERTS: You know a mighty lot. CouncillorJAmEs EVANS : I know as much as you— (" Order")—or at any rate I have considered it as much as you. I don't want your interruption. What lam saying is for the best interests of the town. I have no axe to grind. Continuing, Councillor Evans suggested that a special maeting of the Street Improvement Com- mittee should be hell to which the wholeiCoun.cil should be invited, and then all the details could be given them. He was sure there was no disposition on the part of the committee or any official to withhold the slightest in- formation. He was told that the extra assessment upon the increased value of property under the sehe-ie would increase from practically nil to .£l5O a year. He was certain the Council and public could be satisfied as to which was the best alternative scheme if the committee only got the opportunity of satisfying them. Councillor PumnunToN said he would like to give a personal explanation. He had not seen the land and he did not think Councillor Evans should getup repeatedly and say he was making false statements. Councillor JAMBS EVANS : Have you ever made any remarks about this land?</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: That's another point. The MAYOR: I hope the incident will drop now. Councillor JAMES EVANS : I will bow to your decision, Mr. Mayor, but I think I could justify my position with Councillor Pemberton. _</p>
        <p>n Alderman BOLTON said that what the committee desired to do was to improve the town by the erection of better class property which would pay a sufficient rate to defray the cost of the newstreet. They did not want any more Scotland-roads in Warrington. That was the biggest blunder that was ever ma ide n regard to street improvements. They did not want little bits of shops in Bridge-street, but shops that would let for £l5O or £2OO a year. Thequestion of benefiting One man was not a question for the Corporation to consider, if the improvement would benefit the .Corporation. He thought, however, that the committee should have sub- mitted some estimate of the total cost. He thought this scheme could be carried through amicably. There were certain shops required in Warrington, and the nearer they were to the centre of the town the better. No one would care to go to Scotland-road or Pig-hill for a shop. The proposed new street had been in con- templation for he last 30 or 40 years, and it was calculated that if carried through the Corporation would benefit by increased rate able value. He thought the scheme was worthy of consideration. Councillor TiaotioN: Can I move an amendment?</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON thought the Committee would raise no objeotion to a reConsideration of the question. He could assure them that the committee had no desire to force anything upon the Council which the council might consider undesirable. The committee had been very anxious throughout to consider the questionlairly and honestly. He should like to rayon his own account that he wan, to a large extent, simply the mouthpiece of the committee. He had objected from the first to oertain portions of the resolution, and he objected to the payment of the £B5O which Councillor Tinnion had referred to, on the ground that he did not consider it a fair claim. That matter, on his own suggestion, was brought before Mr. Garnett's notice, and he had fore- gone the claim. The simple question was this : Was it desirable or not to have the street, or to be contented with a passage from Rxlands-street to Sankey-street? It had been said by Councillor William Roberts that there was no necessity for a street of this kind. He (Alderman Sutton) thought one of the freateet mistakes the Corporation ever made was when it refused to pur- chase Mr. Benson's and Mr. Chantry's shops some 20 years ago. He then contended that the improvement would be a great relief to Bridge-street. On that occa- sion there were such a large number of owners and tenants in Bridge-street, who were in the Council— (hear, hear)—that although he brought the matter forward on several occasions—because they could have oonstructed thestreet at much less cost than at present —it was impossible for him to carry it or get anyone to take an interest in it. That was because there was such a number of interested people then residing in Bridge street, who were membars of the Coune:i. The only reason the matter had been brought forw -1 so hurriedly was that by delaying it they were prove ing the Bridge street owners from proceeding With tit- videning of the street. They had agreed to sell to Hodgkinsona piece of land which extented into the ..ereet and— Councillor TINNION : He gets a goo,l bargain. Alderman SurroN : He buys it with other property Continuing, he said there was plenty of room to carry out such alteratio as as were requirod. He was not op- posed, if the Town Clerk thought there was sufficient time, to reconsider the question in its fullest bearings He was convinced that when it had been fully considered the Council would have a different opinion from that they entertained now. C ouncillor TiNNIoN : Was the resolution pasied unani- mously by the committee? Alderman SUTTON: Practically.</p>
        <p>_ . Councillor TINNION : What do you mean by practi- cally? Alderman SUTTON: There was no opponent. They did not all vote, but there was no vote against it. Coupcillor DoDDS suggested that a meeting of the General Purposes Committee should be held to consider the matter.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT : Why cannot the Council go into committee now and de3ide it?</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON: You have not the necessary information before you. Alderman SUTTON said Councillor Pemberton had overlookedthe fact that there were dominant lights in the Eagle and Child property. They would have to do away with the dominant rights people possessed before they could build anything. There were also rights of way. When would Councillor Pemberton take the trouble to go about and look for himself—(laughter)— and get the information he required before attending the Council meeting and making complaints ? It would be far better for him to make inquiries as he (Alderman Sutton) always did. (Laughter* Councillor TINNION: Cannot you give the informa- tion now? We should like some information as to what the whole scheme is likely"to cost. If it is going to cost £lO,OOO say so. Alderman SUTTON: How on earth can we Bats° when we doh% know?</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION : llow on earth did yon get to know as much as you do?</p>
        <p>Alderman SurioN: You are just as competent as any of us to consider what we should have to pay. We shall have to purchase the two shops and a piece of land at the Rylands-street end. With that exception the land will be acquired by exchange. Councillor TINNIO:Isi: Is it absolutely necessary, pro- vided we agree to the scheme, generally, that Benson's and Chantry's shops should be purchased immediately— that is within the next few years? Alderman SUTTON: Yes. That is according to the resolution.</p>
        <p>Councillor TrwNroN: Is it part of theagreament with Garnett's that that must necessarily follow? _</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON: That is one 'condition on which he gave up the £B5O claim. Councillor TINNIoN: Why did you not say so? Alderman SurroN: We have said so. The resolution said so and I also explained it. Councillor DODDS suggested that the matter be referred to the General Purposes Committee to be held that day week. He had a perfeotly open mind on the matter. He was convinced by the speech of Councillor W. Roberts that the matter should be deferred.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEACOCK saie he had inspected the land and was of the opinion that the improvement would cost £25,000.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON ultimately moved that a meeting of the General Purposes Committee be held on Thursday evening at 6.30 to consider the matter. Councillor PEACOCK seconded the motion, which was carried. Councillor PP:MBERTON asked if the Council would meet afterwards.</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK: Subsequently. The IlVfayor: If the General Purposes 'Committee pass it, it will no doubt be passed by the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>LONDON'S BEST.</head>
        <p>LONDON'S BEST.</p>
        <p>"The British Weekly," June 20th, 1903,says :</p>
        <p>Alike among journalists, the general public, and Free Churchmen, there is much said on the growing interest and excellence of" The Daily News." It seems to im- prove day by day, and in all its splendid history, was never more vigorous and vital than it is now.</p>
        <p>Every department of the paper is being brought up to a high level. We are very glad to know that the result has been a largely increased circulation alike in London and in the provinces. " The Daily News "de- served support, were it only for its noble renunciation in the abandonment of betting news. But after all, no paper will succeed for negative merits, and" The Daily News "can stand safely on its quality as a great newspaper. They may begin this support for the sake of the paper but we promise they will continue it for their own sakes.</p>
        <p>Select" The Daily News " as your morning paper,and read it in the Train, Tram, and Bus, as a ailent protest against the Betting and Gambling Evil.</p>
      </div>
