      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>, • • – . • . . •• • . • ./- , . • • • r A :s • – ) • • , 444 : – 4 – – . •</head>
        <p>, • • - . • . . •• • . • ./- , . • • • r A :s • - ) • • , 444 : - 4 - - . •</p>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>'NOTICE TO -CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible,not later than Thursday. Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has a much greater chance of insertion than a long one. We cannot under ordinary cir cumstancesundertake return themanuscripts which are not used.</p>
        <p>PETER DESSF.TT.—In both. That portion of Latchford which is within the Borough is in Lancashire. The Ship Canal is now the borough and County boundary, and that part of Latchford which is on the South side of the Ship Canal is in Cheshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Music in the Park.</head>
        <p>Music in the Park.</p>
        <p>Sir,—With your permission I should like to answer a few of the remarks made by "Onward" in last Satur- day's "Examiner." He says that the Volunteer Band and Fletcher's Band are not, brass but military bands. Evidently he does not know what a military band is,and is equally ignorant of the fact that there is only one mili- tary band in the town (Mr. Downrs). A military band is composed largely of wood, and includes piccolos, flutes, oboes, bassoons, E flat and B flat clarinets, bass and tenor clarinets, bass strings, French horns, and various other brass instruments. A brass band with a few clarinets is not a military band As to his remarks about the "Messiah," eur able Musical Society have given this grand Oratorio at popular prices, and was it a success? "Onward" seems to take credit for the programme played by the Volunteer band on Sunday June 28, but I may state on the autho- rity of the bandmaster, that that particular programme was practised for some weeks before his letter appeared. He asks my definition of secular music. I thought I had made. it plain in my last letter. I would play any music that would be appreciated by the public, provided the music was not wedded to music hall comic songs. I would not on Sunday play waltzes or any dance music, nor was there ever any such music played in the Park on Sundays. I would play any well arranged selections from any of the operas, Sullivan's included. I would not try to cram limy narrow-minded " Unco quid, and rigidly righteous" Tads down other people's throats. The people like that class of music, and what harm does it do? The rigidly righteous ought not to know it when they hear it. I have heard selections from operas played as voluntaries in more than one place of worship, and am much of the opinion of General Booth, who, when remonstrated with for singing his hymns to popular tunes, replied that he did not see why the devil should have all the geed music. "Onward " saysthat he is surprised, that we saw anythinglin his letter to discourage the bands and yet he said they played" old, senseless, and distressing music," and reiterates that the bands have degenerated. Impartial judges at various contests do not think so, or why have so many prizes come to Warrington lately? I again assert that the bands have improved, and I think the public vviarecognise, that the experienced musicians who form the Bands' Association Committee know what good music and good playing are, and will value " Onvtard's" inmature opinion at its 'true worth. I thank "Onward" for his personal regard, but I cannot allow him to make untrue statements without protest, and, if we may judge from the thousands of people who congregate in the park, the public are not of his opinion, and I think that he may safely leave the management of the bands in the hands of the com- mittee, whose.only object like his own, is to further the prosperity of the local bands, and give pleasure and en- joyment to the public. So far as I am concerned this correspondence is now closed, though should ." cl4nward" still think himself capable of improving the 'bands, I again invite him to communicate with the committee direct, and then the value of his musical experience and ability may be estimated, and perhaps his criticisms may have some weight. Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>THOMAS ARMSTRONG, Cliairmsm, Bands' Asfiociation Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Conscience Clause.</head>
        <p>The Conscience Clause.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Hitherto the "Conscience Clause" has been satisfied, where the parents have requested their children's withdrawal from religious instruction, by , giving them secular instruction during the same period. The children havenot necessarily been sent out o the "atmosphere" or even the "hearing" of the lesson given. Indeed. I have known of Protestant children reciting the "Hail Mary," and even children of Baptists averring that " My godfathers and my godmothers did promise and vow three things in my name." Of course "it has always been open to school managers to frame their time tables in such a way as to enable children whose patents desire it to be absent from school during religious instruction." This has not been generally kuown. Moreover' in some parts of the country the children who have been withdrawn from the Scripture lesson have been marked late. This is illegal. Now, sir, a letter of great importance to citizens generally, and Free Churchmen in particular, has been sent by the Secretary of the Education Board, Mr. Morant, to the Rev. Thomas Law, secretary of the National Free Church Council 'Federation, in which he makes the above points perfectly clear. Further Mr. Morant says "that where a parent has notified in writ- ing his intention to withdraw his child from instruction in religious subjects the time during which the child shall be required by law to attend school shall be the whole time for which the school shall be fopen for secular instruction only." This is the proviso con- tained in the new model bylaws which the local educa- tion authorities wishing to revise their own may adopt. -Wherever the local Education Committee adopt these bylaws of the Education Board, or wherever they frame new ones of their own, citizens generally should press for the insertion of this special ,proviso. It is the best safeguard Protestantparents have against the Romanizing tendencies of many Anglican clergy, and the only safe- guard which Free Churchmen have against the prosely- tizing which goes on in some Church schools. Indeed, sir, at Leicester and other towns, for some time past the children of Free Church parents have been withdrawn from Anglican and Roman Catholic Schools, and have met at some central place for regular religious instruction, and have then gone to school for the usual secular lessons. I believe many Free Church Councils have already drafted a syllabus of " Biblicallessons "and are prepared at any moment to take up the training of their own children in Scripture knowledge. It is hoped that the prominence given by the press generally to Mr. Morant's letter to the Rev Thomas Law will have the effect of waking up all Nonconformists to the existenwp of the Conscience Clause, and of causing them to US the "legal right" here admitted to exist. Of course this does not touch the question of passive resistance. Until we get "ease" from the payment of rates for teaching which outrages the "religious convictions" of one-half the church-going citizens of this country we have no alternative but "te resist."—Yours, Scc., JOHN FRANCIS MA.TTHEWS. Baptist Church, Latchford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before T. J. Gillespie (in the chair.) Cot Wood, and Mr. John Unsworth. GAMING,—Thomas Isles and Richard Birkett were charged with gaming with coins at Go'borne on June' 28. Isles did not appear, and a warrant was issued for his arrest.—l3irkett pleaded guilty, and was fined Is. .and costs. THE RAM'S HEAD, EARLESTOWN.— Authority was granted for the temporary transfer of the Ram's Head Hotel, Earlestown, from T. Forster to John Henry Pep- low.—Mr. H. J. Longton made the application. PREPARING A HORSE FOR THE YEOMANRY.—Robert 'Hope, of Earlestown, was charged at the instance of 1 'lnspector Print, R.S.P.C,A. with ill-treating a horse at Vinwick. P.C. Woof! stated that he met defendant in, Warrington-road. He was riding a black horse, which appeared to be suffering great pain. He stopped it and examined it, and found that there were about 50 small wounds in the side, caused by spurs. Mr. Knowles,who defended,stated that the hors was intended to go with -the Yeomanry to camp,and defendant was getting it used -to the saddle. He thought the constable must have been mistalten when he Laid he saw 50 wounds on its side, because he (Mr. Knowles) had seen the horse himself, and .there was no doubt that there were some wounds, and the blood had matted the hair and made it appear as if there were a great many wounds. A fine of 20s. and casts was imposed.—William Forster was charged with ill,treating a horse at Haydock. Inspector Print stated that he saw defendant striking the animal unmercifully, and on examining it found it was covered with wounds caused by defendant thrashing it. A fine of 20s. and costs was imposed. OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—Mary Murray was charged with using obscene language at Newton.—She was fined 10s. and costs or 14 days' hard labour, the Chairman remark- ing that he was,determined to pat a stop to such a dis- gusticg practioe. A DISTURBANCB AT NEWTON.—The first summonses under the new County bylaws, that prohil it making a disturbance in the street, were beard, when John McDermott and Peter Kenny were each fined 2s. 6d. and costs for shoutiag aud challenging each other to fight in Crow-lane,Newton. ALLEGED ASSAULT AT EARLESTONVIC—John Carlin, sweep and ben-ringer,of Earlestown, summoned George Lecke for assaulting him.—Complainant stated that olefendant hit him under the chin. The defendant Zenied on oath.--The case was dismissed.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>PADGATE,</head>
        <p>PADGATE,</p>
        <p>Piestc.—The annual picnic in connection with the Wesleyan Chapel choir washeld on Friday, wben Chester was visited. Excellent arrangements were made by Mr. Leonard Bennett, of Westlauds, for the convenience of the party. A sail up the Dee to Farndon was greatly enjoyed. A short stay was made at Aldford, and tea, partaken of at the Grosvenor Hotel, Padgate was reached shortly before 10 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>MUNICIPAL TRADING.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL TRADING.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR F. R. ROBERTS PROPOSES AN</p>
        <p>AMENDMENT.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bolton Protests.</p>
        <p>The subject. of the proposed inquiry into the tradirtg transactions of the Corporation was again before the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening. Several members fought shy of discussing the subject, inasmuch as they were not in aposition to alter or amend any of the terms of the scheme which was submitted by the Joint Committee, consisting of three representatives of the Town Council and three members of the Non-Trad- ing Committee. It was contended that sufficient time had not been allowed in which to give the five days' notice of amendinents to the motion, which stood in the name of Alderman 'Bolton. After discussion it was decided to adjourn the Council for a weeks° that notice could be given of any proposed amendments. The scheme of inquiry which has been adopted by the Joint Committee, reads as follows:- 1. That an inquiry be held into all trading by past and present members of the Council or officials of the Corporation with the Corporation, either direct or in- direct, through limited companies or officials of such companies, or as contractors or sub-contractors, or any dealings or transactions of any kind whatever, between members of the "gown Council or officials of the Corporation and the Corporation. 2. That the inquiry shall be made by six persons to be called commissioners, three of whom shall be selected by the Town Council and three by the members of the Non-Trading Committee. That each side shall agree to the persons selected without, demur, and that neither side shall appoint a lawyer. 3. That the period of inquiry shall be frem the let of April, 1896, to the 31st March, 1903. 4. That the costs of the inquiry shall 'be borne by the Corporation, and that the commissioners shall receive no remuneration. 5. That the commissioners shall have power to call anybody and everybody, member of the Council, official or servant, contractor, sub-contractor, or other person to give evidence or information touching the inquiry. G. That the commissioners shall have power to call for every minute book, and every book, paper, and document of, the Council and of any and every com- mittee to be at the service of the commissioners, or of such clerks or assistants as are acting for the commissioners. 7. That any person who desires to give evidence (one way or the other) shall be allowed to do so, provided that such evidence has, in the opinion of the commis- sioners, a direct bearing upon the matter to be inquired into.</p>
        <p>8. Taat the Mayor shall give a written indemnity to any witness, being an employe of the Corporation, and requiring it, in respect of any truthful evidence he may give. 9. That neither the public nor the press be admitted to the sittings of the commissioners. 10. That a shorthand report of the proceedings shall be taken by such persons as the commissioners may appoint. 11. That the commissioners shall report their find- ings to the Town Council and to tke Non-trading Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday July 11, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday July 11, 1903.</p>
        <p>was not correct in saying that he has not had an opportunity. Councillor F. R. ROBERTS: It is absolutely in-correct. Councillor TINNION : I deny that it is absolutely incorrect. The Town Clerk sent a copy of the resolu- tions to me and to other members fully a week ago. I said the holidays will be coming on, and it is rather unfair such an important matter should be left over, and it was last Monday night the Town Clerk sent the resolutions away. The TOWN CLERK: On Monday week. Councillor Minos : There were seven or eight days, and anyone could consider the matter. Probably Councillor Roberts may not have had time,but hallos had the opportunity to send in an amendment. I quite admit some of these clauses might have been improved but I think it is desirable to consider them as they are and either reject them or accept them. I don't think the resolutions are the best, but I think they will serve. There is room for an extensive and exhaustive inquiry. If you accept them everyone will have some confidence that the inquiry will be such that it will have the ap- proval of most people in Warrington. I don't see how we can agree to the adjournment, although it might have some advantages. I think on the whole it would be better to discuss the matter to-night. Councillor F. R. ROBERTS: I wish to explain that I should not have been justified in assuming that the motion was going to be brought forward as a motion and incapable of amendment until I received the Town Council agenda. What I received in common with the other members was a report of the investigations and conclusions of the committee. I did not receive the agenda until Friday morning; therefore I could not give five clear days' notice of any amending motion, and my argument is still correct that we were positively to accept the motion in tote or reject it in tote. That is unreasonable and unfair. If it would satisfy the non- traders it would not satisfy some members of the Council, and it would not satisfy the reasonable mem- bers of the community. The MAYOR: I think that is, a personal explanation. Councillor F. R. ROBERTS : It is. Alderman BOLTON : What is the motion? The MAYOR : That we adjourn until next Tuesday at six o'clock. _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <p>Alderman BOLTON : What to do then? Councillor F. R. ROBERTS : I will undertake to give notice of an amending motion which will enable us to discuss various points. Alderman BOLTON : Why not discuss them now? Councillor F. R. ROBERTS: You wont give us an opportunity. Alderman BENNETT : I think the best way would be to discuss it at the General Purposes Committee meeting. Councillor PEMBERTON : We object to the matter being taken to the General Purposes Committee meeting. Our reason is that the proceedings are not reported. This isle public question and it ought to be thrashed out be- fore the public. Alderman BENNETT : We can have the reporters if necessary. The motion for the adjournment until next Tuesday evening was then put. The voting was as follows FOR THE ADJOURNMENT. AGAINST. Alderman Sutton Alderman Bolton „ Smethurst Councillor Davies Francomb Craik „ Hutchinson f James Evans „ Bennett ff Barton Councillor F R. Roberts Brophy • Peacock ft Wilkinson 7f Rogers lf Burton ft Ellison „ Tinnion ff Duncan Forshaw 79 Sampson ft Upson-11. tf W. Roberts-12. The MAYOR: Twelve for the motion, and eleven against. The motion is carried. Alderman BOLTON: I have finished with it. The Council than rose. The fallowing notices of motion were also ad- journed:— Councillor Pemberton to move :—"That the resolu- tion passed at the last meeting of the Cohncil to pull down the old armoury and build in inspector's house be rescinded" and "That a committee be appointed to in- vestigate thelast three slack contracts for Winwick and and Delph-lane waterworks." Councillor Dodds to move :—"That the case of tram cceductor Spencer be investigated by the Tramway and Electricity Committee, and that in harmony with the resolution of the Council of the Ist July, 1902, he be allowed to be represented by such person or persons as he may appoint." Alderman Bennett to move :—" -To consider and con- firm or otherwise such recommendations of the Educa- tion Committee as require the confirmation of the Council."</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR F. R. ROBERTS MOVES THE ADJOURNMENT OF THE COUNCIL. Councillor F. R. ROBERTS said: I Move the adjourn- ment of the Council and Ido so for this reason. I understand that the scheme now before the Council is in the nature of a motion, and as such it is impossible to amend it. If we wish to make any amendment to the motion which is about to be discussed,we can only do so by refusing to pass the motion in. tote or bring forward a new one. It seems to me that there are some portions of the recommendations which will commend them- selves to the minds of all of us; for instance a large part of Clause I. I entirely agree with—that it is necessary and desirable to hold an inquiry. Councillor DODDS : I submit that there is nothing before the Council. The MAYOR: There is a motion to adjourn. Councillor F. R. ROBERTS : ram trying to show that it is not, desirable to reject the motion an .toto. The Council may be satisfied by the speeches which will be made that it is desirable to retain some of these clauses or portions of them whilst finding it desirable to reject others. We have by the procedure of the Council either to accept the resolution in toto or reject it, which is a proposal which would give satisfaction to no one. lam advised that it is neces‘sary to move the adjournment of the Council, so that I may -- give notice of a motion embodying the amendment True to his words—" I have finished with it," Alder- which! propose to make. Otherwise I am perfectly man Bolton withdrew his name from the motion which satisfied that this discussion will lead to no conclusion stood in his name. which will commend itself to the miuds of all of us. Councillor TINNION then gave the Mayor notice that We are all of opinion that the time has come when this he would move the motion, and asked the Mayor to agitation as far as past acts are concerned should cease, call a special meeting of the Council for the purpose to but that can only be done by some form of inquiry. We