Tinnion's supply to him the name or names of the witness or inguiry quite satisfied me. I have nothing .to do with witnesses (if any) who has or have given such evidence, this injuiry. I was appointed by the Council as one of and the person so charged with improper trading shall the three with Councillors Tinnion and James Evans to have the right to call for the production of and to form a basis of inquiry to satisfy the Non-traders. We cross-examine by himself or his solicitor or counsel might as well discuss the matter now as adjourn it be- such witness or witnesses, and the right to call for the cause it will amount to the same thing. If the Council production of all documents containing such evidence don't like this motion, refuse it. The only mistake the and shall be entitled to produce his own witnesses and Council made was that they did not put Councillor evidence, and if any witness shall decline to attend and F. R. Roberts on to consider the matter with the Non- i be cross-examined as aforesaid his evidence shall be traders. .I object to having someone to say " YOU . rejected by the commissioners." should have done this and that. I have done my.best 1 (9.) For Clause 9 read : for the Council and if you don't think it satisfactory lam perfectly satisfied for you to find someone to amend "The inquiry shall be in public, and the Press shall i e admitted." b it. What welave done has satisfied the Non-traders 1. and nothing else will. , Alderman BENNETT : lam sorry to say so, but I do not think that Aldermanßolton's attitude is a 'logical one.</p>
