      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>------- THE NEXT STEP. Discussion Postponed till August.</head>
        <p>------- THE NEXT STEP. Discussion Postponed till August.</p>
        <p>Councillor F. R. Roberts' Amendments.</p>
        <p>GRAPPEN HALL.</p>
        <p>THE CRICKET CLUB.—Frodsham was the scene of Saturday's encounter, and here, again, the Ist eleven proved sacoessful. The home players, on a hard and fiery wicket, took the wickets first. The batsmen gave little trouble against the splendid bowling of the visi- tors, and retired for the total of 28. Carter, 5' for 9, and Warburton, five for 15 runs, shared the honours in the trundling department. Grappenhall responded with 90 runs for .9 wickets. Carter again distinguished him- self with the bat, putting to his credit 43 runs in a dashing and faultless manner. James Burrows came next with 12 to his credit. The 2nd Eleven had as visi- tom Arley, and a close game -ensued, only, 5 runs separating the two elevens* The scores were:—Grappen- hall 2nd, 55; Arley, 50. Gifford for the winners made 29.—Thursday was the annual fixture with the Warring- ton Police Eleven, and for the first time the Policewon. The representatives of the law made the respectable total of 72, to which Grappenhall only answered with 143. Phillips 11, and Hollingwurth and Newey 10 each, were the highest scorers for the visitors. Their bowlers, Houghton and Evans, proved quite effective.—As usual, the players together with their wives, were entertained to tea after the match. SCHOOL TREAT.—The children attending the day and Sunday schools, and those of the Blue Coat School, spent a very pleasant afternoon at Grappenhall Heyes on Friday, by the kindness of Mr. J. Charlton Parr, J.P. D.L. The scholars left the village, headed by the band of the Blue Coat School, at 2 o'clock and marched in procession te the Hayes where they were met by Mr. and Mrs. Parr. On dispersing the young ones set to work and were soon enjoying themselves as only chil- dren can. Cricket, rounders, races for prizes followed, and then tea. After satisfying healthy appetites, more games, and lastly a walk through the beautiful gardens. After the latter, a halt was made in front of the house when Mr. Parr spoke a fewkindly words to the children. The march was then continued homewards, thus bring- ing to an end a most enjoyable outing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <p>free to impose what protective duties they pleas 3 both on foreign countries and upon British com- merce, they should be required to make a small discrimination in favour of British trade, in return for which we are expected to change our whole system and impose duties on food and raw material. Well, I express again my own opinion when I say that there is not the slightest chance that in any reasonable time this country or the Parliament of this country would adopt so one-sided an agree- ment. The foreign trade of this country is so large, and the foreign trade of the Colonies is compara- tively so small, that a small preference given to us upon!that foreign trade by the Colonies would make so trifling a difference—would be so small a benefit to the total volume of our trade—that Ido not believe the working classes of this country would consent to make a revolutionary change for what they would think to be an infinitesimal gain."— London, Jane 9th, 189 G. This is not the less true or cogent because in 1903 Mr. Chamberlain has suddenly become a Protectionist. MR. SEDDON AND BRITISH "STUPIDITY."</p>
        <p>Mr. Seddon, I notice has been expllining why it i s that he is in favour of Mr. Chamberlain's proposals : " During the past twenty years British trade has increased by only 67 millions, whereas American trade has increased by 600 millions,and Germany's commercial expansion during the same period has been equally remArkable. The business methods of the Americans and the Germans were up to date whereas British manufacturers stupidly persisted, in turning out work according to obsolete standards." But what Al r.Seddon does not explain is how Preferential Tariffs are to cure this "stupidity." It is too true that in many directions we need to "wake up," but the greatest national need is a really eficient system of national education, forma to suit the needs of the nation, not to preserve the interests of sects and% denominations.</p>
        <p>"YOU MUST PUT A TAX ON FOOD."</p>
        <p>It is of the first importance that it should always be remembered that Mr. Chamberlain is pledged to put a tax on food. Here are two passages from recent speeches that should be kept in the foreground in every discussion on the subject : "I have already indicated my opinion that a system of Preferential Tariffs is the only system by which this Empire can be kept together."—(Con- stitutional Club, June 26th, 1903.) "If you are to give a preference to the colonies . . . . you must put a tax on food."—(House of Commons. May 28th, 1903.) THE OLD AGE PEITSION BAIT.</p>
        <p>The dear food hook is to be baited by old age pensions and dearer food. As to that there were some instructive speeches at a meeting held in London on Saturday in the discussion on the following resolution : "That this conference of representative London working menand working women, deeply concerned in the welfare of the people as a whole, affirms its belief in the advantages of Free Trade, which has cheapened food, increased wages, and given us a commanding position amongst the nations of the world, and it refuses to make 014-age pensions and</p>
        <p>other social reforms contingent on preferential tariffs in favour of colonial goods, knowing that these would cad to the burdens of the working classes, hamper industry, create capitalist monopolies, and cause international and colonial discords."</p>
        <p>Personally, I have been for over 20 yei stores of materials, upon which to base a it will be a pleasure to me to make so materials helpful to a new generation. "new generation," I want to earnestly imp that this tariff question is not by any mea question for those who have reached a when their positions are assured, as it it tion—a life and death question. indeed—f folks of all classes ; and, next to the youi classes,it is a vital question for the strugl; whether labourers, artisans, or clerks. readers wish to ask me privately, a spe bearing upon the subject of our one-sided shall consider it an agreeable and useful ro my evening leisure hours, to reply, to th ability, on receipt of stamped addressed many readers, who would like further dot] tan t to write for information through th(</p>
        <p>Now supposing a voter said to me that h time or inclination to study the tariff sill self,—in that case, I should bluntly say- for ne!" Mr. Chamberlain is. by aim consent, the greatest all-round statesmen Many of our past statesmen have been "gi or literature, or party politics, but I azn any previous instance, in British history o Statesman who had enjoyed the opportuni mercial, business and municipal training u that Mr. Chamberlain did before he ever e ment. If a great physician or surgeon tel operation is indispensable, we simply special training and,technical skill,and—w question,being satisfied of getting the best a attainable,--we act upon the decision of In the same way, Mr. Chamberlain repres highest combination (and, remember, it i combination to find tip-top business abil political responsibility) in this country, to knowledge and experience essential for what we may term an Empire policy, in cs and common sense are judiciously blend tariff doubt or difficulty, therefore, I wl the political man of the hour, just as y( surgeon or physician.</p>
        <p>Next week, with the editor's permissio into some details as to how and why I tectionist, with some practical facts, trades, which I hope may prove interesti</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
