      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <p>Mr. Frederick Rogers, who is prominently connected with the Old Age Pension movement said: "He knew there was a desire on the part of the nation that there should be a reform in the method of dealing with the aged poor; but old-age pensions ought not to be established on a political quicksand, as they would be if they rested on Preferential Tariffs. Protection was uncertain in its action, but history showed that it had always been a danger to the working classes, and the only thing that was certain was that food would be dearer." _</p>
        <p>"HIGH FALUTIN FISCAL POLICY*"</p>
        <p>I cannot do better than add what was said in support of the resolution by Mr. Crooks,the Labour Member for Woolwich : ft Mr. W. Crooks, M.P., in supporting the reso- lution, asked them to remember the benefits they enjoyed under Free Trade. One of the benefits was the privilege of having a cheaper suit of clothes. Formerly a man used to inherit his suit of clothes from his father. (Laughter.) The father perhaps paids for it, wore it at his wedding, then at a christening, and then at a funeral.— (Laughter.) Then it went to his children,one of whom made money out of it at the finish.— (Laughter.) Now a man could have three suits where his father only had one. As to dearer food, howja ould they appreciate having half a leg of mutton instead of a whole one, and how was that going to increase the volume of trade ?—(Cheers.) To tax the dear old mother for the benefit of the children of the colonies would mean that they would have to go without food which they could now get. One of the speakers had asked how it was that under Free Trade they had fly millions on the verge• of starvation. His answer was because they had not taxed the proper sources of income.—(Cheers.) Why did not the land bear its share ?—(Cheers.) Why were not mining rents and royalties the property of the nation?—(Cheers) The people were to be taxed to bear the burden of this high-falutin fiscal policy. Mr. Crooks cora- s pared the new fiscal proposals to a wonderful swivel gun invented by an American. It had muzzles at both ends; it revolved as it fired, and it was calculated to work groat havoc amongst friends and foes alike.—(Loud laughter.) If these pro- posals came into fore e 25 per cent of the nation would be a. little better off, but 75 per cent would • be infinitely worse off.</p>
        <p>Workmen will do well to ponder this.lt comes from a man who knows; from a man who started life a work- house child, and who therefore knows from actual ex- perience what the struggle for existence'really is.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>MR. H. P. HUGHES ON "ONE-SIDED FREE-TRADE v. PROTECTION."</head>
        <p>MR. H. P. HUGHES ON "ONE-SIDED FREE-TRADE v. PROTECTION."</p>
        <p>Mr. H. P. HUGHES, 3, Fairfield-road, Stockton Heath, writes :</p>
        <p>Some little time ago, in the course of a conversation with a leading bank-oMcial of Warrington, who, at the moment was off duty, the subject of a protective tariff came up for discussion. When he found that I had for many years, and in various lines of business, made a practical study of the subject, he kindly said that—as Warrington is a very important trading centre—l would certainly be serving a useful purpose by placing the results of my study before the public, by courtesy of the local press. Since thit conversation, the tariff question has come ahead, as the vital question of the hour, arid various Warrington business men, who know my Protectionist leanings, have urged me to help in moulding local o pinion. I must confess thit I an venturin3 rather diffidently for the tariff topic is. un3.9ubtedly, the most delicat e and diMcult subject in the entire range of practical politics. Most of the ordinary problems of daily life offer, perhaps, only three or four miin aspects for con- sideration, and a tolerably safe decision can be ma de by a careful weighing of each of these few salient features ; but, when we come to this vast question of a protective tariff, the more one studies it, and the more it seems to fairly bristle with points of view.As an illustration of the state of punlic opinion, just now, I would mention that a Wigan friend of mine, who is one of the coolest and clearest thinkers I know of, came to Warrington on" Walking Day," and asked me to help him to under- stand the great Free Trade problem. He said 'that he had read so many nowspiper letters, giving opposite points of view, until he had become thoroughly confused and undecided. From what I hear, in going about, I think that my friend's state of confusion, perplexity, and indecision, is typical of the mental condition of most of us, at the present juncture. But, and but, and but . . . . a General Election may come upon us, at short notice, and we shall have to promptly decide</p>
        <p>"This proposal requires that we should abandon "the ticket"(as our American kinsfolk say) upon which our systent in favour of theirs, and it is in effect e are going to vote, so it behoves us to look ahead, that while the Colonies should be left absolutely nd to prepare our minds to act definitely, and wisely.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>SECRET COMMISSION. SHEFFIELD ALDERMAN SENT TO PRISON.</head>
        <p>SECRET COMMISSION. SHEFFIELD ALDERMAN SENT TO PRISON.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bovros : I withdraw the motion, and have done with it. Alderman BENNF.TT : Anxious as I am for an inquiry, I don't think that we are in the position that the Non- Traders MIR say, " You must take this word for word" We are not slaves. We are free citizens of a free country—(hear, hear)—and it would be an ignominious position to put us in for the non-traders to say such a thing. I may say that with 19-20ths of the motion I am in entire accord, but there are verbal alterations which might be made and which would tend to seenre the aim in view—that the cloud of suspicion which has hang over Warrington so long should be dissipated and the town recover its good name, and that if any have been guilty of the acts which are alleged, they should be properly punished by public opprobrium. But I think the non-traders are much more reasonably-minded men than you give them credit for, and that they are quite prepared to listen to what is fair. (Hear, hear.) I should be very sorry to think they would say "You must either take this, word for word, or we shall have nothing to do with the matter." That does not com- mend itself to me as a free Englishman, and I would not submit to it although I wish for the fullest inquiry. I am surprised Alderman Bolton should come with this pistol at our heads. I object to the pistol. Alderman BOLTON: You are always objecting. Councillor TINNION : I did not intend to say any- thing either upon this or upon the following resolution I have had quite enough of it—more than I shall ever have again. But there is a great deal of trath in what Alderman Bolton has said. The resolutions we have prepared are what the non-trading section will take and The Joint Committee of the two houses of Parliament nothing else. It was distinctly understood that that appointed to inquire into the question of municipal was the minimum they would accept, and therefore it trading sat again on Monday, the Earl of Crewe presid- was undesirable even to brin