 Since then no effort had been statement is Unaffected by anything Mr. Rylands says made in consequence of the heavy calls which had from in reply to a contention I did not, as a matter of fact, time to time been made upon -the people in various advance. ways. Now that things were brighter, and they hoped there was not the same call upon them, the time had come when they could once more assist this very use- ful institution. A gentlemen's committee had already been formed but they could do nothing without the ladies—(laughter)—and they now ventured to ask for the ladies' sympathy and co-operation in the movement which they were very anxious should be a great success. (Applause.) Mr. WILSON said he had received letters regretting non-attendance from Mrs. Piokmere, Mrs. Monro and others. Mr. E. H. JoiTSsoN addressed the gathering and pointed out that the institution maintained 288 lifeboats round the coasts. The whole of the funds collected at Lifeboat Saturday demonstrations were devoted abso- lutely to the lifeboatmen and to the widows and orphans of lifeboatmen. None of the money went to the general expenditure of the institution. The speaker referred to the recent disaster to the Mumbles lifeboat and said that a fund had been raised whereby the widows and orphans of the men who were lost would be placed in such a position that they woukl never want. That was one of the objects for which the help of Warrington people would be solicited. Other objects to which the sums collected were devoted, were the payment of the lifeboat men, and the granting of pensions and retiring allowances to coxwains and other members of the crews who had long served the institution. The amount raised throughout the country 'last year by Lifeboat Saturday collections was £15,047 and the amount expended by the institution on the objects named was £22,395. In regard toithe working of the institution he did not think there was another organiza- tion in England of a kindred nature that was managed in such an economical way. There were something like 17,000 men enrolled on their books as volunteers who were always ready to go out in the boats. He hoped that Warrington, instead of sending £65, would this year contribute an amount more worthy of the impprt- ance of the town, and that in future years the people would recognize their responsibility and send an annual contribution. (Applause.) of Warrington and neighbourhood heartily approves of 1 Mrs. PICTON moved "That this meeting of the ladies the proposal to make a third collection in aid of the funds of the Royal Lifeboat Institution, and that a ladies' lifeboat Saturday auxiliary be appointed/for the purpose of assisting in the movement." Miss DAVIES seconded. Mr. WILSON explained that it was intended to make a collection in each of the nine wards of the borough, also in Latchford, Grappenhall, Thelwall, Lyrnm, Stockton Heath, Walton, Whitley, Moore, DaresburY, Penketh, Great Sankey, Winwick, l'adgate„and Fea.m- head. It was proposed to appoint a lady president for each ward and district, and one or two ladies to assist her, and the president would be responsible for the col- lection. Collecting books would be provided and literature would be circulated. The meeting then selected ladies who will be asked to superindent the collectiont in the various wards and districts. The MAYORESS was elected President of the auxiliary, Mrs. Pickmere vice-president, and Miss Mabel Fair. dough will be asked to accept the office of hon. secre- tary. The following executive committee was appointed the members to have power to add to their number.— The Mayoress, Mesdames Broadbent, Pickmere, H. Greenall, H. Roberts, Smethurst, Picton, and James Fairclough, and Miss Davies. Mr. WILSON said the collecting books would be issued in the course of a week or two and an appeal would be sent round.</p>
        <p>"THE HAPPY FAMILY IDEA."</p>
        <p>THE UNITED STATES ANALOGY,</p>
        <p>Mr. Rylands tells us that "the ease of the British Empire in territory and populatizn is analogous to the United States." "1 call the United States to witness," he says "That under conditions analogous to the British Empire Protection need not be incompatible with pros- perity." But let us see what that high authority, Sir Robert Giffen, has to say on the matter. "On the question of an Imperial Zollverein in particular, as it has been so much put forward, 1 may be allowed to make a remark. A closer union between the colonies and the mother country can- not, I believe, be found in the formation of a Zollverein. A Zollverein presupposes, indeed, th geographical contiguity of the countries forming i so that, by making one ring fence round the aggre- gate, Customs barriers between one country and. another may be abolished. A more important cause. of extended freedom of trade there could not be But the British Empire cannot become a Zollverein for the simple reason that its constituent parts are not contiguous, and, on the contrary, are widely separated, communicating only by ships, which, for obvious reasons, must always be surper- vised at the different ports by the authori- ties of the Customs. Even with a common tariff, therefore, if that were possible, the Customs barriers and the delays and obstructions incident thereto would remain between the different parts of the Empire. There would be great di:Roulties besides in a common tariff, for the reason that the different countries of the Empire consume different things and want to tax different things ; and the things one country wants to tax are very often mainly the products of another. Oar self- governing colonias for instance, find it convenient to impose duties for revenue purposes on the manufactur's imported mainly from the mother country. India again, taxes at least one article— salt—which is untaxed in the UnitediCin.gdom, if not throughout the r est of the empire. The mother country in turn taxes the wine, tea, and sugar which some of the colonies produce as wall as other articles which they wouldprobably send to 136 if we allowed them to pass our Customs barrier freely. A Zollverein between uncon- tiguous countries with such differing interests as riegards revenue would thus appear a nalterial im; possibility."</p>
        <p>Now the United. States internally has no custom barriers—it is a great Free Trade area. It is not pro- posed to turn the British Empire into one. The Canadian manufacturer is still to be protected against the British, as is the Australian and South African. The conditions in the United States are therefore not now "analogous "to those of the British Empire, nor will they be if Mr. Chamberlain's proposals are accepted.</p>
        <p>WHERE IS THE TRADE TO COME FROM ?</p>
        <p>The answer to that question shows up the imposture of this proposal to establish preferential tariffs. No one can discover: where the increased trade with the Colonies is to coins from. As it is we have got the trade with our own Colonies already, and there's not very much more trade left that we can get. Is the Canadian expected to wear three shirts, or the Australian lady two hats in order to benefit the Mother Country. For all goods the Colonies themselves manufacture the tariff wall is to be maintained against the British manufac, turer, so that the only field for extension is the very small amount of manufactured goods which at present the Colonies buy from foreign nations. Under our pre', sent system we have 71 per cent. of the total imports into Australia, 66 per cent. of the total imports into New Zealand, and 35 per cent. of the total imports into Canada, which largely relies on the United States. The Canadian preferences have not substantially altered theproportions in the case of Canada, •nor does the absence of preference deprive us of the lion's share in Australia and New Zealand. The margin in these two last colonies consists mostly of goods which we do not produce, and which must always come from foreign countries. Where, then, is the possibility of this vast new colonial trade awl wealth which Mr. Chamberlain</p>
        <p>promises? Indeed, Mr. Chamberlain, even since he has been Colonial Secretary, has dealt effectively with this very point : _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>From Bench to Dock.</head>
        <p>From Bench to Dock.</p>
        <p>The trial concluded at Sheffield Quarter Sessions, on Saturday of Alderman Hobson, chairman of the Health Committee, and the first working man to be placed on the Commission of the Peace for the borough, on char Tes of offences under the Corrupt Practices Act, by receiving secret , commission as reward for voting for the purchase of certain land. The prosecution was in- stituted by the Public Prosecutor, and it was alleged that the defendant suggested to a friend that certain land should be offered to the Corporation, and in Coun- cil seconded the purchase, reeeiving £l.OO commission on the sale. Defendant denied the receipt of commis- sion, alleging he had an undisclosed partnership in the land, and that the £l.OO was profit, which constituted no legal offence. The case excited intensepublic interest. Defendant's counsel made a moving speech, alluding to the num- ber of times the occupant of the dock, sitting as a magistrate, had administered justice in the same court. The defendant was found guilty and sent to gaol for three months in the second division.</p>
      </div>
