        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON.— MUNICIPAL BYE-ELECTION. 1903. TO THE BUb GENES OF HOWLEY WARD, LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— Having been requested by a large and influential body of ratepayers of both political opinions,and also by the Conservative Ward Corrmittee,to offer myself as a candidate for election as one of your represents- ' tives in the Town Connell. I have consented to stand as an Independent Conservative. Being a large resident ratepayer in the war I myself you can quite understand there, if elected. I should keep a close watch over your interests ; and every- thing that might tend to the progress and advantage of the borough would certainly have my support. I am a determined advocate of economy in the spending of public money, and I am of opinion that every step possible consistent with efficiency should be taken to reduce the heavy rates that we at present have to pay. I am opposed to all improper and illegal trading with members of the Corporation, and would vote against any such trading. Should you do me the honour of returning me as one of vlur representatives, I assure you that your Interests, collectively and individually, will receive my most earnest consideration and attention. Trusting to receive the favour of your votes and assistance on the day of election, Tuesday next, July 14th. Believe me to remain, Ladles and Gentlemen, Your obedient servant, JACOB SANKEY. 21, Church-street, Warrington, July 7th, 1903.</p>
