        <p>2/6 P ER(IB PrdcBE iIcItuADEDL) QUAR T Fall From a Haystack. Kona AND IRISH WHISKEY A singular accident occurred on Thursday morning, ALSO AT A man named Edward Oxley, 51 years of age, of no 3/-, 3/9, 4/6 AND 5/3 PER QUART, fixed residence, was sleeping on a haystack in a field 118, 2/-, 2/6, 3/-, AND 3/6 PEE BOTTLE, at Longford, when he fell to the ground and broke Fhis leg. He was taken to the Infirmary and detained uuroN BEM, wINE MERCHANT% 20, RENSHAW STREET, LIVERPOOL, Volunteer Manoeuvres. (2 Minutes from Central and Lime Street Stations.) This (Saturday) afternoon the Warrington Volun- teers will parade at four o'clock and march to a position near Stretton where manoeuvres will take place. The band and bugle band will attend.. Leap from a Train at Sankey. As the 11.20 a.m. train from Liverpool to Hull was passion- through Sankey Station on Thursday a man leaped from the train. He was picked up by the rail- way officials, and taken to the Warrington Infirmary , where it was found that he had sustained severe in- juries to the head. The man is a Pole and has, up to , the present, been unable to give any information about ' himself. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC, is the ICING OF TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warring-ton.—Advt. Coming Events. The Infirmary. The Rev. John Yonge will preach at Wycliffe T lie Hon. T reasurer begs to acknowledge the receipt Church to-morrow (Sunday) morning and evening,. f..)e At Golborne-street Baptist Church to-morrow (Sun- he •l ate ate of a sum of 1) Robert 6arnett the and Co'- Messrs. Robert , amount of Per legacy bqueathed by day), the pastor's anniversary will be celebrated. The Rev. H. Dunnico will preaoh morning and evening Davies and there will be special music. Personal. The examiners in the Honour School of Natural Science (Oxford University) have just issued the class list. The first name on the list in class one is that of Mr. H. H. Carleton (Keble), youngest son of the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, vicar of Holy Trinity Church, Warrington. We are pleased to learn that Mr. J. Shaw Green is making very favourable progress and has been able to get out of doors to enjoy the fresh air during the past week. His many friends will hope that the im- provement will continue and that Mr. Shaw Green will soon be restored to good health. The Rev. W. Bracecamp has been appointed acting- chaplain to the Warrington Volunteers. In the list of successful candidates at the recent intermediate examination held in connection with the Institute of Chartered Accountants, appears the name of R. A. Holford, of Arpley-street, Warrington, who is articled with Messrs. Morris, Gregory. Holmes, and Hansford, York-street, Manchester. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRED GE-STREET.—Ad, Licensing. At the Borough Police Court, on Monday Mr. Cherry was granted temporary authority to sell at the King's Head, Winwick-street, in lieu of Mrs. Hilton. PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER the CLOCK BEST for BOOTS in LANCASHIRE.—Advt. An Athletic Clergyman. On Saturday the Rev. E. E.B. May, curate at Dares bury Church, took part in the throwing the hammer amateur championship competition at. Northampton. His distance was 118 feet, and he proved only second best to Tom R. Nicholson, Kyles AC., whose distance was 142 feet seven inches. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF TON[cs.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. The Walking Craze. There was another local walking contest on Satur- day, when 16 turned out from the Boar's Head, Pig- hill, and walked to Irlam and back. The result was: 1, Gibbens, 2hrs, 59minn 2, Whittaker, 3hrs.; third and fourth, Messrs. C. Wood and Warburton (dead heat), 3hrs. 10min.; 5, Whalley ; 6, Wilkinson. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS NIINERAL WATERS.—Advt. Grappenhall Heyes. That the kindness of the Mayor in throwing open the gardens and grounds at Grappenhall Reyes on Sat- urday was appreciated was evident from the large num- ber of visitors from Warrington. The weather was ideal and the excellent condition of the grounds ex- cited general admiration. BESTBOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER the CLOCK.—AdVt. Bird Show. 'P lie eighth annual young bird show in connection with the Warrington Ornithological Society was held at the Blue Bell bun HorsemarCat-street, on Saturday. There were about 1.00 birds entered, the classes being well represented considering the bad breeding season experienced. The judge was Mr. A. Welling, of Pres- cot. The Hydes special prize for the best young bird in the show was awarded to Mr. J. D. Leigh. . ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERA WATERS.—Advt. Music in the Park. The League of the Crossand will occupy the stand in the Park on Sunday and play the following programme under the conductorship of Mr. T. Hynes— Hymn," 0 Salutaris," arranged by T. Hynes ; March, " Cornet," W. Rimmer ; Overture, " Prince and Peasant," H. Round; Selection, " Crispino," Ricci ; Cornet solo, " Ora Pro Nobis," Piccolommi : Glee (descriptive), " Hours of Beauty," H. Round ; " God Save the King." A GOOD RESOLUTION.—Wear ALP TAYLOR'S Wonderful Boots.—Advt. Struggle With a Burglar. A fierce struggle with a burglar took place early on Saturday morning at the shop of Mrs. Jowett, Allen- street. At about three o'clock, Mr. and Mrs. Jowett heard movements in the house, and on Mr. Jowett making a search, three burglars were found on the premises. Two immediately decamped on seeing Mr. Jowett, who at once seized the other one. A fierce struggle took place, in which both Mr. Jowett and the burglar received injuries, but the latter unfortunately ''(tot away. It was found that an entrance had been effected at the rear of the premises by breaking three panes of glass. It is stated that the depredators entered the shop the previous evening, and asked for assist- ance, and oa being refused threatened that they would return later and get what they wanted and more. They were evidently about to carry out their threat when detected. You CANNOT Do BETTER! Buy your boots and shoes at ALP. TAYLOR'S. Cheapest and bast town. Expensive Flowers. At Northwich Petty Sessions on Tuesday, William Howard Ashcroft, insurance agent, 114, Cartwright- street, Warrington, was summoned for damaging rhododendron shrubs at Marbury, on June 10th.—Mr. Brown, from the office of Messrs. Trafford and Cook, prosecuted, and said the damage done to the rhododen- dron shrubs was very frequent. It was difficult to get hold of a responsible person to fix wlth the offence. —P.C. Gilpin said about 11 a.m. on June 20th he was in Marbury Hollows, when he saw the defendant over the fence breaking off the rhododendron blooms and handing them to a lad. When defendant saw witness he said "Am I trespassing ?" Witness told him he was, and took his name and address.—James Wilkin- son, woodman in the employ of Lord Barrymore, esti- mated the damage done at 2s. 6d.—The Bench ordered the defendant to pay the daAnage. and fined him ss. and costs, the total being gd 19s. GIVE THEM A TRIAL.— ALF.T A.vr.ort's woo ler- ful boots for everybody. The Education Office Staff. Mr. A. J. Jolley presided at a meeting of a sub- committee of the Education Authority held on Monday for the purpose of making the final selection of candi- dates for the positions of chief clerk and stationery and stores clerk.—The salary of the chief clerk is to be .£175 and his duties will be to act as assistant to the director, to have charge of all the clerks and the office arrangements, and to direct the clerical work in connection with the elementary schools. The salary of thelstationery and st ores cleal is to be :k:125, and his duties are defined as follows :—To arrange for esti - mates for stationery and school requisites; to take stock in the schools; to be responsible for the proper keeping of the stock and stores account, and the inventory book; and to examine and report upon the materials supplied by the contractor.—The three selected candidates for the chief clerk- ship who appeared before the committee were :—Messrs. William Albert Clegg, age 27 school mancr aement clerk on the official staff of the Salford School Board; Samuel Dixon Glover, age 27, senior clerk in the education department of the county borough of Birkenhead; and Robert Benjamin Roberts, aged 27, private secretary to the clerk to the Liverpool School Board. Messrs. Glover and Roberts tied with four votes each, and the Chairman gave a casting vote in favour of the latter, who was then unanimously agreed to.—The three selected candidates for the other position were :—Messrs. George CRAWFORD'S Atkinson, aged 24, head assistant in the educational department of the North of England School Furnish- ing Company, Limited, Darlington; Arthur S. Elliot, age 23, assistant stock and stores clerk in the office of the Bradford School Board; and John Haslam Greenwood, aged 261, assistant in the accountant's department of the Oldham School Board Mr. Elliot was unanimously chosen.—The appoint- ments will have to be confirmed by the Education Committee, and approved by the Council.</p>
        <p>Messrs. W. D. Houghton. Blackp3ol was the resort selected by the emplo this firm for their annual excursion. About workmen left in two tr ins early on Saturday Im and Blackpool was reached about eight o'clock. weather was splendid, and the trippers dispen various places, and spent the day asthey desired. return journey was commenced at about half-past Warrington being reached before ten o'clock. Exc arrangements had 1)3,n made by the following mittee:—Messrs. Wilding, Ellison, Asnip, Cook,ll Siddall (secretary). Penkath Wesleyan Sunday S:hool. The te milers at this school had their annual sion to Windermere on Saturday. The party num over 50 left Warrington by tho 5.30 train in s saloon, arriving at Windermere soon after 8 a.m.. fast WAS served without dAay, after which the vi in four brakes, were dri yea to B)muess, where the barked on tILt sta,ner for Ambleiide, returning I 12.25 boat via lowness to Lake Side. Tea was pr at the Swan Hotel, Newby Bridge. The return j( was from Lake Side via Inv erston Grange and Car The party arrived at Warrington at about tea and a 'bus was waiting to take them to Penketh. Bold-street Wesleyan Choir. Success attended the annual picnic in conn with this choir which took place on Saturday to VC mere, Arnbleside, and Langdale. A saloon was en and a very enjoyable day was expnienced. The ing wasan exceptional treat on account of the sl oellent weather and bracing atmosphere. The br( 'at 8.30, a.m. and the dinner at 3.30 were spiel served at the Waterhead Hotel, and the party in( in boatingand visiting the rose garden at the h thelake. The verdict of the choir was that the was the beat they had ever had, and thanks were vo the trustees of the Bold-street Church for the' donation, and to the registrar, Mr. C. H. Page, admirable arrangements. The party reached home at 9.45.</p>
