        <p>WARRINGTON LICENSING PROSECUTION. Fine for Permitting Drunkenness. Prosecution under the new Licensing Act occupied „.".• attention of Mr. F. Monks and other magistrates at 'Le Warrington Police Court on Monday. Ann Hargreaves ?lerley street, was summoned for having on the 20th 411,e been found drunk on licensed premises, namely,the I.?°,den Ball Inn, Winwick road, and Martha Welsby,the i,,e4sisee, was charged with permitting the drunkenness ,Z.take place. Mr. Crompton (Browne and Crompton) ded. Chief Constable said the information against the rosst,„„ ee was laid under section 4 of the Licensing Act, • He had to prove that a person was drunk on a'Llised premises; not that he or she was served with ✓. The defendant had to prove the negative and if Islcoulddo so no one in the court would be more pleased -'144 himself. He also wished to say that this was a. r:„secution entirely without malice. Inspector Row-1 %,,‘nam at 25 minutes to nine on Saturday night, the ralune, visited the Golden Ball, and in the kitchen i trunl Mrs. Hargreaves in a deplorably drunken condi- tn--so drunk that whenshe was asked to stand up she 24". Mrs. Welsby's attention was called to her, and 1, replied "I have never seen her before, lam not Ponsible for her condition; she has not been served But he (the Chief Constable) would call a 'wises to say that she had seen Hargreaves at least a ptAr of an hour on the premises, and he contended rs. Welsby had had ample opportunity of notio- Zg her condition, and that she had not taken, the steps ,ZlLternplated by the statute with reference to the re- -Val of a drunken person from the house. ti;:l44Pecaor Rowbotham was called and bore out the lof Constable's statement. On attempting to leave A • kit Chen, he said, Mrs. Hargreaves fell down "all of WherP." Mrs. Hargreaves said she was dizzy, but Mrs. 41,4b,y did not dispute the fact that the woman was aoh,-, Clinton, a cab driver, now employed in Douglas, ,17q that on the evening in question he was calledto the --Q3' Horse Inn at seven o'clock. He drove Mrs. Her- and another woman to the Golden Ball. Mrs. • rgreaves was wider the influence of drink. Witness r,t:ti a drink at the invitation of Mrs. Welsby, and Mrs. *44„srgreavas was in the same room. tlizabeth Bentley, Orford, Lane said she went to the tt,emises on the evening namedand saw Mrs. Hargreaves ',foto was not sober. Mrs. Welsby asked her to leave lban?„. y times and Mrs. Hargreaves replied "Well, love 4m going." Mary Ann Evans, Winwick-road, said Mrs. Hargreaves 'trail drunk and Mrs. Welsby asked her to go home. She blade no attempt to have the woman ejected. John Wm. Kinston, licensee of the Bay Horse Inn, tid Mrs. Hargreaves and another woman called at his 93e on the evening in question, and had a small •Zysisky each. They remained 20 minutes or half an 4our. Mrs. Hargreaves was not drunk. tk_kr• Crompton said it was no ground of his defence -.Mt bin. Hargreaves was not drunk. The only question id Mrs. Welsby permit drunkenness within the tk_mulbig of the new Act? That 411. ct put the onus on ir defendant to show that she used reasonable prec,au- 14nsiWhat precautions was she expected to use? Mrs. teea yee ves had been a personal friend of hem for 20 ts. pv further than that she was an old woman, being t er 60 years of age, and he submitted thatiMrs. Welsby (1 not possibly he expected to deal in a summary 17juer with her, as she might with a young man. It fr,Lbeen proved by the prosecution that whatever drink Cgr„, , eaves had she had had absolutely none in the s'4Gen Ball, and that Mrs. Welsby had continually re- is "tad her to leave. The only question was; "What 11(,.,1t11,(1 what is not—permftting drunkenness?" The 14 7," had been told that as soon as Mrs. Welsby went a 14,76 kitchen she told Hargreaves to go home and that wse not served with drink; she could not possibly IPected to call a barman and turn such an old )10'441 out. Mrs. Welsbyand her husband had kept the itfor twenty pars without any offence whatever. 104 .‘1 Chief Constable: That is not so. I object. If box In i sist upon that you must put Mrs. Welsby n the 1,4411 d I will cross-examine her on my records. Crompton: Your records are very great. IN; Chief Constable: They are records of facts. AT:e don't be insolent. et)ce'r:Crompton: lam not going to stand any impud- e", 'rota a consiable whether he is the head of the '‘vted bodyor not. -the Chairman: Don't lost inlays any exhibition of per, please. The Chief Constable: I won't Billinegate with him , The Bench consulted in private, and on their return I The Chairman said they did not consider a very ._4lltious offencehad been committed, there was a certain `rsount of mitigation. At the same time they thoughtl 1:t1,4 offence had been committed, which they could not rlook. It was a pity Mrs. Welsby did not cell in • Police to take charge of the case. Under the oir- (nstanoes Mrs. Welsby would be fined 10s. and costs "4 2s, in all), and Mrs. Hargreaves ss. ROUND THE 'WORLD. (2,„llenry Frederick, of New York, arrived it Plymouth tm,Yednesday on board the Deutschland, from New %at. For a wager of 20,000 dollars Mr. Frederick is IcLing to encircle the globe within 45 days. He left I),,fvf York on July 2nd, and to succeed must return trel August 15. His only chance of sucoees is to catch e trans-Siberian express, which leaves Moscow on 4tnrcla y,for Port Arthur; failing thathe loses, as the, "toe is only a weekly one. MOAT FARM STOCK SOLD. p,.. 11e live and farming stock formerly in possession of tllal, now lying under sentence of death,. were sold %ftesnetion at the _Moat Farm, °layering, on Wednesday rnoon. Crowds of people assembled from all parts. harness worn by the pony on the night Dougal e off for the last time with Miss Holland fetched and the trap £5 15s. The chestnut gelding Prince, lirihd which Miss Holland had her last ride, realized s;',l guineas, and a six-year-old roan mare made 42 ileas. In all, nine horses, seven head of stock, and Pigs comprised the live stock. %Li the House of Commons on Wednesday, Mr. Crooks, le Member for Woolwich, carried up to the table,on his A,Allider, a formidable petition, signed by 69,684 persons ayour of the remission of the sentence on Sergeant who was convicted of the murder of his wife,and ''`ow serving a sentence of penal servitude foi life.</p>
        <p>TO THE EVRAING STAR. All hail, thou lovely Vesper Star That shin'st in yonder heaven afar. All unattended and alone, Lit up with glory all thine own • To charm the closing hours of day, Now passing from the world away! Thou art the harbinger of rest, And weary men with toil opprest, All grateful turn their eyes to thee And feel their limias again set free, , As by the light thy presence yields They homeward tread the lonely fields. And more than this: thy gentle reign So soothes the throbbing world again 'That no discords from earth arise To jar upon the silent skies, Or mingle with the breath of praise That rises in a thousand ways. Shine on, lone orb of beauty mild, O'er fertile lands and deserts wild, That men of every clime and race May look upon Thy lovely face, And see, as in a vision fair, Eternal power and goodness there. J..H. W. CLINDINNING. July, 1903. CRICKET. rB most pleasant form of entertainment during Bummer is the Garden Party, with its charming amusements of Lawn Tennis, Croquet, Badminton. and Lawn Bowls, every requisite for which is made in its greatest perfection by the famous manufacturers, Gizo Buessir &amp; Co• Ask local Sports Outfitter for their Catalogue. If not procurble, apply to 36 &amp; 38, Queen Victoria Street. London. Factories—Peckham, London; and Himmel'. Suffolk.</p>
