        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. At the meeting in connection with this Society on Sunday, Mr. B. klarrar presided and spoke upon sym- pathy. It was always very cold and icy, he said, where there was no sympathy; bnt very warm and helpful where sympathy existed. The people of Warrington showed their sympathy on Friday with the scholars of the Sunday schools and whoever tried to do away with Walking Day would undertake a difficult and unenviable task.—Mr. Bathgate, of Liverpool, called upon all pre- sent to "think." He asked them to think of Christ ; t 0 think of his brief ministry on earth and yet how much he accomplished. The names of all great men receded into the background, but the name of Christ lived on, became He was Divine. A solo was efficiently sung by Miss Lucy Travis. Mr. W. H. Payton Mus. Bac. pre- sided at the organ. Mr. Downes and Mr. Healey took part in the meeting. Methodist Free Church, Runcorn. At Elleemere-street Church, Runoorn, on Sunday, thee Sunday school anniversary sermons were held, when sermons were preached, morning and evening, by thel Rev. H. W. B. Chapman, of Birmingham. A musical service was held in the afternoon. Special hymns were sung by the choir and scholars at all the services. The morning anthem was "The Lord is lily Shepherd." (Schubert). At the afternoon service Ella Lightfoot delivered th., opening address, and solos, recitations, Scc., were given by the Misses Paul and Ardern, W. Cross, Edith Parker, Joseph Naylor John Parker, and IMessrs. T. Curzon and J. Moores. l'he evening anthem. was "Awake, thou that sleepest " (Stainer). Collec- tions were taken in aid of the school funds. Baptist Church, Golborne-street. On Sunday evening last the pastor preached his second sermon on Haeckel's book entitled The Riddle of the Universe," taking as his subject HaeCkel's -views upon God and upon immortality. At the outset the preacher urged his hearers not to be alarmed at the attacks made upon the Christian faith, for truth could never be destroyed. Haeckel, he stated, called himself a Monist and his message was "Accustom yourselves to believe that God,freedom,and immortality are hallucina- tions." This great scientist, however, did not prove or explain the origin of life. Evolution traced life from its minutest form to its highest development but did not explain how things came into existence in the first instance. If there were no God there would be no meaning in life, and we had to accept the idea of a great creative mind. There was no disagreement be- tween science and religion, for the highest science and the truest religion must be mutually consistent, and if they appeared to be contradictory it was because we misunderstood them. The gift of spiritual discernment is necessary to the understanding of many things, and we could therefore , conclude that there was ; behind this world a spiritual purpose. It needed something more than the intellect to • apprehend things in the spiritual universe; the things of God could not be demonstrated, any more than any fact could be demonstrated without first making an assump- tion, but they fitted in with our experience, and on these grounds we believed that beyond this life there was a great eternal Being, one who loved us and cared for us. The great argument brought forward by Haeckel in ' support of his disbelief in God and in Immortality was that certain savage tribes were absolutely devoid of any idea of God or a future life; other men, however, who were greater authorities on the subject, told us that even these degraded tribes had some idea of God and a future life, but even admitting Haeckel to be right, it only proved that when men lost their faith in God and in Immortality they sunk to the • lowest depths of degradation, and a belief in them was a sign of progress. Mystery should be no reason for disbelief, for character was often enabled by deeds done in mystery. Latchford Baptist Church. There were large congregations at this place of worship on Sunday, when the annual sermons were preached by Mr. R. Cameron, M.P. In the morning a young people's service was held, Mr. C. E. Parker delivering an address. At the evening service there was a crowded congregation, and special music was rendered by the choir. Mr. Peter Harrison presided at the organ. 111. r. Cameron based a most eloquent and helpful discourse on the words "But he that lacketh these things is blind, and cannot see afar off."—ll.Peter 1-9 verses. There were, he pointed out, religions which produced little fruit or morality, which allowed those who professed them to become bankrufots, adulterers, and thieves in business. Such religions had a little check over them; but the religion of God made a bad man good, a dishoneit man honest and honourable in all ' Ins transactions a false man true. The pursuit of knowledge, he went on to say, was a greater pursuit than the pursuit of sport; it was far more entrancing to him than any game of football or cricket. It gave the mind a better training. Young men, if they w ished to be anything, should not waste their youth in playing at football. He was not speaking against the i game; n its place and proportion it might be good and so might cricket, but if it was all sport, if all their i faculties were absorbed in it, and f they had no time to spare for books, classes, science or study, he would tell them what would happen, because he could see far oft in these matters from experience. They would have sown the wind, and they would reap the whirlwind. Who would in these circumstances look up to them for advice wisdom, and judgment in trouble? How could they be strong in understanding if they had not cultivated that understanding at the one period of life when it could be done, namely, when they were young? It was far better for a man to possess knowledge than to be the best footballer and he advised all his younger hearers in particular to spend a proportion of their spare time in the pursuit of knowledge and the cultivation of their mental faculties. Collections were takenand a handsome sum of £22 3s. Bd. was realised. MOO FOR THE DEATH OF A SALT CAPTAIN. Jane Mills and her son, of London road, Northwioh, obtained from the Salt Union, Ltd., in the Northwich County Court on Wednesday £3OO as conrpensation for the death of George Mills, captain of a salt union boat, who was killed presumably by being struck on the head with salt and knocked down the hold, while at work loading. The defence was that the wharf was not part of a factory, and that the deceased was a sailor and not la workman. The Judge found that the place was a factory, and admitted that the latter part of the defence as to the deceased being a sailor and not a workman within the meaning of the Workmen's Compensation Act had presented the gravest difficulties. He,however found a verdict for the applicants for the £3OO claimed to be apportioned £2OO for the widow and £loojbetween the two daughters. Annie Dorothy Morton, the two-year-old child of James and Annie Morton, of Chippinghouse-road, Sheffield, was on Wednesday afternoon found 'drowned in a wash-tub in the yard. The disoovery was made by a neighbour, who seeing her own child near the tub went to fetch the youngster out, of danger, when she found Morton's little one lying drowned in the water. "As A SAFE, PERMA-NENT, AND WIRRA.NTRo OtTRE FOR Scrofula, Scurvy, Eczema, Bad Legs,Skin, and Blood Disease pimples and Sores of all kinds, we can with oonfidence re- commend Olarke's World-famed Blood Mixture. "It is certainly the finest Blood Purifier that science and medical Skill have brought to light." Thousands of Testimonials sold everwhere, at Is, 9d. per bottle. Beware of worthless =nations and substitutes.—Adut 3, CLARKB'S B 41 PILLS are warranted to cure, in either sex, all acquired or constitutional' Discharges from the Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains in the back. Free from Mercury. Established upwards of 30 years. In boxes 4s.